stering youngest-first one-hot forwarding selects and WB data at ID/EX moved forwarding priority off both EX operand paths at no CoreMark cycle cost, and mapping the register file to Gowin distributed RAM raised median Fmax a further 6.0 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (improvement, +31.3\n…[5900 of 8972 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8e285e2d19baec1a]…\node, while comparing captured load RAM rows in EX moved the collision check off the register-file read path.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r10s0 (regression, +3.49%): Checking load hits against the page captured in ID removed EX rs1 forwarding from the load-use interlock, but unresolved bases and page crossings forced MEM reads, leaving the prototype's 89.230 MHz median below the 90.082 MHz needed for replacement.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r10s1 (regression, +2.00%): Deferring load-use repair to the registered hit verdict preserved CoreMark’s 3,131,081 cycles, but forwarding the EX/MEM result through the ALU back to EX/MEM limited median Fmax to 87.218 MHz, below the 89.447 MHz needed for replacement.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r10s2 (regression, +3.05%): Using bits 11:2 of the preceding ADD/ADDI sum or LW candidate to predict a dependent load’s RAM row at byte offsets 0–3 saved 69,286 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, but median Fmax reached only 86.169 MHz of the 87.47 MHz needed for replacement.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
