{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat bench/programs/Makefile && cat test/cosim/build.sh && rg -n 'replay|critical|93\\\\.5' cores/bench/LESSONS.md && date -u && ls -al /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh\"", "aggregated_output": "CROSS   := riscv32-unknown-elf\nCC      := $(CROSS)-gcc\n\n# CoreMark build knobs. Defaults are the canonical EEMBC reporting config\n# (2K data size, -O3); tools/eval/fpga.py's COREMARK_EXPECTED CRCs assume\n# exactly these defaults.\n# - COREMARK_SIZE: TOTAL_DATA_SIZE in coremark.h (#ifndef-guarded). 2000 is\n#   the EEMBC canonical reporting config (used by VexRiscv et al.); 6000\n#   makes the matrix/list/state working sets ~3x larger so the score is\n#   harder to inflate via register-resident working sets.\n# - COREMARK_OPT: gcc optimization. -O3 is the standard CoreMark publication\n#   flag; -O2 is what the V0 baseline used.\n# Defaults match VexRiscv's published CoreMark methodology so the\n# orchestrator's fitness number is directly comparable to public CoreMark\n# results. To override (e.g. for the prior 6K -O2 V0 baseline):\n#     make bench/programs/coremark.elf COREMARK_SIZE=6000 COREMARK_OPT=-O2\nCOREMARK_SIZE ?= 2000\nCOREMARK_OPT  ?= -O3\n\nCFLAGS  := -march=rv32im -mabi=ilp32 $(COREMARK_OPT) -static -nostartfiles \\\n           -specs=nano.specs -specs=nosys.specs \\\n           -T bench/programs/link.ld -Ibench/programs/coremark \\\n           -Ibench/programs/coremark/baremetal \\\n           -DTOTAL_DATA_SIZE=$(COREMARK_SIZE)\n\nSRCS := bench/programs/coremark/core_list_join.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/core_main.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/core_matrix.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/core_state.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/core_util.c \\\n        bench/programs/coremark/baremetal/core_portme.c \\\n        bench/programs/crt0.S\n\nbench/programs/coremark.elf: $(SRCS)\n\t$(CC) $(CFLAGS) $^ -o $@\n\nbench/programs/selftest.elf: bench/programs/selftest.S\n\t$(CC) -march=rv32im -mabi=ilp32 -static -nostartfiles \\\n\t  -T bench/programs/link.ld $< -o $@\n\n# Random full-trace programs (V2). The .S files are generated once by\n# gen_random_trace.py <seed> and committed, so every candidate runs the\n# same programs.\nRANDOM_ELFS := bench/programs/random1.elf bench/programs/random2.elf bench/programs/random3.elf\n\nbench/pro\n…[4350 of 7422 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d83d65478eca705c]…\n producer-predicted load hits in MEM and refetching adjacent consumers on a miss left the EX/MEM address on the critical path to the next load-table row, limiting median Fmax to 83.363 MHz and outweighing the 68,078 cycles saved over ten CoreMark iterations.\n34:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r12s0 (regression, -4.15%): Selecting between the `PC+8` and exception words after parallel replay RAM reads preserved CoreMark’s 3,061,795 marked cycles, but the register-file read feeding the fetch-stage branch comparison and `alternate_pc` mux limited median Fmax to 85.709 MHz.\n35:- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r12s1 (regression, -4.32%): Committing replay reads to alternating banks one clock after issue preserved CoreMark’s 3,061,795 marked cycles, while `imem_ready` still selected between live and replay source indices on the critical path through the register file to `alternate_pc`, limiting median Fmax to 85.553 MHz.\nSun Nov 22 14:48:32 UTC 2026\n-rwxr-xr-x 1 nobody nogroup 24968 Jul 10  2025 /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
