IF/ID flush per correctly predicted taken transfer, while the single-cycle EX divider still limited Fmax to about 5.4 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +40.69%): Padding the 32-bit dividend magnitude to 35 bits let all seven rounds use five restoring steps without a partial-round mux, shifting the\n…[10766 of 13838 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:81c8753d01a68ab2]…\nEM ALU result on the fetch critical path, cutting median Fmax from 89.419 to 84.035 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r14s0 (regression, -7.87%): Storing each word two rows earlier removed the sequential replay adder, but the path from `imem_ready` through instruction selection, the rs1 read and the load-alignment check to `ready_ram_q` limited median Gowin Fmax to 82.604 MHz versus the champion’s 89.419 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r14s1 (regression, -6.72%): Predicting from WB zero flags and JALR targets cost only 5,361 extra CoreMark cycles, but separate control-transfer muxes left ordinary rs1 forwarding through the ALU to EX/MEM critical in placement 0, and median Fmax fell from 89.419 to 83.553 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r14s2 (regression, -3.03%): Replacing the ready-operand BEQ/BNE comparison with two-bit counters added 7,547 CoreMark mispredictions, and registering the EX correction cost an extra cycle on all 54,467 misses while wide-divider feedback limited median Fmax to 88.470 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
