{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_176","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"date -u; rg -c 'DONE \\\\(PASS' formal/last_run-*.log; tail -n 2 formal/last_run-wDvtGgTdX.log; tail -n 2 formal/last_run-wOa2rYJBx.log; tail -n 2 formal/last_run-wS6T0eKII.log; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"Sun Nov 22 18:52:22 UTC 2026\nformal/last_run-wS6T0eKII.log:52\nformal/last_run-wQCWh04cl.log:53\nformal/last_run-wOa2rYJBx.log:52\nformal/last_run-wDvtGgTdX.log:52\nSBY 19:51:02 [insn_sub_ch0] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY 19:51:31 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:05:03  waiting for solver (5 minutes)\nSBY 19:46:59 [cover] DONE (PASS, rc=0)\nSBY 19:49:04 [reg_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:05:03  waiting for solver (5 minutes)\nSBY 19:43:14 [pc_fwd_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:10:03  waiting for solver (10 minutes)\nSBY 19:48:14 [pc_fwd_ch0] engine_0: ##   0:15:03  waiting for solver (15 minutes)\n# Fetch operand-byte comparison\n\nFour distributed RAM read ports mirror the low byte of each integer register. MEM writes update every copy. A shared scrub index uses the architectural written mask to skip initialized rows and clear the remaining rows, including x0, on idle write slots. Both comparisons return equal during the sweep.\n\nThe live instruction addresses one pair directly. The registered instruction RAM response addresses the other pair. Its equality bit advances and holds with the replay candidate. Ready BEQ and BNE use the selected bit with the instruction's polarity. Pending operands use the branch counter.\n\nID registers the fallthrough and direct target separately. EX compares both forwarded 32-bit operands, resolves alignment and traps, and selects the recovery address from the registered successors.\n\nThe pipeline tests cover live and replay byte collisions, misaligned targets, and a stale replay comparison held across a divide. They check fetch addresses, retired PCs and operands, traps, and memory write enables. A register-file test exercises both comparison ports across a warm reset with intervening writes.\n\n## Timing-driven changes\n\nThe selected implementation follows the proposed pipeline changes. It uses an equal cold hint. Ready-load probes and JALR continue to use the full architectural operands.\n\n## Local results\n\nVerilator lint, the cocotb suites, and selftest co-simulation passed. Stalled CoreMark took 3,062,420 cycles between the markers, produced all five expected CRCs, printed “Correct operation validated.” and reached EBREAK with no out-of-bounds access.\n\nThe single-retirement ALTOPS formal suite passed all 53 checks on the first run. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 79.125, 81.524 and 81.524 MHz. The median falls below the 93.55 MHz acceptance threshold. Its worst path ends at the ready-load base register.\n\nA timing experiment moved the ready-load alignment check into EX and compared all four operand bytes in parallel. A further experiment predecoded branch recovery in ID, retimed the replay RAM collision check, split the target window check at bit 20, and recorded exact JALR target eligibility at writeback. The latter reached 90.647 MHz median. Its local PC-forwarding proof was still running when the fully checked implementation was selected.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
