{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Five-bit restoring divider\n\nDIV and REM now use seven five-bit rounds. Issue zero-extends the 32-bit dividend magnitude to 35 bits. Each round consumes the top five dividend bits and registers five quotient decisions and the updated remainder. The low 32 quotient bits feed the existing sign correction. The hardware and ALTOPS model share the same nine-cycle EX schedule and hold the result until consumption.\n\nThe ALU tests check exact latency on every divide and remainder, exercise reset on the last RUN edge and while the result is valid, and divide by one with adjacent dividend bits straddling each round boundary. The stalled-memory pipeline test checks retirement gaps of ten cycles for load to dependent divide, nine for consecutive divides or store to divide, and one for divide to ordinary consumer.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with zero warnings. All five cocotb suites passed.\n- All 53 fast formal checks passed on the first run, including liveness.\n- Cosimulation passed on all five ELFs. The self-test and three random programs matched the ISS with ready and stalled buses. CoreMark passed all five CRC checks with both buses stalled.\n- Ten CoreMark iterations took 4,491,485 cycles between the MMIO markers. Gowin reported 44.763, 45.009 and 45.009 MHz for placement options 0, 1 and 2. The 45.009 MHz median gives 100.21 iterations/s, a 40.7% gain over the 71.23 baseline.\n\nThe implementation follows the proposed datapath and schedule. DIV/REM take three more EX cycles than before. The worst setup path for all three placements now runs from the register file to ID/EX operand capture.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
