{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_95","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && verilator --lint-only -Wall -Wno-MULTITOP -sv +incdir+cores/bench/rtl cores/bench/rtl/*.sv'","aggregated_output":"# Fetch the resolved target in EX\n\nTen CoreMark iterations with both buses stalling took **4,136,096 marker-bracketed cycles**, down from 4,491,485. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 61.249, 63.043 and 63.043 MHz. The median gives **152.42 iterations/s**, 9.26% above the supplied 139.50 baseline.\n\n## Implementation\n\nEX drives the resolved successor to IF and captures the same address for RVFI. IF pairs the selected address with the returned instruction for decode. Each valid EX cycle also saves the successor in the retry PC, including cycles held by a stalled memory operation or divide. An EX bubble fetches from the retry PC, which resets to zero.\n\nTwo details differ from the literal implementation plan. The fall-through increment operates on the RV32IM word address and supplies both the sequential PC and the jump link. EX checks both target alignment bits after JALR bit-zero clearing, then selects the aligned target word or the fall-through word. The ID hold signal covers memory and divider backpressure. A load-use dependency inserts a bubble when that hold is clear.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings. All 53 single-retire fast formal checks passed on the final RTL, with zero failures or counterexamples.\n- All 89 cocotb cases passed. The new tests cover consecutive retirements through JAL, a link consumer, odd-address JALR and a taken BEQ. They also cover misaligned targets and a stalled older store whose acceptance coincides with an instruction-fetch miss. The latter test observes five consecutive requests for the target and exactly one accepted write from each expected store.\n- Cosimulation passed for selftest and all three random programs on ready and stalled buses. CoreMark matched all five CRCs and reported “Correct operation validated.” The start and stop markers occurred at cycles 14,674 and 4,150,770.\n\nThe timing dependency to watch is the combinational instruction response from the EX-selected address into ID. In the timed FPGA wrapper, the median placement's worst setup path runs from the registered rs1 forwarding match through the EX ALU to data memory.\n- V e r i l a t i o n   R e p o r t: Verilator 5.047 devel rev v5.046-331-g2cd9b8df6 (mod)\n- Verilator: Built from 0.576 MB sources in 14 modules, into 0.167 MB in 4 C++ files needing 0.000 MB\n- Verilator: Walltime 0.022 s (elab=0.003, cvt=0.012, bld=0.000); cpu 0.022 s on 1 threads; allocated 22.773 MB\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
