{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat Makefile formal/run_all.sh'", "aggregated_output": ".PHONY: help lint test test-infra cosim formal fpga timing next loop report bench clean\n.DEFAULT_GOAL := help\n\n# Resolve OSS CAD Suite path so commands work even when the user hasn't\n# sourced .toolchain/oss-cad-suite/environment in this shell.\nTOOLCHAIN_DIR := $(CURDIR)/.toolchain\nOSS_BIN       := $(TOOLCHAIN_DIR)/oss-cad-suite/bin\nLOCAL_BIN     := $(TOOLCHAIN_DIR)/bin\nexport PATH := $(OSS_BIN):$(LOCAL_BIN):$(PATH)\n\n# Orchestrator knobs (pass-through to tools.orchestrator):\n#   N        — number of rounds for `make loop` (default 10).\n#   K        — tournament size, slots per round (default 1 = sequential).\n#   AGENT    — codex (default) or claude. Honors a pre-existing\n#              AGENT_PROVIDER env var if AGENT isn't set on the make\n#              command line.\n#   BASE     — when forking a new TARGET, copy from cores/<BASE>/.\n#              Default: baseline (orchestrator-side default).\n#   COREMARK — target CoreMark iterations/sec (e.g. '370').\n#   LUT      — target LUT count (e.g. '3000').\n#   WORKTREE — default ON. `make loop` first creates a dedicated git worktree\n#              at .worktrees/<TARGET>/ on branch core-<TARGET> and re-execs\n#              itself there. Lets two `make loop` invocations for different\n#              TARGETs run in parallel without git index/merge collisions.\n#              To opt out (run directly on the current branch + cwd) pass\n#              an explicit empty value: `make loop TARGET=v1 WORKTREE=`.\nN        ?= 10\nK        ?= 1\nAGENT    ?= $(or $(AGENT_PROVIDER),codex)\nBASE     ?=\nCOREMARK ?=\nLUT      ?=\nWORKTREE ?= 1\n\n# Compose optional CLI flags for the orchestrator. Empty vars produce\n# empty strings so the orchestrator falls back to its defaults.\nORCH_FLAGS  = --iterations $(N) --tournament-size $(K)\nifneq ($(strip $(BASE)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --base $(BASE)\nendif\nifneq ($(strip $(COREMARK)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --coremark-target $(COREMARK)\nendif\nifneq ($(strip $(LUT)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --lut-target $(LUT)\nendif\n\n# Multi-core: TARGET selects the core under cores/<TARGET>\n…[15174 of 18246 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:1a4156bac2e0e834]…\nheses make the assumption satisfiable, so this\n        # branch never matches and the regular DONE (PASS branch covers it.\n        PASS=$((PASS+1))\n    else\n        FAIL=$((FAIL+1)); FAILED+=(\"$name\")\n    fi\ndone\nTOTAL=$((PASS+FAIL))\nif [ $TOTAL -eq 0 ]; then\n    FAIL=1\n    FAILED+=(\"no_checks_generated\")\nfi\n\necho \"\"\necho \"Formal: $PASS passed, $FAIL failed\"\nif [ $FAIL -gt 0 ]; then\n    echo \"Failed: ${FAILED[*]}\"\n    # Surface the FIRST failing check's logfile tail. tools/eval/formal.py\n    # captures this script's stdout/stderr into formal['detail'] and the\n    # orchestrator now records that in experiments/log.jsonl, so what we\n    # print here is what shows up as the diagnostic for the failed run.\n    first=\"${FAILED[0]}\"\n    if [ -f \"$first/logfile.txt\" ]; then\n        echo \"\"\n        echo \"--- $first/logfile.txt (last 30 lines) ---\"\n        tail -30 \"$first/logfile.txt\"\n    fi\n    echo \"\"\n    echo \"Full run log: $LOG\"\n    echo \"Per-check logs in: $CORE_DIR/checks/<check>/logfile.txt\"\nfi\n[ $FAIL -eq 0 ]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/core.yaml cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "name: bench\ndescription: Frozen LLM-benchmark fixture core. Identical RTL to baseline at the bench-fixture-v1\n  freeze point. The benchmark matrix clones from the bench-fixture-v1 orphan branch\n  and operates only on this directory.\nisa: rv32im\nnret: 1\ntarget_fpga: tang-nano-20k\ntargets:\n  fmax_mhz: 135\n  lut4: 10000\n  coremark_iter_s: 300\n  coremark_per_mhz: 2.2\ncurrent:\n  fmax_mhz: 64.198\n  lut4: 3989\n  ff: 746\n  coremark_iter_s: 3.0e-06\n  coremark_per_mhz: 0.0\n  source_id: hyp-20261122-003-r6s2\n  updated: '2026-11-22T05:44:33.288766Z'\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +32.67%): Parallel radix-4 trials raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.44 to 7.22 MHz, but chaining 16 remainder selections in one EX cycle kept it below the multicycle divider’s 32 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +487.71%): Limiting the restoring divider to eight quotient steps per clock shortened the EX critical path, and its six-cycle latency cost no timed CoreMark cycles because `iterate` and its callees execute no DIV or REM instructions.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.52%): The 128-entry bimodal predictor and fetch-time JAL targets cut CoreMark cycles by about 6.4% by avoiding one IF/ID flush per correctly predicted taken transfer, while the single-cycle EX divider still limited Fmax to about 5.4 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +40.69%): Padding the 32-bit dividend magnitude to 35 bits let all seven rounds use five restoring steps without a partial-round mux, shifting the Gowin critical path from divider feedback to the register-file read feeding ID/EX at a median 45.009 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (improvement, +49.09%): Registering 3× the divisor and testing all three multiples in parallel cut divider feedback from eight serial restoring steps per clock to three radix-4 decisions, raising Fmax at no cycle cost in CoreMark’s divide-free timed loop.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (regression, +7.27%): With eight serial divider steps still limiting Fmax t\n…[2797 of 5869 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c1d928c83c171412]…\nuccessor word when its address matched the requested PC, saving 770,138 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r6s0 (regression, +7.26%): When the carry out of the 12-bit base-offset sum equals the I/S displacement's sign bit, decode can reuse the page bits of a ready RAM base, letting an aligned load accepted in EX forward its value to an adjacent consumer without a bubble.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r6s1 (regression, +9.24%): Giving MEM retries priority when reserving the data port in ID lets ready-base accesses issue on consecutive EX cycles, and writing results at MEM completion lets decode use the register file’s write-first bypass for both early and retried loads.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r6s2 (improvement, +11.24%): Selecting the data RAM row in ID from the base plus immediate when ready or a per-PC stride prediction when the base is pending lets EX verify the full word address and forward a hit to an adjacent consumer without a load-use bubble or a MEM bus request.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py'", "aggregated_output": "\"\"\"End-to-end pipeline tests for rtl/core.sv.\n\nDrives a minimal Python-side imem/dmem against the core's combinational\nfetch/load model and captures RVFI retirements. Covers the §8 phase-2\nacceptance set: forwarding (EX->EX, MEM->EX), branches, JAL, JALR,\nSW+LW roundtrip, load-use stall, and the trap discipline (illegal /\nECALL trap, EBREAK does NOT trap).\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport cocotb\nfrom cocotb.clock import Clock\nfrom cocotb.triggers import ClockCycles, RisingEdge, Timer\n\nfrom _helpers import run_cocotb\n\n\n# ── RV32I instruction encoder helpers ──────────────────────────────────────\ndef _r(funct7, rs2, rs1, funct3, rd, opcode):\n    return ((funct7 & 0x7F) << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _i(imm, rs1, funct3, rd, opcode):\n    return ((imm & 0xFFF) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _s(imm, rs2, rs1, funct3, opcode):\n    imm_hi = (imm >> 5) & 0x7F\n    imm_lo = imm & 0x1F\n    return (imm_hi << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | (imm_lo << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _b(imm, rs2, rs1, funct3, opcode):\n    # imm is a 13-bit signed number; bit 0 is implicitly zero.\n    b12  = (imm >> 12) & 1\n    b105 = (imm >> 5)  & 0x3F\n    b41  = (imm >> 1)  & 0xF\n    b11  = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    return (b12 << 31) | (b105 << 25) | ((rs2 & 0x1F) << 20) | ((rs1 & 0x1F) << 15) \\\n         | ((funct3 & 0x7) << 12) | (b41 << 8) | (b11 << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef _j(imm, rd, opcode):\n    # 21-bit signed imm; bit 0 implicit zero.\n    b20    = (imm >> 20) & 1\n    b101   = (imm >> 1)  & 0x3FF\n    b11    = (imm >> 11) & 1\n    b1912  = (imm >> 12) & 0xFF\n    return (b20 << 31) | (b101 << 21) | (b11 << 20) | (b1912 << 12) \\\n         | ((rd & 0x1F) << 7) | (opcode & 0x7F)\n\n\ndef ADDI(rd, rs1, imm): return _i(imm & 0xFFF, rs1, 0b000, rd, 0b0010011)\ndef ADD (rd, rs1, rs2): return _r(0, rs2, rs1, 0b0\n…[38487 of 41559 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6c60b03b21e0d1a8]…\n rets, _ = await _run(dut, program, max_cycles=20)\n    [re] = _by_insn(rets, EBREAK())\n    assert re[\"trap\"] == 0, f\"EBREAK should not trap; got trap={re['trap']}\"\n\n\n@cocotb.test()\nasync def rvfi_order_strictly_monotonic(dut):\n    \"\"\"A short stream must produce rvfi_order = 0,1,2,... with no gaps.\"\"\"\n    program = [\n        ADDI(1, 0, 1),\n        ADDI(2, 0, 2),\n        ADDI(3, 0, 3),\n        ADDI(4, 0, 4),\n        EBREAK(),\n    ]\n    rets, _ = await _run(dut, program)\n    orders = [r[\"order\"] for r in rets]\n    assert orders == list(range(len(orders))), f\"order not monotonic +1: {orders}\"\n\n\ndef test_pipeline_runner():\n    run_cocotb(\n        toplevel=\"core\",\n        sources=[\n            \"core_pkg.sv\",\n            \"alu.sv\", \"decoder.sv\", \"imm_gen.sv\", \"reg_file.sv\",\n            \"if_stage.sv\", \"id_stage.sv\", \"ex_stage.sv\",\n            \"mem_stage.sv\", \"wb_stage.sv\",\n            \"hazard_unit.sv\", \"forward_unit.sv\", \"load_predictor.sv\",\n            \"core.sv\",\n        ],\n        test_module=\"test_pipeline\",\n    )\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
