{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":"# Direct EX/MEM forwarding\n\nEX forwards the registered integer result, extended load-hit data, or completed divide directly to either operand. MEM still selects the architectural result from that register and the two registered product halves.\n\nMUL, MULH, MULHU, and MULHSU reuse the load-miss interlock. An older memory request holds the multiply in EX until it can advance. A matching consumer then waits one issue cycle and captures the product through the register file's write-first port. The hazard-unit input and internal signal were renamed to reflect both producer types. As proposed, the interlock compares both raw source fields, so an immediate field that matches the destination can also cost one cycle.\n\nThe stalled-memory regression checks the four multiply-use bubbles alongside the existing bus retries and load-use bubble. A new pipeline case checks consecutive independent multiplies, an rs2-only dependency, and a multiply to x0 followed by a read of x0.\n\n## Local verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed without warnings. All five cocotb suites passed.\n- Selftest and all three random traces matched the reference with ready and stalled buses.\n- With both buses stalled, ten CoreMark iterations took 3,167,223 marked cycles, up 1.11%. All five canonical CRCs matched and UART reported “Correct operation validated.”\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 73.720, 76.884, and 76.884 MHz. The median gives 242.75 iterations/s, up 6.00% from the champion and above the 239.53 cutoff. Placement 0 used 4,001 logic cells, 739 registers, and two DSPs.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
