{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_153","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; rg -c 'DONE \\\\(PASS' \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/replay-checked-formal.log\"; date -u'","aggregated_output":"# Two-issue instruction replay\n\nFor three consecutive issues A, B, and C, A reads its replay row and B registers the response for C. When B stalls, the first intervening edge saves A's response. C can replay only on a stalled instruction bus with a valid entry and an exact in-window word-PC match.\n\nThe 512 × 51 RAM reads every clock. An in-window live issue with two predecessors queues the older index, current word PC, and bus word for the next clock's write. Only the queued write enable needs the late issue decision. The scrub writes the queued payload with the entry's valid bit clear. Redirects clear the final prediction and preserve training history. Reset and FENCE.I discard history and pending replay state.\n\nThe shared-JALR regression checks both alternate targets on their second visit, including a two-cycle pause before one JALR issues. The original RTL needed one extra bus request at each target. The updated alias and self-modifying-code tests exercise the collision, live-word priority, and fence scrub at the new lookup distance.\n\nOnly the fetch stage implements new hardware. It samples the scrub and physical write-collision checks on every RAM read, then combines that result with the RAM entry's valid bit before saving or forwarding the response. B also requires a predecessor issued since the last invalidation. The write-enable path detects a live FENCE.I directly from the bus. A replayed FENCE.I also cancels training because the bus is stalled. The existing FENCE.I drain still waits for an older code store.\n\nVerilator lint passed without warnings. All five cocotb suites passed. Selftest and the three random programs matched the reference RVFI traces on ready and randomly stalled buses. The ten-iteration stalled CoreMark run used 3,148,248 marked cycles, matched all five CRCs, and printed the validation banner.\n\nGowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 73.081, 82.516, and 82.516 MHz. The median gives 262.10 iterations/s, up 4.78% from 250.15 and 0.44 iterations/s above the 261.66 cutoff. Placement 0 used 4,060 logic cells, 917 registers, nine block RAMs, and two DSPs.\n\nThe first replay implementation passed all 53 fast formal checks. On the selected RTL, 52 checks have passed and the register-consistency check is still running.\n47\nSun Nov 22 08:25:45 UTC 2026\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
