correct-path issues.\n\nTwo timing-driven refinements extend the proposed implementation. ID predecodes the branch comparison that can trigger a redirect. The replay pipeline compares the saved read and fill rows one stage after the exception-table lookup. The core keeps its configured single RVFI retirement channel.\n\n## Local measurements\n\n- Verilator lint passed with all requested warnings enabled.\n- All 108 cocotb tests passed, including 37 pipeline tests. Directed traces cover single- and dual-source repairs, hits without a bubble, trapping loads, stalled branch and divide consumers, and a killed FENCE.I followed by replay through fetch waits.\n- Selftest matched the reference ISS both with and without bus stalls. Stalled CoreMark passed all five CRC checks and printed “Correct operation validated.”\n- The marker interval was 3,131,081 cycles, from cycle 11,719 to 3,142,800.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 85.782, 87.218 and 87.218 MHz. The 87.218 MHz median gives 278.56 CoreMark iterations/s and falls below the 89.447 MHz replacement threshold. The median placement's worst path runs from an EX/MEM result through adjacent forwarding and the ALU back to EX/MEM.\n- Local formal passed all 53 checks on the retained RTL with the single-retirement ALTOPS configuration. No counterexamples or formal fix attempts were needed.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
