{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/core.yaml; cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md | tail -120; cat .gitignore'", "aggregated_output": "name: bench\ndescription: Frozen LLM-benchmark fixture core. Identical RTL to baseline at the bench-fixture-v1\n  freeze point. The benchmark matrix clones from the bench-fixture-v1 orphan branch\n  and operates only on this directory.\nisa: rv32im\nnret: 1\ntarget_fpga: tang-nano-20k\ntargets:\n  fmax_mhz: 135\n  lut4: 10000\n  coremark_iter_s: 300\n  coremark_per_mhz: 2.2\ncurrent:\n  fmax_mhz: 89.419\n  lut4: 4001\n  ff: 915\n  coremark_iter_s: 3.0e-06\n  coremark_per_mhz: 0.0\n  source_id: hyp-20261122-002-r11s1\n  updated: '2026-11-22T12:46:35.718391Z'\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +32.67%): Parallel radix-4 trials raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.44 to 7.22 MHz, but chaining 16 remainder selections in one EX cycle kept it below the multicycle divider’s 32 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +487.71%): Limiting the restoring divider to eight quotient steps per clock shortened the EX critical path, and its six-cycle latency cost no timed CoreMark cycles because `iterate` and its callees execute no DIV or REM instructions.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.52%): The 128-entry bimodal predictor and fetch-time JAL targets cut CoreMark cycles by about 6.4% by avoiding one IF/ID flush per correctly predicted taken transfer, while the single-cycle EX divider still limited Fmax to about 5.4 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +40.69%): Padding the 32-bit dividend magnitude to 35 bits let all seven rounds use five restoring steps without a partial-round mux, shifting the Gowin critical path from divider feedback to the register-file read feeding ID/EX at a median 45.009 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (improvement, +49.09%): Registering 3× the divisor and testing all three multiples in parallel cut divider feedback from eight serial restoring steps per clock to three radix-4 decisions, raising Fmax at no cycle cost in CoreMark’s divide-free timed loop.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (regression, +7.27%): With eight serial divider steps still limiting Fmax \n…[14343 of 17415 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:583d5a467ebb7572]…\npshot: PATH + resolved EDA tool paths)\n# IS tracked, decided at the first v2 rep publish (e1a static, 2026-07-16):\n# it is tiny, contains no secrets, and pins which toolchain binaries\n# produced each rep's numbers.\n\n# Throwaway harness-verification runs (one-model YAMLs + their output\n# trees). These are reliability checks, not benchmark data — output is\n# regenerable and the YAMLs only encode \"rerun this single model to\n# confirm a fix landed\". Naming convention: `*-verify` for output dirs,\n# `models-*-verify.yaml` for the throwaway model YAML.\nbench/*-verify/\nbench/smoke-*/\ntools/bench/models-*-verify.yaml\n\n# Aborted/partial bench output preserved locally with a debug-tag suffix\n# (e.g. `gpt-5_5.failed-pwd-bug/`, `gpt-5_5.validate-v3/`, sibling\n# `results.jsonl.<tag>`). They document a known-broken state for the\n# author's own postmortem; the canonical results live in the un-suffixed\n# per-model dirs.\nbench/*.failed-*\nbench/*.validate-*\nbench/*.prev-*\nbench/*.stale-*\nbench/*.partial-*\nbench/results.jsonl.*\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
