t-first one-hot forwarding selects and WB data at ID/EX moved forwarding priority off both EX operand paths at no CoreMark cycle cost, and mapping the register file to Gowin distributed RAM raised median Fmax a further 6.0 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (improvement, +31.36%): Resolving MEM and WB bypasses in ID and registering each source’s match with the adjacent producer reduced EX forwarding to one 2:1 mux per operand, raising Fmax while preserving CoreMark’s 4,491,485 timed cycles.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r3s2 (regression, +15.49%): Predicting branches and zero-offset JALRs from ready operands in IF saved 50,505 more CoreMark cycles than bimodal branch and direct JAL prediction alone, while the MEM/WB path through EX forwarding to the data-memory address limited Fmax to 50.92 MHz versus 62.66 MHz for the core with decode-stage bypassing.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r4s0 (regression, +9.26%): Fetching the resolved EX target through combinational IF/ID eliminated the redirect bubble on a ready bus, but decode-stage prediction ran faster overall at 64.744 versus 63.043 MHz despite taking 0.68% more CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r4s1 (regression, +8.87%): The two-entry fetch queue buffered instructions during decode stalls and issued them when `imemReady` was low, saving 152,920 CoreMark cycles over same-cycle EX redirects alone, but lower Fmax reduced throughput from 152.42 to 151.88 iterations/s.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r4s2 (improvement, +11.46%): Comparing MEM/WB-bypassed operands when neither source awaits an EX write lets fetch avoid taken BEQ/BNE bubbles, and registering the unused direct successor keeps address calculation off the EX branch recovery path.\n/srv/hwebench/toolchain/bin/riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc\nFilesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\n/dev/md2        905G  746G  114G  87% /srv/hwebench/tmp/19d02a47/6f6c66ac\n20\n 05:49:42 up 55 days,  5:49,  4 users,  load average: 8.04, 10.82, 13.95\n","ex