{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat Makefile; cat cores/bench/core.yaml; cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": ".PHONY: help lint test test-infra cosim formal fpga timing next loop report bench clean\n.DEFAULT_GOAL := help\n\n# Resolve OSS CAD Suite path so commands work even when the user hasn't\n# sourced .toolchain/oss-cad-suite/environment in this shell.\nTOOLCHAIN_DIR := $(CURDIR)/.toolchain\nOSS_BIN       := $(TOOLCHAIN_DIR)/oss-cad-suite/bin\nLOCAL_BIN     := $(TOOLCHAIN_DIR)/bin\nexport PATH := $(OSS_BIN):$(LOCAL_BIN):$(PATH)\n\n# Orchestrator knobs (pass-through to tools.orchestrator):\n#   N        — number of rounds for `make loop` (default 10).\n#   K        — tournament size, slots per round (default 1 = sequential).\n#   AGENT    — codex (default) or claude. Honors a pre-existing\n#              AGENT_PROVIDER env var if AGENT isn't set on the make\n#              command line.\n#   BASE     — when forking a new TARGET, copy from cores/<BASE>/.\n#              Default: baseline (orchestrator-side default).\n#   COREMARK — target CoreMark iterations/sec (e.g. '370').\n#   LUT      — target LUT count (e.g. '3000').\n#   WORKTREE — default ON. `make loop` first creates a dedicated git worktree\n#              at .worktrees/<TARGET>/ on branch core-<TARGET> and re-execs\n#              itself there. Lets two `make loop` invocations for different\n#              TARGETs run in parallel without git index/merge collisions.\n#              To opt out (run directly on the current branch + cwd) pass\n#              an explicit empty value: `make loop TARGET=v1 WORKTREE=`.\nN        ?= 10\nK        ?= 1\nAGENT    ?= $(or $(AGENT_PROVIDER),codex)\nBASE     ?=\nCOREMARK ?=\nLUT      ?=\nWORKTREE ?= 1\n\n# Compose optional CLI flags for the orchestrator. Empty vars produce\n# empty strings so the orchestrator falls back to its defaults.\nORCH_FLAGS  = --iterations $(N) --tournament-size $(K)\nifneq ($(strip $(BASE)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --base $(BASE)\nendif\nifneq ($(strip $(COREMARK)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --coremark-target $(COREMARK)\nendif\nifneq ($(strip $(LUT)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --lut-target $(LUT)\nendif\n\n# Multi-core: TARGET selects the core under cores/<TARGET>\n…[7765 of 10837 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:8b80748488a43bc6]…\n 47.7 MHz radix-4 core, well above the 6.4% saved by avoiding IF/ID flushes.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r3s0 (regression, +20.45%): Registering youngest-first one-hot forwarding selects and WB data at ID/EX moved forwarding priority off both EX operand paths at no CoreMark cycle cost, and mapping the register file to Gowin distributed RAM raised median Fmax a further 6.0 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (improvement, +31.36%): Resolving MEM and WB bypasses in ID and registering each source’s match with the adjacent producer reduced EX forwarding to one 2:1 mux per operand, raising Fmax while preserving CoreMark’s 4,491,485 timed cycles.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r3s2 (regression, +15.49%): Predicting branches and zero-offset JALRs from ready operands in IF saved 50,505 more CoreMark cycles than bimodal branch and direct JAL prediction alone, while the MEM/WB path through EX forwarding to the data-memory address limited Fmax to 50.92 MHz versus 62.66 MHz for the core with decode-stage bypassing.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'rg --files cores/bench/test tools/eval; cat fpga/core_bench_si.sv'", "aggregated_output": "tools/eval/holdout.py\ntools/eval/gowin.py\ntools/eval/formal.py\ntools/eval/test_gate_sanity.py\ntools/eval/_subprocess.py\ntools/eval/test_holdout.py\ntools/eval/fpga.py\ntools/eval/test_formal_workdir_cleanup.py\ntools/eval/test_rvfi_lint.py\ntools/eval/test_process_reaper.py\ntools/eval/test_formal_reclassify.py\ntools/eval/rvfi_lint.py\ntools/eval/test_remove_path.py\ntools/eval/test_subprocess_pgroup.py\ntools/eval/__init__.py\ntools/eval/test_slots.py\ntools/eval/test_bench_stall_gen.py\ntools/eval/test_fpga_report.py\ntools/eval/test_formal_nret.py\ntools/eval/cosim.py\ntools/eval/_slots.py\ncores/bench/test/test_imm_gen.py\ncores/bench/test/conftest.py\ncores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\ncores/bench/test/_helpers.py\ncores/bench/test/test_decoder.py\ncores/bench/test/test_reg_file.py\ncores/bench/test/test_alu.py\n// fpga/core_bench_si.sv\n//\n// FPGA Fmax wrapper for single-issue (`nret 1`) cores. Same shape as\n// core_bench.sv but instantiates `core` with only the channel-0 RVFI port\n// set — no `_1` ports. Selected by the orchestrator (via BENCH env var in\n// synth.tcl) when cores/<target>/core.yaml declares nret: 1.\n//\n// See core_bench.sv for the rationale on LFSR-driven imem, the dmem\n// model, the V2 stall generator, and why the LED observes only the\n// memory-side outputs (RVFI-only logic is pruned).\nmodule core_bench (\n  input  logic clock,\n  input  logic reset,\n  output logic led\n);\n\n  logic [31:0] lfsr;\n  always_ff @(posedge clock or posedge reset)\n    if (reset) lfsr <= 32'h1;\n    else       lfsr <= {lfsr[30:0], lfsr[31] ^ lfsr[21] ^ lfsr[1] ^ lfsr[0]};\n\n  logic [31:0] dmem [0:2047];\n  logic [31:0] dmem_rdata;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_addr;\n  logic [31:0] dmem_wdata;\n  logic [3:0]  dmem_wen;\n  logic        dmem_ren;\n\n  always_ff @