{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'git diff --check && git status --short; cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md'","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/core.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv\n M cores/bench/rtl/load_predictor.sv\n M cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py\n# Dependent issue on a predicted load hit\n\nDecode issues the adjacent consumer when the load has a valid, eligible table entry. EX checks the forwarded word address and access alignment and records the verdict in EX/MEM. A verified hit forwards the extended value and suppresses the bus read. A definite miss retains the existing load-use interlock.\n\nOne bit captured with ID/EX records a dependency on the preceding nonzero destination. On a predicted hit that misses, the consumer holds its PC, operands, and control fields while MEM completes the load. The implementation reuses the forwarding unit's two registered source matches to patch each operand independently from the MEM writeback value. A trapping load leaves the captured operands intact. The completion edge inserts one EX/MEM bubble. Divider start, branch redirect, and branch and load predictor training wait for the repaired operands.\n\nThe load table samples both row addresses, the MEM update enable, and scrub completion alongside each candidate. EX rejects a read/write collision using those samples. The live older-store veto stays active throughout a stalled store.\n\n## Local results\n\n- Verilator lint passed with `-Wall`.\n- All 103 cocotb cases passed. During a delayed aliased-page read, the new test observed fetch at PC 24 after issuing the ADD at PC 20. Running the same test against the original RTL failed at that assertion with fetch still at PC 20. The test also checks accepted bus transactions, RVFI order and values, DIVU, stores, both branch outcomes, and preservation of the old destination after a misaligned load. Load-to-ADD retirement gaps were two cycles on repair and one cycle on a verified hit.\n- Selftest and all three random programs matched the reference ISS on zero-wait and stalled buses.\n- Ten stalled-bus CoreMark iterations took 3,132,302 marked cycles. All five canonical CRCs matched, the UART reported successful validation, and the simulator reported zero out-of-bounds accesses.\n- Gowin placement options 0, 1, and 2 reached 76.875, 77.099, and 77.099 MHz. The median corresponds to 246.14 iterations/s, 7.49% above the 71.73 MHz baseline at the same cycle count.\n- The standard single-channel formal suite passed all 53 checks on the first run. Its 20-cycle bound ends before the 1,024-cycle load-table scrub completes. The directed test exercises the warm-table repair path under backpressure.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
