{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_112","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/implementation_notes.md; tail -n 1 \"$TMPDIR/formal-final.log\"'","aggregated_output":"# Two-issue instruction RAM lookahead\n\nThe local FPGA fitness run measured **273.11 CoreMark iterations/s**. Ten iterations took **3,131,081 bracketed cycles**, and the median Gowin Fmax was **85.513 MHz**, 3.583 MHz above the requested threshold.\n\n| Gowin placement | Fmax (MHz) |\n| --- | ---: |\n| 0 | 84.023 |\n| 1 | 85.513 |\n| 2 | 85.513 |\n\n## Implementation\n\nEach accepted issue probes the 1,024-word instruction RAM for the word expected two issues later. The default row is the issuing PC's word row plus two, modulo 1,024. A 128-entry distributed RAM holds exceptions with four source tag bits. An accepted live instruction in the RAM window buffers a destination-indexed word fill and trains the source from two issues earlier. A sequential outcome clears an exception that matched at prediction time.\n\nThe synchronous read result moves into a preserved 51-bit fabric register on the next issue. Both stages hold through stalls and redirects. Replay requires an in-window full word-PC match, a valid entry and a collision-free probe. The live instruction bus takes priority. Reset and issued FENCE.I invalidate the stages, history and buffered transaction, then scrub the word store and exception table. The existing fence/store interlock orders the first post-fence fetch.\n\nThe additional timing change is in the data-load predictor. It registers the concurrent read row, write row and write enable alongside the candidate. EX rejects an enabled write to the sampled read row, removing the collision comparator from the end of ID's register-file and address-adder path. The original load-use interlock still uses the verified hit.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Verilator lint passed with the requested warning flags.\n- Local formal passed all 53 checks on the final RTL using the core's declared single-channel wrapper and ALTOPS configuration.\n- All 106 cocotb cases passed. The pipeline tests exercise a shared fill across a 4 KiB boundary, consecutive replay-only issues, taken and aliased exceptions, stalls, same-row collisions, full-PC rejection, live-bus priority and a stalled code store followed by FENCE.I.\n- Cosimulation passed selftest and three random instruction traces with ready and stalled buses. CoreMark reached both timing markers and EBREAK, with all five canonical CRCs, the validation banner and zero out-of-bounds accesses.\n\n## Timing and coverage limits\n\nPlacement 0's longest path reaches the instruction RAM enable from EX forwarding control. Direct-mapped row or exception conflicts can cost a replay opportunity. After reset or FENCE.I, fetch relies on the live bus for the 1,024-cycle scrub. The formal wrapper drives both buses ready, so the directed tests and stalled cosimulation cover warmed replay.\nFormal: 53 passed, 0 failed\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
