{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-001-r10s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-001-r10s0\ntitle: Verify load hits from the base and page captured in decode\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  On the champion's median placement, a DSP result passes through rs1 forwarding, address addition and the load-hit comparison before reaching ID control. The hit decision can use the address already needed for the synchronous RAM read. Limiting this fast path to a known base and a displacement that stays on its page removes EX forwarding from the load-use interlock.\nhypothesis: |\n  In ID, add the 12-bit I displacement to two possible base values in parallel. One comes from the raw register-file read and one from the registered EX/MEM result. A matching MEM writer selects the second sum. A matching writer still in EX, or a selected MEM load awaiting external data, makes the probe ineligible. This keeps the live dmem response off the RAM address path.\n  \n  Read the existing 1,024-word RAM using bits 11 through 2 of the selected sum. Capture the byte offset, base selector and immediate sign. For each source separately, register its page bits 19 through 12, its in-RAM flag and the carry out of its low 12-bit addition. EX uses the selected source's metadata. When its carry equals the displacement sign, the full effective address is on the base page. The RAM row already identifies the word within that page, so each entry needs only its eight-bit page tag, valid bit and data. The PC-indexed stride tables can be removed.\n  \n  An aligned, eligible load with a valid matching RAM page can forward on the existing schedule. All other loads take the ordinary path using the full forwarded effective address. The raw word, captured byte offset, read/write collision test, older-store veto and reset scrub stay aligned across dmem holds.\n  \n  The prototype completed ten bracketed iterations in 3,153,333 cycles with the five canonical CRCs. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 81.535, 89.230 and 89.230 MHz. The median gives 282.97 iterations/s, 3.61% above the champion. Replacement requires a median above 90.082 MHz at that cycle count. The selftest and three random instruction streams matched the reference on both ready and stalled buses.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 4\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Supply raw RF rs1 and EX/MEM result as separate probe bases. Select MEM on a matching enabled nonzero destination. Veto a pending EX writer or a matching MEM load miss. Connect the captured lane offset to EX and remove the stride training connections.\n  - file: rtl/load_predictor.sv\n    description: Form parallel low address sums, select the read row, and capture each source's page, range and carry alongside the selected offset. Store 41 bits per RAM word. Check the selected page and carry in EX, qualify with scrub completion and both store or fill collision checks, and hold lookup state with the consumer.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Use the verified page probe, RAM valid and captured offset for load-hit, alignment and subword extension. Deliver the full forwarded byte address to MEM on hits and misses. Remove the obsolete stride-training output.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Check exact bus and retirement behavior for RF and MEM-sourced same-page hits, pending-base and page-crossing misses, aliased tags, MMIO and stalled stores. Update the stride-dependent tests to require the ordinary bus read for an unresolved base. Exercise both end rows on warm reset with same-page bases.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
