{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-001-r1s0.yaml'","aggregated_output":"{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-29140aa\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 12.12, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 5.442, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 12312, \"ff\": 460, \"seeds\": [5.14, 5.442, 5.442], \"perturbations\": [[0, 5.14, 150], [1, 5.442, 149], [2, 5.442, 149]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -189.542, \"arrival_ns\": 199.837, \"logic_cells\": 156, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_fwd\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 150, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T02:35:47.912155Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\nid: hyp-20261122-001-r1s0\ntitle: \"Resolve two quotient bits per step in the combinational divider\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The baseline's 199.837 ns worst path runs from the MEM/WB destination\n  register through forwarding and the EX ALU. The ALU evaluates signed and\n  unsigned division and remainder combinationally. A chain of quotient-bit\n  decisions is a plausible source of the reported 156-cell path. Shortening\n  it would raise the clock rate for every CoreMark instruction.\nhypothesis: |\n  Implement DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU with one unrolled radix-4 restoring\n  divider inside the ALU. Each of 16 steps consumes two dividend bits and\n  computes three trial differences in parallel. Their borrow flags select\n  both the next quotient digit and an already computed remainder. This\n  halves the number of serial digit decisions relative to binary restoring\n  division. The ALU still returns its result in EX, so the prediction is\n  the same 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles at a higher median Gowin Fmax.\n  The main timing risk is the routing and selection delay of the three\n  parallel differences per step.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 25\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      In the real DIV/REM datapath, form unsigned operand magnitudes and\n      precompute D, 2D and 3D once at 34-bit precision. Start with remainder\n      zero. At step k (1 through 16), append the next two most-significant\n      unconsumed dividend bits to the remainder. This trial dividend fits\n      in 2k bits. For each divisor multiple, subtract its low 2k bits with\n      an explicit borrow output. A trial fits when its upper multiple bits\n      are zero and the subtraction has no borrow. Select the largest fitting\n      multiple (or zero), emit its two-bit index and pass the corresponding\n      difference (or the trial dividend) to the next step. Decode the fit\n      flags into mutually exclusive selects so the three trials stay parallel.\n      Select quotient or remainder, then apply the quotient's XOR sign or\n      the dividend's sign for remainder. Handle a zero divisor explicitly\n      with all ones for DIV/DIVU and the original dividend for REM/REMU.\n      Use 32-bit magnitude and sign correction so INT_MIN / -1 returns\n      INT_MIN with zero remainder. Preserve the existing ALTOPS formulas.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise quotient-digit transitions with dividends one below, at and\n      one above D, 2D and 3D wherever representable. Include large unsigned\n      divisors with 2D or 3D above 32 bits, mixed signed operands, and\n      quotients with alternating base-4 digits. Check both quotient and\n      remainder against an integer reference that truncates toward zero.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
