{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261122-003-r4s2.last cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r4s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "Wrote and schema-validated the [predictor hypothesis](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-astra_xhigh-v2-rep5/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261122-003-r4s2-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r4s2.yaml). It uses ready decode operands to predict BEQ and BNE and registers the alternate PC for recovery.\n\nThe scratch prototype reached **149.63 CoreMark iter/s**, **7.26% above the champion**, clearing the 4.6% acceptance margin. Ten iterations took 4,164,193 marked cycles at a median Gowin Fmax of 62.308 MHz. All five CRCs matched, and all 53 standard formal checks passed.id: hyp-20261122-003-r4s2\ntitle: Predict equality branches from decode operands and register the recovery PC\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion fetches PC+4 and discards one fetch slot per taken transfer.\n  Its 4,491,485 marked CoreMark cycles include 294,425 conditional branches\n  with a nonsequential successor. Earlier bimodal branch and JAL prediction\n  saved 289,213 cycles. The champion now assembles both operands in decode,\n  so fetch can also predict data-dependent equality branches and register\n  jumps using values the datapath already provides.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give fetch one 32-bit equality comparator. For a legal BEQ or BNE whose\n  sources are both ready, use the comparison of the existing MEM/WB-bypassed\n  ID operands. A source is ready when the instruction in ID/EX has no pending\n  write to that nonzero register. For ordered branches and busy-source\n  equality branches, use a PC-indexed table of 128 two-bit saturating counters.\n  Predict direct JAL taken. For legal zero-offset JALR with a ready rs1,\n  use rs1 with bit zero cleared. Require word alignment and a target inside\n  the 1 MiB instruction window for a taken prediction. Default to PC+4.\n\n  Form PC+4 and the direct B/J target during fetch. Register the unused\n  direct successor at issue so EX has the recovery address before it resolves\n  the condition. A predicted JALR target is exact because its base is ready\n  and its displacement is zero. A single dire\n…[3016 of 6088 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:9442fa9e5f09a2a9]…\nved successor to RVFI. Jump link data now passes through\n      the regular ALU result path. Suppress its write on a trap. Train once\n      per advancing nontrapping conditional branch by saturating its captured\n      counter toward the outcome. A dmem freeze defers recovery and training.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Qualify the raw fetch source comparisons for load-use with imem_ready.\n      A recovery kills the younger instruction even if imem is waiting.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check fetch addresses for BEQ/BNE using a MEM load bypass and for an\n      equality branch with a younger pending writer to the same register.\n      Warm a table-predicted ordered loop, then exit it while imem is stalled.\n      Exercise ready and busy zero-offset JALRs, a nonzero offset and a\n      misaligned target. Hold resolution behind an older stalled load.\n      Check RVFI successor, link, trap and order, and monitor dmem write\n      enables to catch a wrong-path store.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
