ing priority off both EX operand paths at no CoreMark cycle cost, and mapping the register file to Gowin distributed RAM raised median Fmax a further 6.0 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (improvement, +31.3\n…[5294 of 8366 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:39cc4847ffc20000]…\nat a cost of 0.51% more CoreMark cycles under bus stalls.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r9s1 (regression, +7.03%): Addressing dedicated register-file ports from the live instruction bus and probing the base word directly for byte offsets 0–3 removed the replay mux and ID address adder from load-table row selection, raising median Fmax from 78.353 to 85.141 MHz in the prototype at a cost of 1.39% more CoreMark cycles.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r9s2 (improvement, +9.18%): Reading the destination-indexed replay RAM two issues ahead using `PC+8` or a learned exception let a fabric register drive decode, while comparing captured load RAM rows in EX moved the collision check off the register-file read path.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r10s0 (regression, +3.49%): Checking load hits against the page captured in ID removed EX rs1 forwarding from the load-use interlock, but unresolved bases and page crossings forced MEM reads, leaving the prototype's 89.230 MHz median below the 90.082 MHz needed for replacement.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
