ing priority off both EX operand paths at no CoreMark cycle cost, and mapping the register file to Gowin distributed RAM raised median Fmax a further 6.0 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (improvement, +31.3\n…[10020 of 13092 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2436ffaffa45f03a]…\n-alignment check to `ready_ram_q` limited median Gowin Fmax to 82.604 MHz versus the champion’s 89.419 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r14s1 (regression, -6.72%): Predicting from WB zero flags and JALR targets cost only 5,361 extra CoreMark cycles, but separate control-transfer muxes left ordinary rs1 forwarding through the ALU to EX/MEM critical in placement 0, and median Fmax fell from 89.419 to 83.553 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r14s2 (regression, -3.03%): Replacing the ready-operand BEQ/BNE comparison with two-bit counters added 7,547 CoreMark mispredictions, and registering the EX correction cost an extra cycle on all 54,467 misses while wide-divider feedback limited median Fmax to 88.470 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r15s0 (regression, -8.85%): Precomputing live and replay BEQ/BNE hints from operand low bytes added only 625 CoreMark cycles, but load probes still read the full-width rs1 after instruction selection, leaving the path to `ready_ram_q` critical at a median Fmax of 81.524 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
