{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261122-002-r2s1.last cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r2s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261122-002-r2s1.last cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r2s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "Wrote and schema-validated the [structural hypothesis](./cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r2s1.yaml).\n\nIt targets the measured divider critical path by reducing eight serial decisions to three radix-4 decisions per clock. Division takes nine EX cycles, but the timed CoreMark loop executes no DIV or REM instructions.\n\n**Projected fitness is 89 iterations/s, up 25% from 71.23, with medium confidence.** The proposed arithmetic matched reference results on 10,144 operand pairs. FPGA timing remains unmeasured.id: hyp-20261122-002-r2s1\ntitle: \"Shorten divider feedback to three radix-4 digits per clock\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Gowin traces the champion's 36.909 ns worst path from remainder_q to quotient_q inside the divider. Each RUN clock chains eight dependent subtract-and-restore decisions. The champion already demonstrated that increasing divide latency costs no instruction cycles inside the CoreMark timing markers. Its median was 31.991 MHz over a 4,491,485-cycle run. At that cycle count the 4.6% acceptance margin requires just over 33.46 MHz.\nhypothesis: |\n  Rebuild the blocking divider as a clocked radix-4 engine. On issue, capture unsigned operand magnitudes and the existing sign and operation flags. In one preparation cycle, register three times the divisor. Use the registered divisor and its left shift for the other two multiples. Each RUN cycle produces six quotient bits through three successive radix-4 digit decisions. For each incoming bit pair, form T = 4R + pair and compute T-D, T-2D and T-3D in parallel. Select the highest digit with a nonnegative difference, using its difference as the new remainder. Digit zero passes T through.\n\n  Initialize a 36-bit quotient shift register with four leading zeros and the 32-bit dividend magnitude. Six identical RUN cycles consume all 36 bits and leave the quotient in the low 32 bits. Unsigned trials and multiples need 34 bits of magnitude and an explicit borrow bit on subtraction. Apply the existing quotient/remainder selection and sign correction at completion. Use the same issue, preparation, six RUN cycles and result transfer for real arithmetic and ALTOPS. This gives nine EX cycles per divide and a ten-cycle retirement gap after a dependent load on ready buses, within the configured formal liveness window.\n\n  I predict about 40 MHz median Gowin Fmax and roughly the champion's marker-to-marker cycle count, or about 89 CoreMark iterations/s. Measure both independently. The chief uncertainty is whether routing the three parallel trials per digit or a path outside the divider limits Fmax first.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 25\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Implement the preparation register and six-round radix-4 datapath behind the existing div_start, div_ready, div_valid and div_consume interface. Use widened unsigned differences for borrow detection. Preserve signed truncation, dividend-signed remainder, divide-by-zero and INT_MIN / -1 results. Keep DONE stable until consume and reset able to cancel any phase. Drive the captured-operand ALTOPS formulas with the same controller.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Check the preparation cycle and six RUN cycles explicitly in the handshake and reset tests, retaining all arithmetic vectors and held-result assertions. Add unsigned cases at q*D-1, q*D and q*D+1 for q in 1, 2, 3 where representable, plus a small dividend against D=0x55555556 to catch accidental truncation of 3D.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Update the exact retirement-gap expectations in dependent_divides_with_stalled_memory to ten cycles for the load-dependent divide and nine for each immediately following divide or remainder. Retain the one-cycle result-consumer gaps, single memory transfers, ordered retirement and forwarded operand checks.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
