{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r4s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-002-r4s1\ntitle: \"Feed decode from a two-entry fetch queue with same-cycle EX redirects\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 4,491,485 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. It cannot\n  retain a ready fetch while decode waits for data memory, a load result or\n  a divide. At a taken transfer, EX already knows the successor, but fetch\n  waits one edge to present its address. Both costs\n  arise at the combinational fetch-to-decode boundary.\nhypothesis: |\n  Put a two-entry fall-through queue of (PC, instruction) pairs between the\n  external fetch port and decode. Use two indexed slots with one-bit read\n  and write pointers. Issue the oldest buffered word, or pass a ready bus\n  word directly to decode when empty. While decode is held, accept sequential\n  words until the queue fills. Allow a pop and refill on the same edge. Decode\n  can drain the queue on cycles when imemReady is low.\n\n  Give an aligned taken branch, JAL or JALR resolved in EX priority at the\n  instruction address port. Its target response can enter ID/EX on that same\n  edge. Invalidate both sequential entries on redirect. If the target cannot\n  issue, remember the exact target for retry. Store the last request address\n  and one bit indicating whether the next sequential request adds four.\n  On a redirect, set that bit only if imemReady is high and older dmem work\n  can advance. On the sequential path, advance on an accepted bus word with\n  space available.\n  Compare load-use source fields from the sequential head or bus in parallel\n  with redirect resolution. A load in EX cannot also redirect. Keep both RF\n  reads and forwarding matches on the actual word selected for issue.\n  Hold speculative reads at a 4 KiB boundary until queued words drain. A\n  resolved target takes priority over that hold.\n\n  With both bus stalls, same-cycle EX redirects alone took 4,136,096 cycles.\n  The two queue entries saved another 152,920, bringing the marker interval\n  to 3,983,176 cycles with all five canonical CRCs. This cuts cycles by\n  11.32% versus the champion. On the scratch prototype, Gowin\n  measured 58.720, 60.196 and 60.196 MHz, yielding 151.13 iterations/s at\n  the median, 8.33% above 139.50 despite the lower clock. The required median\n  at this cycle count is just over 58.12 MHz. The median placement's worst path\n  crosses the EX ALU to data RAM. Stalled and zero-wait RVFI traces matched\n  the reference on selftest and all three random programs. In the time-limited\n  ALTOPS run, 24 of 53 checks passed, including both PC checks, liveness\n  and unique retirement order. The remaining checks await implementation eval.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Implement a two-slot (PC, word) ring with empty/full tracking and same-cycle pop/refill. Clear occupancy and pointers on reset or redirect. Drive a parked in-range address and ignore its response when a speculative refill would cross a 4 KiB boundary. Remember an unaccepted redirect target for retry.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Produce a decode hold for load-use, dmem and divide waits. Use availability of the selected instruction to decide whether to insert a bubble. A target word delivered on a redirect is eligible to issue.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Connect imemReady and the older dmem hold to fetch. Use the issued PC and word for decoding, both RF reads and adjacent-producer match capture. Feed the load-use comparator directly from the oldest sequential candidate.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: \"Fill both slots behind a stalled load, drain during an imem stall and redirect with both slots occupied. Check target retry, link forwarding, exact RVFI order and suppression of a queued wrong-path store. Exercise a JALR back into RAM from its final word with older work stalled.\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
