{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_15", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-00{2-r5s1,3-r5s2}.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-002-r5s1\ntitle: Replay the issued instruction stream through a second decode input\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Decode currently consumes the live instruction bus. Even with the right\n  next PC, every imem wait creates an empty issue slot. The round-4 fetch\n  queue showed that decode can use buffered instructions during a wait,\n  but it depended on fetching spare words during earlier pipeline holds.\n  Repeated paths offer another source of instructions that can supply a\n  whole run of issue slots during consecutive bus waits.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give decode a registered replay input backed by a circular history of\n  1,024 issued (word PC, instruction) pairs. Record misses in issue order\n  so a newly recorded straight-line run occupies successive slots. At each issued\n  branch or jump, a 128-entry table indexed by the transfer PC points to\n  the history slot of its last observed successor. Read that slot, or the\n  following physical slot for ordinary instructions, in parallel with\n  the existing next-PC calculation.\n\n  A ready instruction bus supplies the live word. During a bus wait,\n  admit the registered word only if its recorded PC equals the current\n  fetch PC and its slot was initialized when read. Each accepted word\n  starts the next lookup, including a word issued from replay. EX still\n  resolves control flow. An aliased restart pointer or an unexpected\n  branch outcome costs a replay opportunity and the live path refills it.\n\n  A scratch prototype reduced ten stalled CoreMark iterations from\n  4,164,193 to 3,493,911 marked cycles with all five canonical CRCs.\n  Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 61.141, 62.392 and 62.392 MHz.\n  The median gives 178.57 iterations/s, 14.85% above 155.48. At this\n  cycle count the acceptance margin requires more than 56.82 MHz. Resource\n  use rose from 3,327 to 3,951 logic cells and from four to seven block\n  RAMs. The prototype passed all 53 standard formal checks. Selftest and\n  the three random programs matched the reference with and without b\n…[4595 of 7667 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4cdb3e3fda7b8b14]…\nt a 512-cycle scrub through the write port. Use live fetches\n      during the scrub. Hold younger issue while FENCE.I occupies EX so\n      an older stalled store can complete before the next word is accepted.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Use the selected word consistently for decode, prediction, both\n      register-file addresses, source readiness and forwarding matches.\n      Feed effective fetch validity to the existing load-use and PC\n      interlocks. Qualify the predictor's issue pulse with if_id_w.valid,\n      !stall_id and !flush_id. Keep EX redirect priority over a fetch hold.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm a loop, then drive unrelated data on a stalled instruction bus\n      during trained fetches. Check RVFI instruction words, PC sequence,\n      order and store results across a data stall and loop exit. Exercise\n      colliding predecessor indices with different successor addresses\n      and verify fresh code after a stalled store followed by FENCE.I.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
