{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r6s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-003-r6s2\ntitle: \"Predict a load's data RAM row one cycle before its address resolves\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The 512-entry instruction successor predictor already recovered 770,138\n  marked cycles. Loads still wait for MEM and interlock an adjacent consumer.\n  An earlier ready-base data-port experiment recovered 243,368 cycles on\n  the previous core. Predicting the data RAM row at issue can recover both\n  an adjacent load-use bubble and a data-bus wait on the same load.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict one of 1024 word rows at issue. With a ready source, add the\n  I-immediate to the bypassed decode base and select sum[11:2]. With a\n  pending base, use the load PC's last observed byte stride to predict its\n  next row.\n  This launches one synchronous block RAM read while the load moves into EX.\n  Compare the candidate's entire word address to the true forwarded load\n  address. A valid, aligned RAM hit can forward the extended word on the\n  next clock and complete locally. On a miss, MEM requests the resolved\n  address and the usual load-use interlock protects a dependent consumer.\n\n  A scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,122,498 marked cycles\n  with both bus stalls and all five canonical CRCs, down from 3,394,055.\n  EX verified 435,226 of 544,852 loads, including 29,196 for which the\n  preceding instruction was still producing the base at issue.\n  Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 gave 61.281, 64.904 and 64.904 MHz.\n  The median yields 207.86 iterations/s, 12.47% above the champion. At\n  this cycle count 60.37 MHz clears the tournament margin. The prototype\n  used 4032 logic cells and nine block RAMs. It passed all 53 fast formal\n  checks, all five cocotb suites and the selftest and three random\n  reference traces with and without bus stalls.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/load_predictor.sv\n    description: |\n      Select the synchronous word-table read address from the decode sum\n      or a 128-entry PC[8:2] stride table. Each st\n…[2013 of 5085 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5f82daeb3cfc1c57]…\nr\n      loads select that value into the EX/MEM result independently of the\n      hit decision. Only a verified hit releases the interlock.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Drive memory addressing, lane masks and alignment checks from the\n      registered effective address. Suppress the bus read on a verified\n      hit and use its saved raw word for RVFI and the saved extended result\n      for decode bypass and WB. Misses, traps, stores and MMIO retain their\n      ordinary handshake and retirement behavior.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Add load_predictor.sv to the pipeline test's RTL source list.\n      Exercise repeated and strided loads with an immediately dependent\n      consumer and dmemReady low on a trained hit. Check a changed stride,\n      aliased load PCs and RAM rows, partial stores, store/read collisions,\n      warm reset, wrong-path loads, alignment traps and signed byte lanes.\n      Verify RVFI memory fields, register results, order and bus requests.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
