ing priority off both EX operand paths at no CoreMark cycle cost, and mapping the register file to Gowin distributed RAM raised median Fmax a further 6.0 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (improvement, +31.3\n…[8468 of 11540 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:966c1af61d1975b0]…\nile to `alternate_pc`, limiting median Fmax to 85.553 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r12s2 (regression, -3.29%): Reading sequential and inline exception words in parallel saved 877 cycles over ten CoreMark iterations, but the path from EX through `issue` to the exception RAM’s read enable limited median Fmax to 86.455 MHz versus the champion’s 89.419 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r13s0 (regression, -21.97%): Rereading words displaced by replay probes on vetoed issues kept the CoreMark cycle penalty to 0.25%, while the path from the EX multiplier through decode to fetch’s `other_pc_q` limited median Fmax to 69.944 MHz versus the champion’s 89.419 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r13s1 (regression, -2.84%): None of CoreMark’s 93,960 timed MULs fed an immediately following branch or JALR, so interlocking those consumers removed product forwarding from fetch recovery without adding cycles, while wide-divider feedback limited the queued core’s median Fmax to 87.145 MHz versus the champion’s 89.419 MHz.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
