{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r13s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-003-r13s2\ntitle: Predict the fetch PC from the successor stored with the replay word\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Two round-12 designs preserved all 3,061,795 marked cycles, yet their\n  longest reported paths ran from instruction selection through the register\n  file and the equality predictor into the saved recovery PC. The replay\n  engine already predicts a word two issues ahead. Repeated instruction\n  sequences can supply the successor of that word on the same read.\nhypothesis: |\n  For each observed sequence P, Q, R, S of accepted instruction PCs, train\n  one synchronous RAM entry indexed by P's word address. Store R's full\n  in-window word PC, its accepted instruction, and S's in-window word PC.\n  On issue at P read the entry, then clock the response into fabric on the\n  next issue. At R a valid full-PC match supplies a successor directly from\n  registers. Use it for next-PC prediction even on a live-bus issue. A ready\n  bus still supplies the architectural instruction. On a predictor miss\n  predict PC+4. The same entry can supply R's instruction during a bus wait.\n  This gives one prediction table a direct PC index and removes both the\n  asynchronous exception lookup and the RF comparison from fetch steering.\n\n  Verify the prediction for every issued instruction. In parallel with ID,\n  compare the proposed word PC with PC+4 and with the current instruction's\n  direct target. Register both match bits and both addresses. EX selects\n  the appropriate match with the resolved direction and alignment result.\n  For a legal taken JALR compare against the full forwarded target after\n  clearing bit zero. Recover to the architectural successor on any mismatch,\n  including changed live code or an ordinary instruction predicted taken.\n  The principal tradeoff is extra recoveries where a source has multiple\n  paths. Measure their cycle cost against the placement-median clock gain.\n  At the original cycle count the acceptance threshold is 93.53 MHz.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the exception and direction tables with a 1024-row sequence\n      table. Pipeline its synchronous response on accepted issues. Use only\n      a validated replay or ready bus word in training, sample S when it\n      actually issues and delay the write. Gate tagged replay on a clean\n      read, including the same-edge write collision. Flush buffered records,\n      training history and pending writes on reset and surviving FENCE.I,\n      retain the scrub and serialize refetch after the older code store.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry the predicted successor and its sequential and direct match metadata across the appropriate pipeline boundaries.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Capture the match bits, direct target, predicted PC and fallthrough with the same issue as the decoded instruction.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Use the registered matches for direct recovery and compare resolved JALR targets explicitly. Qualify recovery with valid EX advance and retain precise trap, link and RVFI behavior.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Remove fetch-only RF readiness and bimodal training connections and route recovery through the existing flush and stall controls.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Exercise an alternating successor, a changed live word, a changing JALR target, read/write aliasing, and a wrong-path store or FENCE.I during a bus hold. Check actual RVFI PCs, words, links, traps and store handshakes for each recovery.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
