{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r14s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-003-r14s2\ntitle: Pipeline conditional branch prediction and verify the direction one cycle later\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  Two round-12 timing reports traced fetch through the register file and the equality comparator to the saved alternate PC. Round 11 also exposed the path from an adjacent producer through EX branch comparison to the fetch enable. The champion takes 3,061,795 marked cycles. Its current one-cycle recovery makes prediction errors cheap, but puts both the prediction and its correction on paths into fetch.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict all six legal conditional branch instructions from a PC-indexed two-bit counter in fetch. Use the decoded displacement for the target and retain the existing alignment and fetch-window checks. At issue, capture the predicted direction, the direct target and PC+4. Remove the IF register-file equality comparison and the direction-controlled wide alternate-PC mux from the conditional branch path.\n\n  Verify conditional predictions in two stages. EX compares the actual forwarded operands and registers a misprediction bit when the branch advances. In parallel, register its recovery word address, selecting between the two captured alternatives using the predicted direction. For a direction mismatch the other successor is precisely the one wanted, including fallthrough on a taken misaligned branch. In the following cycle this registered event redirects fetch and annuls the one younger instruction already in EX as well as the instruction in decode. A correct prediction continues at one instruction per cycle. A miss costs one additional cycle. Keep the existing exact JAL and JALR resolution and let the older registered correction win every conflict.\n\n  The experiment trades the extra recovery cycles for shorter paths at both ends of the predictor. Measure conditional recoveries and marked cycles together with the median Gowin Fmax. At 3.10 million cycles a median above 94.70 MHz clears the tournament margin.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Use the existing swept bimodal table for conditional direction. Drive fetch from that bit and the checked direct target. Retain the tagged two-issue replay mechanism and fence serialization.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Drop the fetch-only equality operand connections, retaining the ready base for JALR. Route the registered conditional correction with priority over younger EX redirects and interlocks.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry the branch prediction and both successor addresses through ID/EX using the existing PC+4 and direct-target fields. Remove any redundant recovery field after accounting for direct jumps.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Capture target, fallthrough and direction with the issued branch and hold them across stalls.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Register conditional outcome disagreement and its alternative address on a valid branch advance. During recovery annul younger EX/MEM valid and controls, divider start, predictor training and younger redirects. Retire the resolving branch once with the true successor and precise trap state.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Check cold and warm branch directions, aliased counters, misaligned targets and an older waiting store. Force a miss with a younger store, divide, branch or FENCE.I. Check RVFI PCs, order and memory handshakes, including coincident redirect and front-end holds.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
