{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"baseline-bench-29140aa\", \"title\": \"Baseline retest for cores/bench\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 12.12, \"delta_pct\": 0.0, \"vs_baseline\": 0.0, \"fmax_mhz\": 5.442, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 12312, \"ff\": 460, \"seeds\": [5.14, 5.442, 5.442], \"perturbations\": [[0, 5.14, 150], [1, 5.442, 149], [2, 5.442, 149]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_mem/reg_q.rd_3_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_0_dmem_0_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -189.542, \"arrival_ns\": 199.837, \"logic_cells\": 156, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_fwd\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 150, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T02:35:47.912155Z\", \"round_id\": 0, \"slot\": 0}\n{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-001-r1s0\", \"title\": \"Resolve two quotient bits per step in the combinational divider\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline's 199.837 ns worst path runs from the MEM/WB destination\\nregister through forwarding and the EX ALU. The ALU evaluates signed and\\nunsigned division and remainder combinationally. A chain of quotient-bit\\ndecisions is a plausible source of the reported 156-cell path. Shortening\\nit would raise the clock rate for every CoreMark instruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline's 199.837 ns worst path runs from the MEM/WB destination\\nregister through forwarding and the EX ALU. The ALU evaluates signed and\\nunsigned division and remainder combinationally. A chain of quotient-bit\\ndecisions is a plausible source of the reported 156-cell path. Shortening\\nit would raise the clock rate for every CoreMark instruction.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Implement DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU with one unrolled radix-4 restoring\\ndivider inside the ALU. Each of 16 steps consumes two dividend bits and\\ncomputes three trial differences in parallel. Their borrow flags select\\nboth the next quotient digit and an already computed remainder. This\\nhalves the numb\n…[11070 of 14142 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e70f85c3a30c6636]…\n\\n\"}], \"outcome\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 71.23, \"delta_pct\": 487.71, \"vs_baseline\": 487.71, \"fmax_mhz\": 31.991, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 2883, \"ff\": 562, \"seeds\": [31.631, 31.991, 31.991], \"perturbations\": [[0, 31.631, 48], [1, 31.991, 47], [2, 31.991, 47]], \"lutram\": 8, \"bsram\": 6, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/remainder_q_4_s0\", \"to\": \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu/quotient_q_0_s1\", \"slack_ns\": -26.615, \"arrival_ns\": 36.909, \"logic_cells\": 254, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 48, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T02:44:51.627537Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 1, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +487.71%): Limiting the restoring divider to eight quotient steps per clock shortened the EX critical path, and its six-cycle latency cost no timed CoreMark cycles because `iterate` and its callees execute no DIV or REM instructions.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261122-003-r1s2.last cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r1s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "Wrote and schema-validated the [128-entry bimodal predictor hypothesis](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-astra_xhigh-v2-rep5/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261122-003-r1s2-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r1s2.yaml).\n\nThe prototype reached **13.06 CoreMark iter/s**, 7.75% above the 12.12 baseline and above the 4.6% acceptance threshold. It saved 289,213 bracketed cycles, achieved a median Gowin Fmax of 5.488 MHz across three placements, and matched all five CoreMark CRCs.id: hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\ntitle: Predict direct branches at fetch with a 128-entry bimodal table\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  IF and ID share a cycle in this core. Fetch always selects PC+4, so a taken\n  transfer resolved in EX discards one fetch slot. Between the MMIO markers,\n  294,425 conditional branches select a nonsequential PC and 49,367 JALs\n  execute. With both bus stalls enabled, the 128-entry prototype completed\n  ten CoreMark iterations in 4,202,272 bracketed cycles and matched all five\n  canonical CRCs. This saves 289,213 cycles and raises iterations/cycle by 6.88%.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use the fetched instruction to identify legal conditional branches and JAL.\n  In parallel with immediate generation, read a 128-entry table of two-bit\n  saturating counters indexed by the fetch PC. For a conditional branch use\n  the counter MSB, and for JAL predict taken. Select the aligned PC-relative\n  target on a taken prediction and PC+4 otherwise. JALR resolves in EX.\n  Computing direct targets from the current instruction makes table aliasing\n  affect only direction and requires just one prediction bit per instruction.\n\n  After initialization, train once per valid conditional branch advancing\n  from EX. A correct prediction lets an accepted fetch enter ID/EX on the\n  resolution edge.\n  A mismatch flushes that fetch and loads the architectural successor into\n  the PC. The prototype's three Gowin placement options reached 5.155,\n  5.488 and 5.488 MHz. Combining the median with the measured cycle count\n  yields 13.06 iter/s, 7.\n…[1272 of 4344 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e83bad1eb7ccf2dd]…\n_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Derive actual_taken from the forwarded branch comparison or jump and\n      the existing misalignment check. On valid EX advance, redirect only on\n      a direction mismatch, to the resolved target or PC+4 as appropriate.\n      Use the same resolved successor for RVFI pc_next. Emit the training PC,\n      direction and one update pulse per advancing legal conditional branch.\n      Preserve the existing trap and link-write behavior.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Wire EX training to IF and use the qualified EX recovery pulse for the existing hazard flush and PC redirect inputs.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      After table initialization, check a warmed taken loop and its exit.\n      Exercise two oppositely biased branches at the same index and hold\n      a resolving branch behind a stalled data access.\n      Check fetch timing on a correct prediction, recovery across an imem wait,\n      RVFI successor/order and suppression of a wrong-path store.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
