{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/.agent.hyp-20261122-003-r3s2.last cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r3s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "Wrote and schema-validated [hyp-20261122-003-r3s2.yaml](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6_2-astra_xhigh-v2-rep5/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20261122-003-r3s2-hypgen/cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r3s2.yaml). The predictor uses ready register values during fetch to resolve conditional branches and zero-offset JALRs. A 128-entry direction table predicts branches whose operands are still pending.\n\nThe prototype reached **118.94 CoreMark iter/s**, **12.0% above** the 106.20 champion, at 49.383 MHz median over 4,151,767 bracketed cycles. All five CoreMark CRCs matched, and all 53 standard formal checks passed.id: hyp-20261122-003-r3s2\ntitle: Predict control transfers from ready operands during fetch\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The 47.7 MHz radix-4 champion takes 4,491,485 cycles for ten marked\n  CoreMark iterations. IF and ID share a cycle, so every EX redirect\n  discards one fetch slot. The previous 128-entry direction predictor\n  reduced that count to 4,202,272 on two earlier cores. The regfile read\n  ports and the previous instruction results are already visible during\n  fetch. They can resolve data-dependent branch directions and common\n  zero-offset JALR targets before the next PC is clocked.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use the youngest available producer to predict control flow. For each\n  source of the fetched instruction, start with the write-first regfile\n  value. Bypass a matching non-load result from EX/MEM. Mark the source\n  busy on a matching ID/EX writer or a matching EX/MEM load. The ID/EX\n  match takes priority even when EX/MEM also matches. Ignore writes to x0.\n\n  For a legal conditional branch with both sources ready, evaluate its\n  comparison in IF. If either is busy, predict from a 128-entry table\n  of two-bit counters. Form one PC-relative sum for branches and JAL.\n  For a legal zero-offset JALR with rs1 ready, use the ready value with\n  bit zero cleared. Other JALRs predict PC+4. Require every speculative\n  target to be word aligned and inside the 1 MiB instruction window.\n\n  Carry the ch\n…[3053 of 6125 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:91d8d557b1904190]…\nnditional branch by saturating\n      its captured counter toward the actual direction and writing at the\n      resolving PC index. A concurrent same-index fetch can use the old\n      counter. A dmem freeze defers both training and recovery until release.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Qualify load-use comparisons against the raw fetch fields with imem_ready so an undelivered instruction cannot request a dependency stall.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check fetch addresses on ready signed and unsigned branches, busy\n      loop predictions and loop exit. Cover a JALR using WB bypass, one\n      using EX/MEM, and one with two pending writers to rs1 where the\n      younger writer must win. Exercise an unavailable load result,\n      nonzero JALR offset, bit-zero clearing and a misaligned target.\n      Hold a branch behind a stalled data access and deny imem on the\n      recovery edge. Check RVFI successor, link, trap and order, plus\n      suppression of a wrong-path store.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'tail -n 2 cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'", "aggregated_output": "{\"id\": \"hyp-20261122-001-r3s0\", \"title\": \"Select EX forwarding sources one cycle earlier in decode\", \"category\": \"micro_opt\", \"motivation\": \"The champion reaches 47.7 MHz and runs ten CoreMark iterations in\\n4,491,485 cycles. Its median Gowin placement shows two nearly equal\\noperand paths. One feeds a branch and the fetch interlock at 26.259 ns.\\nThe other feeds the multiplier and the data-memory address at 26.246 ns.\\nBoth pay for register-file write-through and EX forwarding before their\\nresults can be used. Decode already has the consumer's source numbers\\nand the destinations of all three older potential writers.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The champion reaches 47.7 MHz and runs ten CoreMark iterations in\\n4,491,485 cycles. Its median Gowin placement shows two nearly equal\\noperand paths. One feeds a branch and the fetch interlock at 26.259 ns.\\nThe other feeds the multiplier and the data-memory address at 26.246 ns.\\nBoth pay for register-file write-through and EX forwarding before their\\nresults can be used. Decode already has the consumer's source numbers\\nand the destinations of all three older potential writers.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Compute two four-way, one-hot forwarding selections during decode, one\\nper source. Compare raw fetch bits 19:15 and 24:20 with the destinations\\ncurrently in EX, MEM and WB. Reject x0 and qualify each match with the\\nproducer's effective write enable. Resolve overlapping matches youngest\\nfirst. At the ID/EX edge, register the selections and a single shared\\ncopy of current WB data alongside the instruction's register-file reads.\\n\\nIn the consumer's EX cycle the four inputs, in priority order, are the\\nEX/MEM result, MEM/WB writeback data, the saved decode-cycle WB data and\\nthe saved register-file operand. Mask each input with its registered\\nselect bit and OR the four words. The third input covers writes colliding\\nwith a register-file read in ID. This moves the write-through choice\\nand forwarding priority off both the branch and multiply operand paths.\\n\\nAn isola\n…[14425 of 17497 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4c920424b3779d84]…\nme\": \"improvement\", \"fitness\": 139.5, \"delta_pct\": 31.36, \"vs_baseline\": 1050.99, \"fmax_mhz\": 62.655, \"ipc_coremark\": 2e-06, \"cycles\": 4491485, \"iterations\": 10, \"lut4\": 3150, \"ff\": 573, \"seeds\": [60.653, 62.655, 62.655], \"perturbations\": [[0, 60.653, 10], [1, 62.655, 10], [2, 62.655, 10]], \"lutram\": 32, \"bsram\": 4, \"dsp\": 6, \"critical_path\": {\"from\": \"cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_25_s0\", \"to\": \"dmem_2_dmem_2_0_0_s\", \"slack_ns\": -11.487, \"arrival_ns\": 21.782, \"logic_cells\": 10, \"modules\": [\"clock_ibuf\", \"cpu/u_ex\", \"cpu/u_ex/u_alu\", \"cpu/u_ex\"]}, \"logic_levels\": 10, \"formal_passed\": true, \"cosim_passed\": true, \"error\": null, \"implementation_notes\": \"\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-11-22T03:50:51.091903Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 3, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (improvement, +31.36%): Resolving MEM and WB bypasses in ID and registering each source\\u2019s match with the adjacent producer reduced EX forwarding to one 2:1 mux per operand, raising Fmax while preserving CoreMark\\u2019s 4,491,485 timed cycles.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
