g selects and WB data at ID/EX moved forwarding priority off both EX operand paths at no CoreMark cycle cost, and mapping the register file to Gowin distributed RAM raised median Fmax a further 6.0 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (improvement, +31.3\n…[2815 of 5887 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c98507b76f9ef9de]…\nnsecutive EX cycles, and writing results at MEM completion lets decode use the register file’s write-first bypass for both early and retried loads.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r6s2 (improvement, +11.24%): Selecting the data RAM row in ID from the base plus immediate when ready or a per-PC stride prediction when the base is pending lets EX verify the full word address and forward a hit to an adjacent consumer without a load-use bubble or a MEM bus request.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r7s0 (regression, -4.29%): Registering both DSP operands left the fetch path from the successor table to the PC limiting median Fmax to 62.015 MHz, while adjacent producer dependencies forced a one-cycle EX repair on 29,155 of CoreMark’s 93,960 MULs.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r7s1 (improvement, +11.38%): Registering the full product beside the ALU result at EX/MEM moves word selection off the EX multiply path, and forwarding the selected word from MEM lets even dependent multiplies execute on consecutive advancing cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
