{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_66","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor x in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if 'r7s0' in x['id']:print(x['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_66","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor x in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if 'r7s0' in x['id']:print(x['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Give the multiplier registered inputs. Capture the two MEM/WB-bypassed\noperands on decode issue, with the signedness appropriate to MUL,\nMULH, MULHSU or MULHU. The DSP can start directly from these flops.\nA multiply with an unresolved adjacent writer takes one repair cycle\nin EX to overwrite the same input flops with the actual forwarded\nvalues. It then executes through the identical product path. This\nremoves both the adjacent bypass and PC/immediate selection from the\nDSP inputs while preserving one-cycle issue for independent multiplies.\nMeasure repair cycles and median Fmax together. The acceptance clock\nis 67.15 MHz at the original cycle count and about 69.2 MHz if every\nmultiply costs one extra cycle. Fetch and divider timing are the main\nrisks to reaching that range.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
