{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if 'r10s1' in r['id'] or 'r11s0' in r['id'] or 'r10s0' in r['id']: print(r['id'], r['hypothesis'],r['critical_path'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-001-r10s0 {'motivation': \"On the champion's median placement, a DSP result passes through rs1 forwarding, address addition and the load-hit comparison before reaching ID control. The hit decision can use the address already needed for the synchronous RAM read. Limiting this fast path to a known base and a displacement that stays on its page removes EX forwarding from the load-use interlock.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"In ID, add the 12-bit I displacement to two possible base values in parallel. One comes from the raw register-file read and one from the registered EX/MEM result. A matching MEM writer selects the second sum. A matching writer still in EX, or a selected MEM load awaiting external data, makes the probe ineligible. This keeps the live dmem response off the RAM address path.\\n\\nRead the existing 1,024-word RAM using bits 11 through 2 of the selected sum. Capture the byte offset, base selector and immediate sign. For each source separately, register its page bits 19 through 12, its in-RAM flag and the carry out of its low 12-bit addition. EX uses the selected source's metadata. When its carry equals the displacement sign, the full effective address is on the base page. The RAM row already identifies the word within that page, so each entry needs only its eight-bit page tag, valid bit and data. The PC-indexed stride tables can be removed.\\n\\nAn aligned, eligible load with a valid matching RAM page can forward on the existing schedule. All other loads take the ordinary path using the full forwarded effective address. The raw word, captured byte offset, read/write collision test, older-store veto and reset scrub stay aligned across dmem holds.\\n\\nThe prototype completed ten bracketed iterations in 3,153,333 cycles with the five canonical CRCs. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 81.535, 89.230 and 89.230 MHz. The median gives 282.97 iterations/s, 3.61% above the champion. Replacement requires a median above 90.082 MHz at that cycle count. The selftest and three random instruction streams matched the re\n…[9419 of 12491 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:53b937a4f7198cac]…\n and RVFI.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': \"Exercise move-to-load and LW-to-load chains, a stale pointer word\\nfollowed by a stalled refill, an aliased page, a displacement that\\ncrosses a word, and an adjacent multiply producing a base. Check\\naccepted reads, RVFI values and retirement gaps. Exercise the new\\nindex bypass in the existing stride-specific cases and retain the\\nfirst-row and last-row warm-reset and older-store collision checks.\\nIn the stopped-bus replay test use a pointer of 0x80 with offsets\\n0 and 1 to exercise the same two byte lanes. In the changing-stride\\ncopy chain, expect reads at 0x3e and 0x41 after warm-up and hits\\nfor the prefilled rows. Set the reset test's high base to 4092 and load\\nwith offset zero so the last row is eligible before and after reset.\\n\"}]} {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/rs2_ordinary_q_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/other_pc_q_2_s1', 'slack_ns': -7.263, 'arrival_ns': 17.557, 'logic_cells': 40, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu', 'cpu/u_if']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
