lip-flop output to start from. The tradeoff is that a PC two issues back can identify the desired instruction less often at a branch.\nhyp-20261122-002-r9s1 The champion scores 250.15 iterations/s. Its reported 18.321 ns path runs from the successor block RAM through the register file to lookup_ok_q. Fetch currently selects the live or replayed instruction before reading rs1 and rs2 for both early branch resolution and the speculative\n…[2001 of 5073 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0b4f5bfd7a531031]…\n word index for those chains can reach the RAM before the producer traverses the ordinary EX bypass. The champion also puts rs1 forwarding, a full address add and a tag comparison on the load-use interlock.\nhyp-20261122-002-r11s1 Of the champion's 108,865 load-table misses in ten marked CoreMark iterations, 94,913 had an adjacent producer for the base. Round 10 showed that probing from the producer could save 69,286 cycles, but its 86.169 MHz median fell short. The existing single read port puts row selection before the RAM and the full forwarded address check before fetch can release a dependent consumer.\nhyp-20261122-003-r11s2 In round 10, 94,913 of 108,865 load-table misses had an adjacent writer of the base register. A stride indexed by the load PC has little information about a pointer just read from a linked list. The producer has a candidate pointer or operands for an address sum a cycle earlier. The champion also puts full load-address verification on the path that enables the instruction replay RAM.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
