{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_55", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor d in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if d['round_id']==12 or d['id'].endswith('r9s2'): print(d['title'],d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Predict the instruction RAM row two issues ahead On each accepted issue at PC P, launch a synchronous read for the instruction expected two accepted issues later. Use the word row for P+8 by default. A 128-entry distributed RAM indexed by P[8:2] records exceptions. Each entry holds a valid bit, four more PC tag bits and a ten-bit destination row. Keep two issued PCs' prediction metadata. When a live bus word at Q issues, train the older PC's exception if Q's row differs from its sequential prediction, or clear its previously matched exception if the stream returned to sequential execution. Buffer the fill and training transaction for one cycle.\n\nFill a 1,024-word block RAM at Q[11:2] with the instruction, a valid bit and the full in-window word PC. Read the predicted row on issue, then capture its returned word and tag in fabric registers on the next issue. Both stages hold across stalls. On an imem wait issue the registered word only when its valid full address matches the actual aligned PC in the RAM window. A live bus word always wins. Flush both lookahead stages, history and pending fills on reset or an issued FENCE.I, scrub the word store and retain the existing fence/store interlock. Reject read/write collisions. A redirect never advances issue history and every candidate is still checked against the redirected PC.\n\nA scratch build completed ten bracketed iterations in 3,131,081 cycles with all five canonical CRCs and the validation banner. Gowin placement 1 reached 80.366 MHz. The tournament requires a median above 81.93 MHz at that cycle count, so the implementation's clock margin is the open question.\n\nSelect the instruction replay row after the block RAM read Read PC+8 and the exception entry's row concurrently from two copies\nof the instruction-word RAM. Register the exception tag-match bit with\nthose reads. At the next accepted issue use that bit to select which RAM\noutput enters the existing fabric candidate register. Register the two\nread addresses and concurrent fill metadata at the read edge and t\n…[2219 of 5291 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4b4ee39cb6b1dded]…\ntag match, otherwise select the sequential response. Register the\nselected word before decode. Sample read and simultaneous fill addresses\nindependently at the read edge and veto a collision in the response\nstage. A stalled instruction bus can use the registered word only on a\nfull in-window destination-PC match. A ready bus always supplies its\nlive word. Train the exceptional bank on live accepted nonsequential\ndestinations using the source from two accepted issues ago. Retain\nsequential fallback across source aliases. Keep both response stages\naligned through holds and redirects, and invalidate both banks and all\npending response and fill permissions on reset or surviving FENCE.I.\n\nThis targets fetch timing while preserving the ability to issue through\nconsecutive bus waits. At the champion's cycle count, replacement\nrequires a placement median above 93.53 MHz. Measure both marked cycles\nand median Fmax because an inline exception can have a different miss\nrate from a pointer to a shared destination word.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
