{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if any(k in r['id'] for k in ['r12','r13','r11s1']):\n  print(r['id'],r['hypothesis'],r['changes'],r['perturbations'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-002-r11s1 {'motivation': \"Of the champion's 108,865 load-table misses in ten marked CoreMark iterations,\\n94,913 had an adjacent producer for the base. Round 10 showed that probing from\\nthe producer could save 69,286 cycles, but its 86.169 MHz median fell short.\\nThe existing single read port puts row selection before the RAM and the full\\nforwarded address check before fetch can release a dependent consumer.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Split the load lookup into two independently addressed synchronous read ports\\nwith identical contents. One always probes the write-first RF base plus the\\nload displacement. The other probes the low word index of the adjacent\\nproducer's prospective result. For ADD or ADDI, capture a 12-bit sum from\\nready operands in ID. For LW, tap bits 11:2 of its raw primary-port word.\\nRemove the per-PC stride tables. Both ports read in parallel with issue.\\n\\nVerify each response at its point of use. For a ready base, the sampled row\\nalready includes the displacement. Compare the captured base page to the\\nreturned page and accept only when the low 12-bit carry equals the displacement\\nsign, so the access stayed on that page. For a pending ADD, ADDI or LW base\\nand a displacement of 0 through 3, compare the ordinary EX/MEM result bits\\n31:2 directly to the pending port's zero-extended page and registered row.\\nCheck the two-bit lane addition for word crossing and the access for alignment.\\nThese tests let the load-use interlock use the two parallel verdicts while\\nthe full forwarded effective address is computed independently for MEM and RVFI.\\nA failed check takes the ordinary MEM access and dependency bubble.\\n\\nA scratch implementation completed ten iterations in 3,061,795 marked cycles\\nwith all five canonical CRCs. Gowin placements reached 79.600, 86.496 and\\n86.496 MHz, giving 282.50 iterations/s, about 3.4 percent above the champion.\\nReplacement at this cycle count requires more than 87.47 MHz.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 3, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes':\n…[25726 of 28798 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c6cba1b04efb8fbd]…\ny the predicted successor and its sequential and direct match metadata across the appropriate pipeline boundaries.'}, {'file': 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'description': 'Capture the match bits, direct target, predicted PC and fallthrough with the same issue as the decoded instruction.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Use the registered matches for direct recovery and compare resolved JALR targets explicitly. Qualify recovery with valid EX advance and retain precise trap, link and RVFI behavior.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Remove fetch-only RF readiness and bimodal training connections and route recovery through the existing flush and stall controls.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Exercise an alternating successor, a changed live word, a changing JALR target, read/write aliasing, and a wrong-path store or FENCE.I during a bus hold. Check actual RVFI PCs, words, links, traps and store handshakes for each recovery.'}] [[0, 84.754, 12], [1, 84.035, 11], [2, 84.035, 11]]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
