{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv && cat test/cosim/build.sh'", "aggregated_output": "// IF and ID share the instruction bus and register-file read addresses.\n// The PC is the fetch register. ID/EX captures the prediction on issue.\nmodule if_stage (\n  input  logic        clock,\n  input  logic        reset,\n  input  logic        stall,\n  input  logic        issue,\n  input  logic        stall_id,\n  input  logic        redirect,\n  input  logic [31:2] redirect_target,\n  input  logic [31:0] rs1_data,\n  input  logic [31:0] rs2_data,\n  input  logic        rs1_ready,\n  input  logic        rs2_ready,\n  input  logic        train,\n  input  logic [6:0]  train_index,\n  input  logic [1:0]  train_counter,\n  output logic [31:0] imem_addr,\n  input  logic [31:0] imem_data,\n  input  logic        imem_ready,\n  output if_id_t      out\n);\n  logic [31:2] pc_word;\n  logic [31:0] pc, pc_plus4, direct_target, direct_offset;\n  logic        is_jal, is_branch, eq_branch_ready, branch_direction;\n  logic        direct_taken, jalr_taken;\n\n  // P predicts the word for the second issue after P. An exception only\n  // steers the RAM read. The word itself carries the full in-window PC.\n  typedef struct packed {\n    logic        valid;\n    logic [19:2] pc;\n    logic [31:0] instr;\n  } fetch_word_t;\n  typedef struct packed {\n    logic        valid;\n    logic [3:0]  tag;\n    logic [9:0]  row;\n  } exception_t;\n  typedef struct packed {\n    logic [12:2] source;\n    logic        matched;\n  } history_t;\n\n  (* syn_ramstyle = \"distributed_ram\" *) logic [14:0] exceptions [0:127];\n  (* syn_ramstyle = \"block_ram\" *) logic [50:0] words [0:1023];\n  exception_t exception_entry;\n  history_t recent, older;\n  logic [1:0] history_valid;\n  logic [10:0] scrub;\n  logic [9:0] sequential_row, read_row, write_row;\n  logic matched, nonsequential, in_window, hit;\n  logic fill_q, train_exception_q, exception_valid_q;\n  logic [12:2] source_q;\n  logic [19:2] fill_pc_q;\n  logic [31:0] fill_instr_q;\n  logic [6:0] exception_write_index;\n  fetch_word_t ram_word;\n  // Preserve the fabric stage that separates the block RAM from decode.\n  (* syn_preserve = 1 *) fetch_wo\n…[6992 of 10064 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:82878ab37adb57ba]…\nRET:-2}\"\n\n# Ensure OSS CAD Suite tools are on PATH for non-interactive shells.\nTOOLCHAIN=\"$REPO_ROOT/.toolchain\"\nif [ -d \"$TOOLCHAIN/oss-cad-suite/bin\" ]; then\n  export PATH=\"$TOOLCHAIN/oss-cad-suite/bin:$PATH\"\nfi\n\nmkdir -p \"$OBJ_DIR\"\n\n# Glob $RTL_DIR/*.sv. core_pkg.sv first; the rest in a stable lexicographic\n# order (modulo case-insensitive sort, which Verilator/gcc don't care\n# about). If core_pkg.sv is missing the build will catch that downstream\n# via undefined-typedef errors.\nRTL_FILES=()\n[ -f \"$RTL_DIR/core_pkg.sv\" ] && RTL_FILES+=(\"$RTL_DIR/core_pkg.sv\")\nfor f in \"$RTL_DIR\"/*.sv; do\n  [ \"$(basename \"$f\")\" = \"core_pkg.sv\" ] && continue\n  RTL_FILES+=(\"$f\")\ndone\n\nif [ ${#RTL_FILES[@]} -eq 0 ]; then\n  echo \"ERROR: no rtl/*.sv files found.\" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\n\nverilator --cc --exe --build \\\n  -Mdir \"$OBJ_DIR\" \\\n  \"+incdir+$RTL_DIR\" \\\n  --top-module core \\\n  -Wall -Wno-fatal -Wno-style \\\n  -CFLAGS \"-DNRET=$NRET\" \\\n  \"${RTL_FILES[@]}\" \\\n  \"$COSIM_DIR/main.cpp\" \\\n  -o cosim_sim\n\necho \"Built: $OBJ_DIR/cosim_sim\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'\nfor l in open(p):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if 'r13s0' in r['id'] or 'r5s2' in r['id'] or 'r7s2' in r['id']:\n  h=r.get('hypothesis','')\n  print(r['id'],h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h, dict) else h)\nPY\ncat bench/programs/Makefile | head -112\nrg -n 'coremark|usage|argc|argv|--' test/cosim/main.cpp | tail -65\nls /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh\ncommand -v riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\np='cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'\nfor l in open(p):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if 'r13s0' in r['id'] or 'r5s2' in r['id'] or 'r7s2' in r['id']:\n  h=r.get('hypothesis','')\n  print(r['id'],h.get('hypothesis','') if isinstance(h, dict) else h)\nPY\ncat bench/programs/Makefile | head -112\nrg -n 'coremark|usage|argc|argv|--' test/cosim/main.cpp | tail -65\nls /opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh\ncommand -v riscv64-unknown-elf-gcc\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r5s2 Add a 512-row last-successor predictor indexed by the issuing PC[10:2].\nAfter instruction A is followed by B, A's row holds one valid bit, B's\nPC[19:2] and B's instruction word. When A issues again, read its row\nsynchronously while the existing direction and target logic selects the\nnext PC. Use the live word whenever imemReady is high. On a bus stall,\nissue the saved word only if its address exactly matches the requested\nPC inside the 1 MiB instruction window. If a branch changes direction\nor two predecessor PCs alias, a tag miss waits for the bus.\n\nAn isolated RTL prototype completed ten CoreMark iterations in 3,394,055\nmarked cycles with both stall generators enabled and all five CRCs correct.\nIt saved 770,138 cycles and used two additional block RAMs. Gowin\nplacement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 59.839, 61.317 and 61.317 MHz.\nThe median gives 180.66 iterations/s, 16.20% above the champion.\nAt this cycle count, 55.20 MHz clears the acceptance margin. The prototype\npassed all 53 fast formal checks and the directed stalled-bus replay tests.\n\nhyp-20261122-003-r7s2 Predict the next PC from a 128-entry tagged branch target buffer and the\nexisting two-bit direction counters. Use that address both to advance\nfetch and to launch a synchronous read of a 1,024-word instruction RAM.\nA single fill at a destination PC then serves sequential, taken and\nrecovery paths to that instruction. The predicted address is available\nwithout waiting for decode to read branch operands.\n\nSplit the instruction field instr[31:15] from the block RAM row. It\ncontains both RF addresses and the I-immediate used to predict a load\nrow. Give these 17 bits a distributed RAM with an asynchronous read at\nthe predicted next index and a capture flop on the same edge as the\nblock RAM read. Reassemble the saved instruction in decode. This makes\nthe saved operand fields available at flop clock-to-Q and keeps the\ndata-RAM address calculation out of the instruction block RAM output path.\n\nA prototype with the entire instruction in block\n…[4341 of 7413 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d40e6548dc97e26d]…\ngc, char** argv) {\n62:    if(argc < 2) {\n63:        std::cerr << \"usage: sim <elf> [maxcycles] [--bench] [--istall] [--dstall] [--seed N]\\n\";\n66:    uint64_t maxcycles = argc > 2 ? atoll(argv[2]) : 50000000ULL;\n67:    // --bench:  suppress per-retirement output; print only the final record.\n68:    // --istall: random ~22% imem-bus backpressure (VexRiscv-style).\n69:    // --dstall: same on dmem bus. The orchestrator's fitness eval\n70:    //           (tools/eval/fpga.py:run_coremark_ipc) passes BOTH so the\n76:    for (int i = 1; i < argc; i++) {\n77:        if      (std::strcmp(argv[i], \"--bench\")  == 0) bench_mode = true;\n78:        else if (std::strcmp(argv[i], \"--istall\") == 0) istall     = true;\n79:        else if (std::strcmp(argv[i], \"--dstall\") == 0) dstall     = true;\n80:        else if (std::strcmp(argv[i], \"--seed\")   == 0 && i + 1 < argc)\n81:            lfsr_state_main = (uint32_t)atoll(argv[++i]);\n84:    ELF elf; elf.load(argv[1]);\n97:    Verilated::commandArgs(argc, argv);\n/opt/gowin/IDE/bin/gw_sh\n", "exit_code": 1, "status": "failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if 'r9s0' in r['id'] or 'r9s2' in r['id']:\n  print(r['id'],r['hypothesis'].get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if 'r9s0' in r['id'] or 'r9s2' in r['id']:\n  print(r['id'],r['hypothesis'].get('hypothesis',''))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20261122-001-r9s0 Retime the existing 512-row instruction replay RAM. For three successive\nissued instructions A, B and C, read row A when A issues. On issue of B,\ntransfer that response into a fabric register for use at C. Train row A\nwith C's word PC and instruction when C issues from a ready bus. Replay\nrequires a valid entry and an exact match to the current PC inside the\nRAM window. Live bus data takes priority.\n\nRead the block RAM every clock. If issue pauses between A and B, save\nA's response on the first intervening edge and hold it until B issues.\nQueue each training write for the following clock. These two port\nchanges remove the late issue decision from both RAM enables.\n\nA scratch prototype completed ten iterations with randomized instruction\nand data stalls in 3,148,248 marked cycles versus 3,132,302 for the\nchampion. All five CRCs and the validation banner matched. Gowin\nplacements 0, 1 and 2 reached 78.619, 82.612 and 82.612 MHz. The\n5.44 percent median clock gain outweighs the 0.51 percent cycle\nincrease. The resulting throughput is 262.41 iterations/s, 4.90 percent\nabove 250.15 and just above the 261.66 cutoff. Placement 0 used\n4,056 logic cells, 917 registers, nine block\nRAMs and two DSPs. All five cocotb suites passed, including the new\nshared-JALR replay test. Selftest and all three random programs matched\nthe reference RVFI traces on both ready and stalled buses. All 53\nfast formal checks passed.\n\nhyp-20261122-003-r9s2 On each accepted issue at PC P, launch a synchronous read for the instruction expected two accepted issues later. Use the word row for P+8 by default. A 128-entry distributed RAM indexed by P[8:2] records exceptions. Each entry holds a valid bit, four more PC tag bits and a ten-bit destination row. Keep two issued PCs' prediction metadata. When a live bus word at Q issues, train the older PC's exception if Q's row differs from its sequential prediction, or clear its previously matched exception if the stream returned to sequential execution. Buffer the fill and training transaction for one cycle.\n\nFill a 1,024-word block RAM at Q[11:2] with the instruction, a valid bit and the full in-window word PC. Read the predicted row on issue, then capture its returned word and tag in fabric registers on the next issue. Both stages hold across stalls. On an imem wait issue the registered word only when its valid full address matches the actual aligned PC in the RAM window. A live bus word always wins. Flush both lookahead stages, history and pending fills on reset or an issued FENCE.I, scrub the word store and retain the existing fence/store interlock. Reject read/write collisions. A redirect never advances issue history and every candidate is still checked against the redirected PC.\n\nA scratch build completed ten bracketed iterations in 3,131,081 cycles with all five canonical CRCs and the validation banner. Gowin placement 1 reached 80.366 MHz. The tournament requires a median above 81.93 MHz at that cycle count, so the implementation's clock margin is the open question.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
