{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_13", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('round_id') in (12,13,14):\n  print(d['id'],str(d['motivation'])[:1600])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-001-r12s0 On the champion's 89.419 MHz median placement the longest reported setup\npath starts at the fetch PC selector and ends at read_ok_q. It crosses\nthe asynchronous exception RAM, its tag check, the replay read-address\nmux and the comparison against the concurrent fill row. The two-issue\nlookahead gives fetch an intervening clock edge at which to select the word.\n\nhyp-20261122-002-r12s1 The champion takes 3,061,795 cycles for ten marked iterations. On placement 0,\nits longest path runs from the adjacent producer's result to the clock enable\nof fetch history. On placements 1 and 2 the longest path ends at read_ok_q.\nA load-hit check or branch correction can arrive late because the replay\nRAM, its candidate register and the two-PC history all advance on issue.\n\nhyp-20261122-003-r12s2 The champion takes 3,061,795 marked cycles. Reproducing its 89.419 MHz\nmedian placement shows the longest path from the fetch PC bank select\nthrough the asynchronous exception table, tag match, row mux and fill\ncollision check to read_ok_q. The predictor already has two issue\nintervals to deliver an instruction. Spending the first interval on two\nindependent RAM reads would move selection into the second interval.\n\nhyp-20261122-001-r13s0 The champion's placement-0 critical path starts at EX/MEM alu_result and ends at the clock enable of older.source in fetch. The same issue signal controls the instruction RAM read. Both structures generate hints, and IF already checks each returned word against the full fetch address. A probe can run before EX decides whether the fetched instruction may issue.\n\nhyp-20261122-002-r13s1 The champion spends 3,061,795 marked cycles at 89.419 MHz. In round 12,\ntwo replay designs preserved that cycle count, but Gowin traced 42- and\n43-cell paths through instruction selection, register read and branch\nprediction to the ID/EX alternate PC. The champion also has an EX result\npath to the fetch-history enable. Both paths cross the current fetch and\noperand-issue boundary in one clock.\n\nhyp-20261122-003-r13s2 Two round-12 designs preserved all 3,061,795 marked cycles, yet their\nlongest reported paths ran from instruction selection through the register\nfile and the equality predictor into the saved recovery PC. The replay\nengine already predicts a word two issues ahead. Repeated instruction\nsequences can supply the successor of that word on the same read.\n\nhyp-20261122-001-r14s0 On the champion's 89.419 MHz median Gowin placement, the fetch PC selects\nan exception-table entry, checks its tag, selects a replay row and tests\nthat row for a concurrent fill before read_ok_q can settle. Duplicating\nthe instruction RAM in round 12 preserved the 3,061,795 marked cycles\nbut reduced median Fmax. The address encoding can shorten this path while\nretaining one synchronous instruction RAM.\n\nhyp-20261122-002-r14s1 The champion retires 2,922,016 instructions in 3,061,795 marked cycles.\nA registered live-word refill added 165,592 cycles in simulation. A better\nplace to separate fetch from the wide operand path is at result writeback.\nIn Gowin placement 1 the champion takes 16.461 ns from imemReady through\nthe integer register file to the ID/EX alternate PC. Round 13 also found\nthat none of the 93,960 timed multiplies fed an adjacent branch or JALR.\n\nhyp-20261122-003-r14s2 Two round-12 timing reports traced fetch through the register file and the equality comparator to the saved alternate PC. Round 11 also exposed the path from an adjacent producer through EX branch comparison to the fetch enable. The champion takes 3,061,795 marked cycles. Its current one-cycle recovery makes prediction errors cheap, but puts both the prediction and its correction on paths into fetch.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
