{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-001-r3s0.yaml')\np.write_text(\\\"\\\"\\\"id: hyp-20261122-001-r3s0\ntitle: Move register-file reads into distributed RAM with a per-register reset bit\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  Both leading setup paths in the champion's 47.7 MHz median placement start\n  at a register-file block RAM. One goes through EX branch resolution into\n  the load-use interlock. The other goes through the multiplier into data\n  memory. Their reported data arrival times are 26.259 and 26.246 ns.\n  Shortening their common operand-read path can improve both at once.\nhypothesis: |\n  Map the 32 by 32 register file to asynchronous-read Gowin distributed RAM\n  so operand data can be captured in fabric flip-flops at the existing ID/EX\n  edge. Keep one synchronous write port and two combinational read ports.\n  Give the data array a single indexed write with no bulk clear. Track\n  initialized registers in a 32-bit bitmap that clears on a reset edge and\n  sets the addressed bit on an accepted nonzero write. For each read port,\n  give x0 first priority, a matching current write second priority, and\n  otherwise return the stored word if initialized or zero if unwritten.\n\n  An isolated prototype moved the register file out of two block RAMs.\n  Gowin reported 51.000, 50.396 and 50.396 MHz across the three placement\n  options. CoreMark validated all five CRCs in 4,491,485 bracketed cycles\n  for ten iterations. That gives 112.20 iterations/s, 5.65% above 106.20.\n  The margin above the acceptance threshold is about one percentage point,\n  so reproducing the RAM inference and the priority chain matters.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Mark the existing regs array with the Gowin syn_ramstyle attribute\n      set to \\\"distributed_ram\\\". In one always_ff block, clear the written\n      bitmap on reset or set written[w_addr] on w_en and w_addr \"'!= 0.\n      Write regs[w_addr] in a separate always_ff block enabled by !reset,\n      w_en and w_addr != 0. Retain each port'\"'s existing x0 and write-first\n      priority chain and qualify its final RAM read with written[rs_addr].\n  - file: test/test_reg_file.py\n    description: |\n      Write distinct nonzero values to multiple registers and reset again.\n      Read them on both ports, then rewrite just one and confirm that only\n      that address becomes visible. Include a write coincident with the\n      reset edge to check reset priority over the synchronous write.\n\\\"\\\"\\\")\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
