{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_63", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-001-r3s0.yaml')\np.write_text(\\\"\\\"\\\"id: hyp-20261122-001-r3s0\ntitle: Select EX forwarding sources one cycle earlier in decode\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion reaches 47.7 MHz and runs ten CoreMark iterations in\n  4,491,485 cycles. Its median Gowin placement shows two nearly equal\n  operand paths. One feeds a branch and the fetch interlock at 26.259 ns.\n  The other feeds the multiplier and the data-memory address at 26.246 ns.\n  Both pay for register-file write-through and EX forwarding before their\n  results can be used. Decode already has the consumer's source numbers\n  and the destinations of all three older potential writers.\nhypothesis: |\n  Compute two four-way, one-hot forwarding selections during decode, one\n  per source. Compare raw fetch bits 19:15 and 24:20 with the destinations\n  currently in EX, MEM and WB. Reject x0 and qualify each match with the\n  producer's effective write enable. Resolve overlapping matches youngest\n  first. At the ID/EX edge, register the selections and a single shared\n  copy of current WB data alongside the instruction's register-file reads.\n\n  In the consumer's EX cycle the four inputs, in priority order, are the\n  EX/MEM result, MEM/WB writeback data, the saved decode-cycle WB data and\n  the saved register-file operand. Mask each input with its registered\n  select bit and OR the four words. The third input covers writes colliding\n  with a register-file read in ID. This moves the write-through choice\n  and forwarding priority off both the branch and multiply operand paths.\n\n  An isolated prototype reached 53.428, 54.573 and 54.573 MHz in the three\n  Gowin placement options. CoreMark validated all five CRCs in 4,491,485\n  marker-to-marker cycles. The median gives 121.50 iterations/s, 14.4%\n  above 106.20. The forecast allows for placement changes in the final RTL.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 14\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Generate and register the four exclusive selects for each decode\n      source. The default selects the register-file word. Use the ID/EX\n      payload capture enable so that a held consumer keeps its choices.\n      A flushed bubble ignores its choices and the next accepted fetch\n      replaces them.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the raw fetch source fields, ID/EX and EX/MEM destinations,\n      their trap-qualified write intents and the current WB port to the\n      selector. On \"'!stall_id save the current WB data in a shared 32-bit\n      register. Hold this register and both selects through dmem or divide\n      stalls. Instantiate the register file in raw-read mode.\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a default-on write-first bypass parameter. With it disabled,\n      both reads return stored data with the existing x0 behavior. The\n      integrated core implements same-edge write-through via the saved WB\n      source. Preserve synchronous reset and standalone write-first tests.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export ctrl_with_trap.reg_write as the write intent of the instruction\n      about to enter EX/MEM, so a misaligned jump cannot win a match. Use\n      each four-bit mask to select the forwarded rs value for arithmetic,\n      branches, stores, divide issue and RVFI.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export ctrl_with_trap.reg_write for the instruction about to enter\n      MEM/WB. A trapping load must allow an older matching writer to win.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise three consecutive writes to one rd followed by a consumer\n      of both sources. Also cover a WB write colliding with decode, a\n      consumer held behind a stalled store, and a misaligned JAL or load\n      shadowing an older write. Check retired operands and results while\n      retaining the existing divide and load-use cycle checks.\n\"\"\")\nPY'", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
