ons/s, 14.4%\n  above 106.20. The forecast allows for placement changes in the final RTL.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 14\n  confidence: high\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/forward_unit.sv\n    description: |\n      Generate and register the four exclusive selects for each decode\n      source. The default selects the register-file word. Use the ID/EX\n      payload capture enable so that a held consumer keeps its choices.\n      A flushed bubble ignores its choices and the next accepted fetch\n      replaces them.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Wire the raw fetch source fields, ID/EX and EX/MEM destinations,\n      their trap-qualified write intents and the current WB port to the\n      selector. On \"'!stall_id save the current WB data in a shared 32-bit\n      register. Hold this register and both selects through dmem or divide\n      stalls. Instantiate the register file in raw-read mode.\n  - file: rtl/reg_file.sv\n    description: |\n      Add a default-on write-first bypass parameter. With it disabled,\n      both reads return stored data with the existing x0 behavior. The\n      integrated core implements same-edge write-through via the saved WB\n      source. Preserve synchronous reset and standalone write-first tests.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export ctrl_with_trap.reg_write as the write intent of the instruction\n      about to enter EX/MEM, so a misaligned jump cannot win a match. Use\n      each four-bit mask to select the forwarded rs value for arithmetic,\n      branches, stores, divide issue and RVFI.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Export ctrl_with_trap.reg_write for the instruction about to enter\n      MEM/WB. A trapping load must allow an older matching writer to win.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise three consecutive writes to one rd followed by a consumer\n      of both sources. Also cover a WB write colliding with decode, a\n      con