{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_105", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-001-r5s0.yaml\ntail -3 /srv/hwebench/tmp/19d02a47/d26b37b6/bench-mul.0I3X59/timingclean/gowin_p1/gw.log'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-001-r5s0\ntitle: Issue ready-base loads and stores one cycle early\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The champion waits for EX/MEM before presenting every data request. When\n  decode already has the correct base and the older instruction leaves the\n  data port idle, that costs a bus opportunity. An adjacent load consumer\n  also takes a bubble even when RAM could have returned the word in EX.\nhypothesis: |\n  Compute the effective address in decode from its bypassed rs1 and the\n  signed I/S displacement. A valid, aligned RAM load or store with a ready\n  base can use the idle data port in EX. On acceptance, capture the result\n  and mark the access complete. A dependent consumer can immediately use\n  the registered load result through the existing EX/MEM forwarding path.\n  An outstanding older access takes priority. A denied early request\n  continues in MEM at the same address with the same lanes and store data.\n\n  A scratch prototype completed ten iterations with both bus stalls and the\n  five canonical CRCs in 3,920,825 marked cycles, down from 4,164,193.\n  Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 61.259, 64.260 and 64.260 MHz.\n  The median gives 163.89 iterations/s, 5.41% above the 155.48 champion.\n  At this cycle count the acceptance threshold requires more than 63.765 MHz.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the signed 12-bit offset to the MEM/WB-bypassed rs1. Bits 11:5\n      of the offset come from instruction bits 31:25. Opcode bit 5 selects\n      instruction bits 11:7 for stores or 24:20 for loads. Capture the sum\n      and existing rs1 readiness on the operand-capture edge.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry the early address and base-ready flag in ID/EX. Carry an\n      early-completion bit, address and raw response word in EX/MEM for\n      access accounting and RVFI.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Use a single registered dmem address. When EX advances, select the\n…[1257 of 4329 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:4a8a6c5fd0893e48]…\nor an access already accepted in EX. For that\n      access use its saved address for masks and aligned RVFI address, its\n      saved raw response for RVFI read data, and its registered load result\n      for the decode bypass and writeback. Keep the regular MEM path for\n      busy-base, misaligned, MMIO and retried accesses.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check consecutive retirements for a ready-base load and its consumer,\n      with signed byte and halfword lanes. Deny the first early handshake\n      and check the stable retry, original load-use interlock and exactly\n      one accepted transfer. Exercise adjacent early store/load to one\n      address, a forwarded store value, contention with an older stalled\n      access, a pending base writer, a wrong-path store, MMIO and\n      misalignment. Check accepted bus transactions alongside RVFI data,\n      masks, trap and order.\n[60%] Tech-Mapping Phase 0 completed\n[65%] Tech-Mapping Phase 1 completed\n[75%] Tech-Mapping Phase 2 completed\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
