{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_28", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"mktemp -d && cat formal/checks_si.cfg && rg -n '(divide|div_|cycles|latency|bubble)' cores/bench/test/test_pipeline.py | tail -54 && sed -n '199,257p' tools/eval/fpga.py\"", "aggregated_output": "/srv/hwebench/tmp/19d02a47/4f8e379b/tmp.pdVYh2Eo7D\n[options]\nisa rv32im\nnret 1\nsolver bitwuzla\n\n[depth]\n# BMC depth per check. 2nd column (where present) is the k-induction prove\n# depth. Keep depths matched to the current pipeline so bugs within ~20\n# cycles of any symbolic instruction stream are exposed.\ninsn          20\nreg       10  20\npc_fwd    10  20\npc_bwd    10  20\ncausal    10  20\n# unique   — no two retirements share rvfi_order (catches double-retire)\n# cover    — sanity that the CPU retires (prevents vacuous-pass suites)\n# ill      — illegal instructions must trap; rvfi_trap from decoder.is_illegal\n# liveness — CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic stream\nunique     1 10 20\ncover      1    20\nill             20\nliveness   1 10 20\n\n[defines]\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Same as checks.cfg. See checks.cfg for\n# the full rationale on what ALTOPS does and does not prove. M-extension\n# arithmetic correctness is covered by test/test_alu.py and `make formal-deep`.\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\nalways @* if (!reset) cover (rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap);\n\n[verilog-files]\n# run_all.sh rebuilds this section from the actual rtl/ glob; the list\n# below is overwritten before genchecks runs. Kept as a placeholder so\n# the section is syntactically present for any reader inspecting the cfg.\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\n77:async def _run(dut, program, dmem_init=None, max_cycles=200, dmem_ready=None,\n79:    \"\"\"Drive imem/dmem; capture RVFI retirements until EBREAK or max_cycles.\n119:    for cycle in range(max_cycles):\n171:        raise AssertionError(f\"max_cycles={max_cycles} reached without EBREAK\")\n262:    assert load[\"cycle\"] == base[\"cycle\"] + 4  # three wait cycles\n263:    assert add[\"cycle\"] == load[\"cycle\"] + 2   # one load-use bubble\n340:async def dependent_divides_with_stalled_memory(dut):\n372:    # before the divider finishes, must reach RVFI with the r\n…[2446 of 5518 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b98d3c7f01c543fb]…\nerations': 0,\n                'reason': 'maxcycles_hit_before_ebreak'}\n\n    # OOB sticky flag from the sim: if the CPU addressed outside 1 MiB at any\n    # point during this run, the cycle count we're about to divide by is tainted\n    # by silently-wrapped memory accesses. Treat as a benchmark failure — the\n    # same rule cosim applies, so a CPU that produces a correct CRC via aliased\n    # memory doesn't earn a fitness score.\n    if marker.get('oob', False):\n        return {'completed': False, 'iter_per_cycle': 0.0, 'cycles': 0, 'iterations': 0,\n                'reason': 'oob_memory_access'}\n\n    uart = marker.get('uart', '')\n    iterations = parse_iterations(worktree)\n    valid, reason = validate_coremark_uart(uart, iterations)\n    if not valid:\n        return {'completed': False, 'iter_per_cycle': 0.0, 'cycles': 0, 'iterations': 0,\n                'reason': reason}\n\n    # Bracketed cycles (start_time..stop_time) are the contract per\n    # bench/programs/coremark/baremetal/core_portme.c's start/stop_time.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
