{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_37", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrows=[json.loads(l) for l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')]\nfor r in rows[4:6]: print(r['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "{'motivation': 'The 31.991 MHz champion has a 36.909 ns Gowin path from remainder_q to\\nquotient_q through eight serial trial subtractions and remainder selections.\\nTen CoreMark iterations took 4,491,485 marker-bracketed cycles, and the\\nmeasured loop executes no DIV or REM. This makes divider latency inexpensive\\nwhile divider feedback delay sets the clock period for every instruction.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Compute five binary restoring steps per RUN clock and register the result\\nafter each group. Seven identical rounds cover a 35-bit dividend formed\\nby zero-extending the 32-bit magnitude. The three leading zero bits allow\\na fixed datapath with no partial-round mux on the feedback path.\\n\\nIssue, seven rounds and transfer take nine EX cycles. A load feeding a\\ndivide adds one load-use bubble, giving a ten-cycle retirement gap with\\nready buses. The prototype passed all 53 fast formal checks, including\\nliveness, all five cocotb suites, and cosimulation on all five test ELFs.\\n\\nOn the Tang Nano 20K, the prototype reached 44.763, 45.009 and 45.009 MHz\\nacross Gowin's three placement options. The worst path moved from the\\ndivider to the register-file read and ID/EX capture path. CoreMark with\\nboth bus stalls passed all five CRC checks and took 4,491,485 cycles\\nbetween markers for ten iterations. The 45.009 MHz median gives 100.21\\niterations/s, 40.7% above the 71.23 champion.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 41, 'confidence': 'high'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': 'Widen the combined dividend/quotient shift register to 35 bits. At issue\\nload three zero bits followed by the unsigned magnitude. Each step takes\\nthe next bit from bit 34, shifts in its quotient decision at bit 0 and\\nupdates the 32-bit remainder using the existing trial subtraction.\\nUse five steps per round, count rounds 0 through 6, and take the final\\nquotient from bits 31:0. Retain the existing zero-divisor and signed\\noverflow behavior, sign correction and held-result handshake. Share the\\nseven-round controll\n…[3056 of 6128 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:78e72dde200b9786]…\ne. Use widened unsigned differences for borrow detection. Preserve signed truncation, dividend-signed remainder, divide-by-zero and INT_MIN / -1 results. Keep DONE stable until consume and reset able to cancel any phase. Drive the captured-operand ALTOPS formulas with the same controller.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_alu.py', 'description': 'Check the preparation cycle and six RUN cycles explicitly in the handshake and reset tests, retaining all arithmetic vectors and held-result assertions. Add unsigned cases at q*D-1, q*D and q*D+1 for q in 1, 2, 3 where representable, plus a small dividend against D=0x55555556 to catch accidental truncation of 3D.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Update the exact retirement-gap expectations in dependent_divides_with_stalled_memory to ten cycles for the load-dependent divide and nine for each immediately following divide or remainder. Retain the one-cycle result-consumer gaps, single memory transfers, ordered retirement and forwarded operand checks.\\n'}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
