{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_5", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/LESSONS.md'", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r1s0 (regression, +32.67%): Parallel radix-4 trials raised median Gowin Fmax from 5.44 to 7.22 MHz, but chaining 16 remainder selections in one EX cycle kept it below the multicycle divider’s 32 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 (improvement, +487.71%): Limiting the restoring divider to eight quotient steps per clock shortened the EX critical path, and its six-cycle latency cost no timed CoreMark cycles because `iterate` and its callees execute no DIV or REM instructions.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r1s2 (regression, +6.52%): The 128-entry bimodal predictor and fetch-time JAL targets cut CoreMark cycles by about 6.4% by avoiding one IF/ID flush per correctly predicted taken transfer, while the single-cycle EX divider still limited Fmax to about 5.4 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r2s0 (regression, +40.69%): Padding the 32-bit dividend magnitude to 35 bits let all seven rounds use five restoring steps without a partial-round mux, shifting the Gowin critical path from divider feedback to the register-file read feeding ID/EX at a median 45.009 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r2s1 (improvement, +49.09%): Registering 3× the divisor and testing all three multiples in parallel cut divider feedback from eight serial restoring steps per clock to three radix-4 decisions, raising Fmax at no cycle cost in CoreMark’s divide-free timed loop.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r2s2 (regression, +7.27%): With eight serial divider steps still limiting Fmax to about 32 MHz, fetch-time branch and JAL prediction needed to cut CoreMark cycles by about 33% to match the 47.7 MHz radix-4 core, well above the 6.4% saved by avoiding IF/ID flushes.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r3s0 (regression, +20.45%): Registering youngest-first one-hot forwarding selects and WB data at ID/EX moved forwarding priority off both EX operand paths at no CoreMark cycle cost, and mapping the register file to Gowin distributed RAM raised median Fmax a further 6.0 MHz.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r3s1 (improvement, +31.3\n…[4030 of 7102 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b8ad1916507ffe2d]…\n[31:15]` read early, EX divider feedback limited median Fmax to 64.834 MHz below the 66.8 MHz replacement threshold.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-001-r8s0 (regression, +6.00%): Forwarding the ordinary EX/MEM result directly removed product selection from both EX operand paths and raised median Fmax from 71.730 to 76.884 MHz, outweighing the 1.11% CoreMark cycle cost of stalling adjacent multiply consumers.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-002-r8s1 (improvement, +9.24%): Qualifying each source’s ID/EX forwarding match by result bank let EX select the product half in parallel with the ordinary bypass mux, shortening the path into the address adder without stalling adjacent multiply consumers.\n- 2026-11-22 hyp-20261122-003-r8s2 (regression, +7.48%): Issuing an adjacent consumer on a valid, eligible load-table entry removed the full address check from the fetch stall path, while patching its ID/EX operands from MEM on a false hit cost the same one bubble as the original interlock and preserved CoreMark’s 3,132,302 cycles.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
