{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_133","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-001-r9s0.yaml; date -u'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-001-r9s0\ntitle: Start instruction replay two issues ahead to register the fetch word\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  On the champion's median Gowin placement, the slowest path launches\n  from the successor block RAM, passes through the register file and ends\n  at the load-table collision check. The same RAM also feeds the fetch\n  branch decision. Starting its read one issue earlier gives both paths a\n  flip-flop output to start from. The tradeoff is that a PC two issues\n  back can identify the desired instruction less often at a branch.\nhypothesis: |\n  Retime the existing 512-row instruction replay RAM. For three successive\n  issued instructions A, B and C, read row A when A issues. On issue of B,\n  transfer that response into a fabric register for use at C. Train row A\n  with C's word PC and instruction when C\n  issues from a ready bus. A replay still requires a valid entry and an\n  exact match to the current PC inside the RAM window. Live bus data\n  takes priority.\n\n  Read the block RAM every clock. If issue pauses between A and B, save\n  A's response on the first intervening edge and hold it until B issues.\n  Queue each training write for the following clock. These two port\n  changes remove the late issue decision from both RAM enables.\n\n  A scratch prototype completed ten iterations with randomized instruction\n  and data stalls in 3,148,248 marked cycles versus 3,132,302 for the champion. All five\n  CRCs and the validation banner matched. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2\n  reached 78.619, 82.612 and 82.612 MHz. The median yields 262.41\n  iterations/s, 4.90 percent above 250.15 and just above the 261.66\n  cutoff. Placement 0 used 4,056 logic cells, 917 registers, nine block\n  RAMs and two DSPs. The pipeline cocotb suite passed. Selftest and all\n  three random programs matched the reference RVFI traces on both ready\n  and stalled buses.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 5\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep the 512 by 51 RAM and shift PC[10:2] through two predecessor\n      registers only on issue. Enqueue a write when both predecessors\n      exist, the scrub has finished, and a live in-window word issues.\n      Register the older index, current PC[19:2] and live instruction.\n      Give the scrubber priority over the queued write. Cancel the queue\n      and invalidate both history and replay stages on reset or FENCE.I.\n\n      Read at the current PC each clock. Pipeline a lookup-valid bit on\n      issue that rejects a physical read/write collision with the queued\n      write. Register issue itself. When the previous cycle issued, save\n      the RAM output in a holding register. On the current issue edge,\n      capture the RAM output directly if the previous cycle issued, or\n      the held response after a gap. Carry lookup validity alongside\n      this data. On redirect clear final replay validity. Retain issued\n      history for learning the resolved path and the existing FENCE.I\n      drain for an older code store.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise two paths that approach a shared zero-offset JALR from\n      different PCs and jump to different targets. Train both paths on a\n      ready bus, then withhold imemReady at each target on its second\n      visit. Check that both target words replay on the first requested\n      cycle and retire once, with the exact PC sequence and register\n      results. This distinguishes the two-issue lookup from a table\n      indexed by the shared JALR alone.\nSun Nov 22 07:55:12 UTC 2026\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
