{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_114","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r11s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-002-r11s1\ntitle: Give ready and forwarded load bases separate address-verification paths\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 3,131,081 marked cycles at 85.513 MHz. Its load-use decision\n  puts EX operand forwarding, a 32-bit address addition and a tag comparison in\n  series before fetch can advance. Round 10 shortened that path by verifying\n  ready bases in decode, but lost 22,252 cycles when pending bases and page\n  crossings went to memory. A pending base from an ordinary producer is already\n  available directly at the EX/MEM result register.\nhypothesis: |\n  Split load verification at ID/EX according to where the base comes from. For\n  a ready base, use the existing ID row sum and register its low byte offset,\n  page carry, base page and displacement sign. In EX compare the candidate tag\n  against that page when the carry equals the sign. The synchronous RAM read\n  already establishes the row. Send a crossing on this path through MEM.\n\n  For a base awaiting an ordinary adjacent producer, keep the stride-directed\n  RAM read. Give its verifier a direct tap on the registered ordinary result.\n  Compare base plus signed displacement with the candidate word using parallel\n  short groups. For each group above the byte offset the two operands and the\n  expected low sum bit imply its carry-in by XOR. Check each local sum and\n  carry-out against its expected bits and the next group's implied carry-in.\n  Derive the first carry and byte lane from the two low address bits, include\n  all upper address bits in the check, and discard carry out of bit 31. This\n  accepts exact word matches even across a page boundary. An adjacent multiply\n  base takes the normal MEM path. Keep the full forwarded address adder for\n  the bus and RVFI in parallel with both verifiers.\n\n  Register which base path applies on operand capture. A legal aligned match\n  from either path can forward the correctly extended data and release the\n  adjacent consumer. Both paths share the existing RAM-valid, scrub and store\n  collision checks. At the champion's cycle count, a median above 89.45 MHz\n  clears the acceptance margin. The main risk is moving the longest path to\n  the decode row sum or to the final reduction of the parallel checks.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/load_predictor.sv\n    description: Capture the ready-base row sum, offset, page and carry information beside the RAM response. Keep stride training and row selection for pending bases, and retain both collision vetoes.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Supply the write-first base and route its captured address metadata to EX. Align the verification path selection with the existing operand capture enable.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Use the ready-base page check and an independent grouped word comparison from reg_q.alu_result for an ordinary forwarded base. Select the corresponding byte lane for alignment and extension. Carry the complete architectural address to MEM for every access.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Exercise a stride hit with an adjacent ALU or cached LW base, positive and negative page crossings, a multiply-produced base, an out-of-window alias, and a same-row older store under backpressure. Check bus requests, consumer operands and RVFI order.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
