{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor d in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if d['id'].endswith(('r10s1','r9s1','r9s0','r11s1','r8s2','r10s0','r9s2')):\n  print(d['id'],d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n  print(d['critical_path'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r8s2 Use a valid, eligible load-table candidate to predict that the adjacent\nconsumer can issue. Keep the existing interlock for a definite miss.\nOn the issue edge, register one bit recording whether either source\nmatches the preceding nonzero destination. In parallel, EX verifies\nthe load's full forwarded word address and alignment. Only an exact\nhit can supply data or suppress the external read.\n\nIf the prediction was wrong, hold the consumer in ID/EX while MEM\ncompletes the older load. On completion, patch each matching source\nwith the extended load result and insert one EX/MEM bubble. A trapping\nload preserves the captured pre-load operands. The consumer then\nexecutes once at its original PC. This costs one bubble on a dependent\nmiss and preserves back-to-back execution on a hit.\n\nA scratch implementation made 189,167 such predictions in ten marked\nCoreMark iterations. It repaired all 31,113 false hits in place and\nfinished in the champion's 3,132,302 cycles with the canonical CRCs.\nGowin placement 1 reached 77.694 MHz, equivalent to 248.04 iterations/s\nat that cycle count. The selftest and three random RVFI comparisons\npassed on both zero-wait and stalled buses.\n\n{'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_26_s0', 'slack_ns': -8.008, 'arrival_ns': 18.302, 'logic_cells': 37, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\nhyp-20261122-001-r9s0 Retime the existing 512-row instruction replay RAM. For three successive\nissued instructions A, B and C, read row A when A issues. On issue of B,\ntransfer that response into a fabric register for use at C. Train row A\nwith C's word PC and instruction when C issues from a ready bus. Replay\nrequires a valid entry and an exact match to the current PC inside the\nRAM window. Live bus data takes priority.\n\nRead the block RAM every clock. If issue pauses between A and B, save\nA's response on the first intervening edge and hold it until B issues.\nQueue each training write for the following clock. These two po\n…[9036 of 12108 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:c9ea3c2282e601c7]…\nent\nsign, so the access stayed on that page. For a pending ADD, ADDI or LW base\nand a displacement of 0 through 3, compare the ordinary EX/MEM result bits\n31:2 directly to the pending port's zero-extended page and registered row.\nCheck the two-bit lane addition for word crossing and the access for alignment.\nThese tests let the load-use interlock use the two parallel verdicts while\nthe full forwarded effective address is computed independently for MEM and RVFI.\nA failed check takes the ordinary MEM access and dependency bubble.\n\nA scratch implementation completed ten iterations in 3,061,795 marked cycles\nwith all five canonical CRCs. Gowin placements reached 79.600, 86.496 and\n86.496 MHz, giving 282.50 iterations/s, about 3.4 percent above the champion.\nReplacement at this cycle count requires more than 87.47 MHz.\n\n{'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/older.source_4_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.93, 'arrival_ns': 17.225, 'logic_cells': 11, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
