s, cpu/u_if/accepted_q_s0 -> cpu/u_id/reg_q.alternate_pc_10_s0\n          through: clock_ibuf > cpu/u_if > cpu/u_rf > cpu/u_if\n  full report: /srv/hwebench/tmp/19d02a47/059bd0d9/benchstruct.Ki2472/cores/bench/generated/timing/gowin_p1/impl/pnr/core_bench.tr\nplace_option 2: Fmax 79.742 MHz, 14 logic levels; logic 4116\n…[1217 of 4289 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:e39bb8da1153ee53]…\nor: 'make -C cores/bench/obj_dir -f Vcore.mk -j 1' exited with 2\n        ... See the manual at https://verilator.org/verilator_doc.html?v=5.047 for more assistance.\n%Error: Command Failed ulimit -s unlimited 2>/dev/null; exec /opt/hwe-toolchain/oss-cad-suite/bin/verilator_bin --cc --exe --build -Mdir cores/bench/obj_dir +incdir+cores/bench/rtl --top-module core -Wall -Wno-fatal -Wno-style -CFLAGS -DNRET=2 cores/bench/rtl/core_pkg.sv cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv cores/bench/rtl/core.sv cores/bench/rtl/decoder.sv cores/bench/rtl/ex_stage.sv cores/bench/rtl/hazard_unit.sv cores/bench/rtl/id_stage.sv cores/bench/rtl/if_stage.sv cores/bench/rtl/imm_gen.sv cores/bench/rtl/load_predictor.sv cores/bench/rtl/mem_stage.sv cores/bench/rtl/radix4_path.sv cores/bench/rtl/reg_file.sv /srv/hwebench/tmp/19d02a47/059bd0d9/benchstruct.Ki2472/test/cosim/main.cpp -o cosim_sim\nmake: *** [Makefile:116: cosim-build] Error 2\nls: cannot access '/srv/hwebench/tmp/19d02a47/059bd0d9/benchstruct.Ki2472/v1_coremark*': No such file or directory\n", "exit_code": 2, "status": "failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'pc_|control|issue|ahead|stage' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys\nfor l in sys.stdin:\n d=json.loads(l.split(\\\":\\\",1)[1])\n if d[\\\"id\\\"].endswith((\\\"r7s2\\\",\\\"r11s0\\\")): print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'pc_|control|issue|ahead|stage' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys\nfor l in sys.stdin:\n d=json.loads(l.split(\\\":\\\",1)[1])\n if d[\\\"id\\\"].endswith((\\\"r7s2\\\",\\\"r11s0\\\")): print(d[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"Predict the next PC from a 128-entry tagged branch target buffer and the\nexisting two-bit direction counters. Use that address both to advance\nfetch and to launch a synchronous read of a 1,024-word instruction RAM.\nA single fill at a destination PC then serves sequential, taken and\nrecovery paths to that instruction. The predicted address is available\nwithout waiting for decode to read branch operands.\n\nSplit the instruction field instr[31:15] from the block RAM row. It\ncontains both RF addresses and the I-immediate used to predict a load\nrow. Give these 17 bits a distributed RAM with an asynchronous read at\nthe predicted next index and a capture flop on the same edge as the\nblock RAM read. Reassemble the saved instruction in decode. This makes\nthe saved operand fields available at flop clock-to-Q and keeps the\ndata-RAM address calculation out of the instruction block RAM output path.\n\nA prototype with the entire instruction in block RAM completed ten\niterations in 3,104,776 marked\ncycles with both bus stalls, the five expected CRCs and the validation\nbanner. Gowin measured 62.193, 65.910 and 65.910 MHz, yielding 212.29\niterations/s. That is 3.25% above 205.60. At that cycle count, the\n4.6% replacement margin requires more than 66.77 MHz. Its median\nplacement had a path from the instruction block RAM through the\nload predictor to lookup_ok_q. The proposed split targets that path.\nThe split layout has not been timed. A 67.5 MHz median at the same\ncycle count would yield 217.4 iterations/s.\n\nGive the load RAM a ten-bit bypass from the preceding producer. For\nADD or ADDI, compute the low twelve result bits from write-first\noperands in ID and register bits 11 through 2 when the used sources\nare ready. For an LW in EX, tap bits 11 through 2 of its raw candidate\nword. When the younger load needs that producer and its displacement\nis 0 through 3, use the bypass as the synchronous RAM read index.\nThis replaces the per-PC stride history with wires and an ID register.\n\nVerify the word using two short comparisons in EX. For a ready base,\nID already selected the exact row with its low twelve-bit sum. Capture\nwhether carry out equals displacement sign, then compare the saved\nbase's page against the RAM page tag and check the RAM window. For a\ndependent base, compare the ordinary EX/MEM result directly against\nthe candidate's page and the captured producer index. Require the low two\nbits plus displacement to stay in that word. The producer index reaches\nthe RAM address port before the LW hit verdict. If that LW misses, the\nexisting interlock holds its dependent load until the pointer arrives\nfrom MEM. Only a verified word can forward or suppress a bus request.\nPage crossings, unsupported producers and other misses use the existing\ninterlock and the full architectural address.\n\nA scratch implementation completed ten marked iterations in 3,061,795\ncycles with all five canonical CRCs, saving 69,286 cycles. Gowin\nplacement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 84.303, 88.308 and 88.308 MHz.\nThe median gives 288.42 iterations/s, 5.61% above 273.11. Replacement\nrequires more than 285.673 iterations/s. Selftest and all three\nrandom RVFI traces matched the reference on ready and stalled buses.\nAll five cocotb suites passed with the three test updates below.\nThe clock margin is narrow. A version using the muxed RAM read-row\nregister for the pending-base comparison reached 79.818 MHz on\nplacement option 1. Measure the placement median on the final RTL.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_32", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'd=\"$TMPDIR\"/benchstruct.Ki2472\ncd \"$d\"\ncat > refine.py <<'\"'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport shutil\nrtl=Path('cores/bench/rtl')\nsrc=Path('base_rtl')\nfor n in [2,3,4,5]:\n dest=Path('v'+str(n))\n shutil.copytree(src,dest)\n if n in (2,3):\n  s=(rtl/'if_stage.sv').read_text()\n  a=s.index('    candidate <= next_candidate;')\n  b=s.index('  assign fence_issue',a)\n  s=s[:a]+'''    if (issue) begin\n      candidate <= next_candidate;\n      candidate_ok_q <= next_candidate_ok;\n    end\n  end\n  logic [1:0] replay_valid;\n  assign hit = replay_valid[1] && candidate_ok_q && candidate.valid && in_window && candidate.pc == pc[19:2];\n  assign instr = imem_ready ? imem_data : candidate.instr;\n'''+s[b:]\n else:\n  s=(src/'if_stage.sv').read_text()\n if n==3:\n  # Single candidate issue enable is from local availability and oldest memory stop only.\n  # On a missed dependence or redirect skip the overadvanced candidate and wait for bus.\n  # For loops with arbitrary imem holds prefer the original exact history behavior.\n  s=s.replace('    if (issue) begin\\\\n      candidate <=', '    if (out.valid && \"'!stall_id) begin'\"\\\\n      candidate <=')\n  s=s.replace('assign hit = replay_valid[1]', 'assign hit = replay_valid[1]')\n if n==4:\n  # Probe a 3-issue destination. Register a trained row or sequential row at A\n  # to drive the RAM read at B, then register the returned word at C.\n  s=s.replace('history_t recent, older;', 'history_t recent, middle, older;')\n  s=s.replace('logic [1:0] history_valid;', 'logic [2:0] history_valid;')\n  s=s.replace(\\\"10'd2\\\",\\\"10'd3\\\").replace('history_valid[1] &&','history_valid[2] &&').replace(\\\"{history_valid[0], 1'b1}\\\",\\\"{history_valid[1:0], 1'b1}\\\").replace('older <= recent;', 'middle <= recent;\\\\n      older <= middle;')\n  s=s.replace('  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\\\\n    if (\"'!scrub[10] || fill_q)'\"', '''  logic [9:0] launch_row;\n  always_ff @(posedge clock) begin\n    if (issue) launch_row <= read_row;\n    if (\"'!scrub[10] || fill_q)'\"''')\n  s=s.replace('words[read_row]', 'words[launch_row]').replace('fill_q && read_row ==', 'fill_q && launch_row ==')\n if n==5:\n  # Replicate into a sequential and a nonsequential bank. For an exception\n  # fetch the hint before knowing if it matches, select at RAM->fabric edge.\n  s=s.replace('exception_t exception_entry;', '''(* syn_ramstyle = \\\"block_ram\\\" *) logic [50:0] seq_words [0:1023];\n  fetch_word_t seq_response;\n  logic match_q;\n  logic [9:0] seq_row_q, hint_row_q, rd_write_q;\n  logic read_fill_q, read_enabled_q;\n  exception_t exception_entry;''')\n  s=s.replace('    if (\"'!scrub[10] || fill_q)'\"\\\\n      words[write_row] <=', '    if (\"'!scrub[10] || fill_q)'\"\\\\n      seq_words[write_row] <= {scrub[10], fill_pc_q, fill_instr_q};\\\\n    if (\"'!scrub[10] || fill_q)'\"\\\\n      words[write_row] <=')\n  s=s.replace('if (issue)\\\\n      ram_word <= words[read_row];','''if (issue) begin\n      ram_word <= words[exception_entry.row];\n      seq_response <= seq_words[sequential_row];\n      match_q <= matched;\n      seq_row_q <= sequential_row;\n      hint_row_q <= exception_entry.row;\n      rd_write_q <= fill_pc_q[11:2];\n      read_fill_q <= fill_q;\n      read_enabled_q <= scrub[10];\n    end''')\n  s=s.replace('candidate <= ram_word;', 'candidate <= match_q ? ram_word : seq_response;')\n  s=s.replace('candidate_ok_q <= read_ok_q;', 'candidate_ok_q <= history_valid[0] && read_enabled_q && \"'!(read_fill_q && (match_q ? hint_row_q : seq_row_q) == rd_write_q);'\"')\n (dest/'if_stage.sv').write_text(s)\nPY\npython refine.py\ncat > measure.py <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport sys\nfrom tools.eval.gowin import build\na=sys.argv[1]\nopt=int(sys.argv[2]) if len(sys.argv)>2 else 1\nr=build('.',a,'fpga/core_bench_si.sv',opt,Path('builds') / (a+'p'+str(opt)))\nprint(a,opt,r)\nPY\ncat > simbench.sh <<'SH'\nset -eu\nRTL_DIR=\\\"\"'$1\" NRET=1 OBJ_DIR=\"obj_$1\" bash test/cosim/build.sh > \"$1-build.log\" 2>&1\n\"obj_$1/cosim_sim\" bench/programs/coremark.elf 20000000 --bench --istall --dstall > \"$1-iter.log\"\nSH\nls -d v?'", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
