eir addresses directly\nfrom the live bus. When imemReady is high, fetch can use those values\nfor its existing ready-operand BEQ/BNE and zero-offset JALR predictions.\nWhen issuing a replayed word, fetch uses the existing branch counter\nfor a conditional branch and lets EX resolve JALR. Architectural ID\ncontinues to read the selected instruction through its write-first ports.\n\nUse the same live rs1 port to launch a data-table probe without an ID\naddress adder. For a ready live base and a byte displacement from 0\nthrough 3, read the base word row directly. For every other case,\nincluding replay, read th\n…[7969 of 11041 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6b37e63b9aaf009c]…\nEX until the product reaches the\nwrite-first register-file ports. This removes the DSP result from the\nrecovery path and lets fetch use the spare queue entry during the hold.\nCorrect a stale prediction even\nwhen the live instruction at a tagged PC has become a non-branch.\nOn correction, discard both younger queue entries and the in-flight\nfetch, and probe the actual successor. Train only once per advancing\nresolved control transfer. Invalidate pending fetches and cached code\non FENCE.I and resume after older stores complete. Suppress uninitialized\nand read/write-colliding RAM responses, including across stalls and reset.\n\nA scratch queue with shared EX forwarding validated all five CRCs in\n3,070,974 marked cycles. Its Gowin median was 85.846 MHz. The limiting\npath on placements 1 and 2 ran from the registered product to the\nrecovery-PC enable. At that cycle count, replacing the champion needs\nmore than 93.81 MHz. Measure both the median and the cycle cost of\ninterlocking product-dependent control transfers.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
