{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat formal/checks_si.cfg | head -60'","aggregated_output":"[options]\nisa rv32im\nnret 1\nsolver bitwuzla\n\n[depth]\n# BMC depth per check. 2nd column (where present) is the k-induction prove\n# depth. Keep depths matched to the current pipeline so bugs within ~20\n# cycles of any symbolic instruction stream are exposed.\ninsn          20\nreg       10  20\npc_fwd    10  20\npc_bwd    10  20\ncausal    10  20\n# unique   — no two retirements share rvfi_order (catches double-retire)\n# cover    — sanity that the CPU retires (prevents vacuous-pass suites)\n# ill      — illegal instructions must trap; rvfi_trap from decoder.is_illegal\n# liveness — CPU makes forward progress under any symbolic stream\nunique     1 10 20\ncover      1    20\nill             20\nliveness   1 10 20\n\n[defines]\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALIGNED_MEM\n# RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS — fast mode. Same as checks.cfg. See checks.cfg for\n# the full rationale on what ALTOPS does and does not prove. M-extension\n# arithmetic correctness is covered by test/test_alu.py and `make formal-deep`.\n`define RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS\n\n[cover]\n# Sanity cover: the CPU must retire at least one non-trapping instruction.\nalways @* if (!reset) cover (rvfi_valid && !rvfi_trap);\n\n[verilog-files]\n# run_all.sh rebuilds this section from the actual rtl/ glob; the list\n# below is overwritten before genchecks runs. Kept as a placeholder so\n# the section is syntactically present for any reader inspecting the cfg.\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/core.sv\n@basedir@/cores/@core@/wrapper.sv\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r8s0','r7s1','r2s1')): print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"].get(\\\"hypothesis\\\"))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_49", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r8s0','r7s1','r2s1')): print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"].get(\\\"hypothesis\\\"))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "Rebuild the blocking divider as a clocked radix-4 engine. On issue, capture unsigned operand magnitudes and the existing sign and operation flags. In one preparation cycle, register three times the divisor. Use the registered divisor and its left shift for the other two multiples. Each RUN cycle produces six quotient bits through three successive radix-4 digit decisions. For each incoming bit pair, form T = 4R + pair and compute T-D, T-2D and T-3D in parallel. Select the highest digit with a nonnegative difference, using its difference as the new remainder. Digit zero passes T through.\n\nInitialize a 36-bit quotient shift register with four leading zeros and the 32-bit dividend magnitude. Six identical RUN cycles consume all 36 bits and leave the quotient in the low 32 bits. Unsigned trials and multiples need 34 bits of magnitude and an explicit borrow bit on subtraction. Apply the existing quotient/remainder selection and sign correction at completion. Use the same issue, preparation, six RUN cycles and result transfer for real arithmetic and ALTOPS. This gives nine EX cycles per divide and a ten-cycle retirement gap after a dependent load on ready buses, within the configured formal liveness window.\n\nI predict about 40 MHz median Gowin Fmax and roughly the champion's marker-to-marker cycle count, or about 89 CoreMark iterations/s. Measure both independently. The chief uncertainty is whether routing the three parallel trials per digit or a path outside the divider limits Fmax first.\n\nMove result selection across the EX/MEM register. Capture the full product beside\nthe ordinary EX/MEM result. In MEM, use registered enables to select the high\nproduct, the low product or the ordinary result. Forward that value to the adjacent\ninstruction and write the register file when MEM completes. Decode sees that write\nthrough the file's write-first port. Fetch takes the array output before the\nbypass and uses its existing operand-based predictions only when all required\nvalues are already in the array.\n\nFit division to the sam\n…[1502 of 4574 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:90364ab8417bfb00]…\nlies can still issue on consecutive cycles.\nThis trades occasional dependency bubbles for a shorter path on every\nordinarily forwarded operand, including the load base and branch operands.\nA multiply that consumes an adjacent ordinary ALU result still gets that\noperand on time through the direct bypass.\n\nA scratch implementation completed ten iterations in 3,167,223 marked cycles,\nup 1.11% from 3,132,302. With both bus stalls enabled it produced all five\ncanonical CRCs and the validation message. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and\n2 reached 73.720, 76.884 and 76.884 MHz. The median gives 242.75 iterations\nper second, 6.00% above the champion and above the 239.53 acceptance cutoff.\nPlacement 0 used 4,001 logic cells, 739 registers and two DSPs. The scratch\ncore passed all five cocotb suites with the revised timing expectations\nand matched the reference on selftest and all three random traces with\nready and stalled buses. All four ALTOPS multiply checks, single-channel\nretirement uniqueness and liveness passed.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
