{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_91", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r14s1.yaml')\np.write_text('''id: hyp-20261122-002-r14s1\ntitle: Give control transfers a dedicated operand path from fetch through execute\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion retires 2,922,016 instructions in 3,061,795 marked cycles.\n  A registered live-word refill added 165,592 cycles in simulation. A better\n  place to separate fetch from the wide operand path is at result writeback.\n  In Gowin placement 1 the champion takes 16.461 ns from imemReady through\n  the integer register file to the ID/EX alternate PC. Round 13 also found\n  that none of the 93,960 timed multiplies fed an adjacent branch or JALR.\nhypothesis: |\n  Give fetch a narrow register file written from the registered WB result.\n  Each nonzero register's entry records whether its value is zero and whether\n  it gives a legal in-window zero-offset JALR target, together with target\n  bits 19:2. Bit 0 is ignored and bit 1 must be zero. Initialize the flags\n  for unwritten registers and hardwire x0. Look up the live and replay source\n  indices in parallel, then select the narrow verdict with imemReady. This\n  takes the instruction-source mux off the register-address inputs without\n  replicating 32-bit architectural operand ports.\n  \n  Predict BEQ/BNE exactly when the source numbers match or a ready zero flag\n  establishes the comparison. Otherwise use the existing counter. Predict\n  a zero-offset JALR only when its target entry is valid and current. For\n  each needed register, veto stale WB metadata on a matching writer in EX,\n  MEM or WB. Capture fallthrough, direct target and the direction bit\n  separately so the late comparison does not select a wide recovery PC in ID.\n  \n  In EX, give the branch comparator and JALR adder their own operand muxes\n  between captured ID values and the ordinary EX/MEM result. Interlock a\n  branch using either source of an adjacent multiply, or a JALR using its\n  rs1, until ID can capture the product from the write-first port. Keep the\n  producer advancing and apply the usual memory backpressure and redirect\n  priority. An interlocked transfer resolves, trains and retires exactly once.\n  \n  A scratch version with one selected metadata read address per source\n  validated all five CoreMark CRCs in 3,067,156 marked cycles. Placement 1\n  reached 88.370 MHz with the common EX operand muxes. At this cycle count\n  acceptance needs a median above 93.6968 MHz. The parallel index lookups\n  and ordinary-only resolution bypass target the remaining control paths.\n  The MEM-to-load eligibility path could still limit Fmax.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Maintain WB zero and JALR metadata, look up the live and replay source indices separately, and identify true adjacent multiply dependencies of control transfers.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: Select narrow direction and target predictions from the appropriate instruction source and pass both possible direct successors into ID.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: Resolve branches and JALR with ordinary-only operand forwarding and select recovery from the captured direction and complete successor addresses.\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Hold a product-dependent control transfer for one advancing cycle, with older dmem and divide holds taking precedence.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: Check both instruction sources, WB metadata freshness, x0, JALR bit-one faults and MUL-high dependencies on each branch operand under data-bus stalls.\n'''.replace('\\\\n  \\\\n','\\\\n\\\\n'))\nPY\npython - <<'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r14s1.yaml'); r=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()); jsonschema.validate(r,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text())); assert p.stem==r['id'] and r['category']=='structural';print('schema valid')\nPY\ngit status --short --untracked-files=all\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
