{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r.get('round_id') in (12,13,14):\n  print(r['id'],r['title'],r.get('critical_path'),r['hypothesis']['motivation'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][-1400:])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-001-r12s0 Select the instruction replay row after the block RAM read {'from': 'stall_gen/imem_ready_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.alternate_pc_15_s0', 'slack_ns': -7.345, 'arrival_ns': 17.64, 'logic_cells': 43, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_rf', 'cpu/u_if']} On the champion's 89.419 MHz median placement the longest reported setup\npath starts at the fetch PC selector and ends at read_ok_q. It crosses\nthe asynchronous exception RAM, its tag check, the replay read-address\nmux and the comparison against the concurrent fill row. The two-issue\nlookahead gives fetch an intervening clock edge at which to select the word.\n Read PC+8 and the exception entry's row concurrently from two copies\nof the instruction-word RAM. Register the exception tag-match bit with\nthose reads. At the next accepted issue use that bit to select which RAM\noutput enters the existing fabric candidate register. Register the two\nread addresses and concurrent fill metadata at the read edge and test\nthe selected port's collision in the response stage. This removes tag\nqualification and row selection from the RAM address setup path and moves\ncollision checking out of that cycle.\n\nThis retiming should preserve the champion's 3,061,795 marked cycles.\nAt that count the replacement threshold is just above 93.53 MHz.\nCompare the three-placement median and the marked interval, since the\nRF and EX paths are close to the replay path on the current placement.\n\nhyp-20261122-002-r12s1 Commit instruction replay reads one clock after issue {'from': 'lfsr_22_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_id/reg_q.alternate_pc_6_s0', 'slack_ns': -7.291, 'arrival_ns': 17.585, 'logic_cells': 42, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_rf', 'cpu/u_if']} The champion takes 3,061,795 cycles for ten marked iterations. On placement 0,\nits longest path runs from the adjacent producer's result to the clock enable\nof fetch history. On placements 1 and 2 the longest path ends at read_ok_q.\nA load-hit check or branch correction can arrive late because the replay\nRAM, its candid\n…[14524 of 17596 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:993ed7960d5faaff]…\n.\n\nVerify conditional predictions in two stages. EX compares the actual forwarded operands and registers a misprediction bit when the branch advances. In parallel, register its recovery word address, selecting between the two captured alternatives using the predicted direction. For a direction mismatch the other successor is precisely the one wanted, including fallthrough on a taken misaligned branch. In the following cycle this registered event redirects fetch and annuls the one younger instruction already in EX as well as the instruction in decode. A correct prediction continues at one instruction per cycle. A miss costs one additional cycle. Keep the existing exact JAL and JALR resolution and let the older registered correction win every conflict.\n\nThe experiment trades the extra recovery cycles for shorter paths at both ends of the predictor. Measure conditional recoveries and marked cycles together with the median Gowin Fmax. At 3.10 million cycles a median above 94.70 MHz clears the tournament margin.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
