{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r['round_id']==13 and r['slot']==1:\n  print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\ncat formal/checks_si.cfg\ncat fpga/core_bench_si.sv\"", "aggregated_output": "Give fetch an independent PC and a two-entry registered instruction queue.\nFetch predicts the successor from the fetch PC alone. A small tagged\nbranch-target table supplies an aligned in-RAM target and conditional\ndirection counter. A miss predicts PC+4. Probe two parallel 2,048-row\nsynchronous block RAMs at the ordinary successor index, one holding\ntagged instructions and one holding tagged target and counter entries.\nOn a hold, reprobe the held PC. A correction can leave the RAM response\none address behind, so carry the probed row and verify the complete\nin-window PC before using either response. Use the live word on ready,\nor a valid full-tag cache\nhit on a wait. Enqueue the instruction, PC and predicted successor\ntogether. Decode reads registers and probes the existing load tables\nfrom the registered queue head. With one queue entry occupied, enqueue\nand dequeue can sustain one instruction per cycle. Registered occupancy\nsupplies space for one more fetch when decode first stalls.\n\nIn decode, compare the recorded successor separately against fallthrough\nand the decoded direct target. Carry those match bits into EX, where the\nresolved direction selects the verdict. Check the actual aligned JALR\ntarget against the recorded address. Give the branch comparator and JALR\nadder a dedicated operand path from ID/EX or the ordinary EX/MEM result.\nWhen a queued control transfer consumes an immediately preceding MUL\nresult, hold the queue head and bubble EX until the product reaches the\nwrite-first register-file ports. This removes the DSP result from the\nrecovery path and lets fetch us