{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_75","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport random\nrng=random.Random(0x52)\nscale=1<<32\ndef check(n,d):\n e=max(0,d.bit_length()-10)\n u=(d+(1<<e)-1)>>e\n m=(scale//u)>>e\n q=0;r=n\n for _ in range(3):\n  z=(r*m)>>32\n  q+=z;r-=z*d\n assert 0<=r<3*d\n c=(r>=d)+(r>=2*d)\n assert (q+c,r-c*d)==divmod(n,d)\n return c\nmaxcorr=0; total=0\nfor e in range(23):\n for b in range(512,1025):\n  for d in [x for x in ((b<<e)-1,b<<e,(b<<e)+1) if 2<=x<scale]:\n   for n in [d-1,d,scale-1,((scale-1)//d)*d]:\n    maxcorr=max(maxcorr,check(n,d));total+=1\nfor _ in range(300000):\n d=rng.randrange(2,scale);n=rng.randrange(scale)\n maxcorr=max(maxcorr,check(n,d));total+=1\nprint(total,maxcorr)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_75","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport random\nrng=random.Random(0x52)\nscale=1<<32\ndef check(n,d):\n e=max(0,d.bit_length()-10)\n u=(d+(1<<e)-1)>>e\n m=(scale//u)>>e\n q=0;r=n\n for _ in range(3):\n  z=(r*m)>>32\n  q+=z;r-=z*d\n assert 0<=r<3*d\n c=(r>=d)+(r>=2*d)\n assert (q+c,r-c*d)==divmod(n,d)\n return c\nmaxcorr=0; total=0\nfor e in range(23):\n for b in range(512,1025):\n  for d in [x for x in ((b<<e)-1,b<<e,(b<<e)+1) if 2<=x<scale]:\n   for n in [d-1,d,scale-1,((scale-1)//d)*d]:\n    maxcorr=max(maxcorr,check(n,d));total+=1\nfor _ in range(300000):\n d=rng.randrange(2,scale);n=rng.randrange(scale)\n maxcorr=max(maxcorr,check(n,d));total+=1\nprint(total,maxcorr)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"441580 1\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_76", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r15s1.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261122-002-r15s1\ntitle: Fold RV32M execution into one registered DSP multiplier\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Round 14 exposed two expensive RV32M paths. A registered product can feed\n  the next EX result through the operand muxes, and the wide divider chains\n  two quotient-digit decisions per clock. The timed loop executes 93,960\n  MULs and no DIV or REM. Putting division on the same DSP used for MUL\n  could remove both divider feedback networks while preserving single-cycle\n  issue of multiplies whose operands are ready.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use one 33-by-33 signed multiplier with registered input operands for all\n  eight M operations. On ordinary MUL issue, load those registers from the\n  write-first ID operands and capture the signed extension bits and required\n  product half at the same edge. Interlock a MUL whose source still depends\n  on the adjacent EX writer until MEM can supply the value. On the next\n  advancing edge capture the selected product word in the ordinary EX/MEM\n  result register. A consumer then gets it through the same bypass as ADD.\n  The input registers also accept divider micro-operations during a divide.\n  This puts operation selection before the DSP input registers and removes\n  the separate product-half muxes from both consumer operands.\n\n  For DIV and REM, capture unsigned magnitudes X and D from the actual\n  forwarded EX operands. For D >= 2, prepare a conservative reciprocal.\n  Let e = max(0, floor(log2(D)) - 9), U = ceil(D / 2\"'^e), and use a constant\n  ROM indexed by U to obtain M = floor(2''^32 / U) >> e. Represent U = 1024\n  explicitly. From R = X and Q = 0, use three pairs of DSP clocks. The\n  first clock of each pair captures z = high32(R * M). The second computes\n  R = R - low32(z * D) and accumulates Q = Q + z. Zero-extend both DSP\n  operands for these steps. The estimate never exceeds the true quotient,\n  so z * D fits in 32 bits and Q * D + R always equals X.\n\n  Correct the result with parallel comparisons of the final R against D and\n  a 33-bit 2*D. Add 0, 1 or 2 to Q and subtract the corresponding multiple\n  from R. For e = 0, rounding the reciprocal down leaves R < 2D after one\n  pair. For e > 0, each pair takes r = R/D to less than r/512 + 2 because\n  the rounded-up denominator differs from D by less than D/512 and the\n  fixed-point reciprocal loses less than one unit. Here the initial r is\n  below 2''^22, so three pairs leave R < 3D. Restore the RV32M sign after\n  correction. Handle magnitudes D = 0 and D = 1 explicitly, including\n  signed overflow, on the same completion schedule.\n\n  Reuse the existing divide start, PREP, six RUN clocks and held DONE\n  response. An isolated multiply-input interlock prototype validated ten\n  CoreMark iterations in 3,091,220 marked cycles, 0.96 percent above the\n  champion. It reached 86.260 MHz median with the original divider. At\n  that cycle count the shared unit needs over 94.44 MHz to qualify. An\n  integer model checked the proposed recurrence against exact division on\n  441,580 boundary and random input pairs. The shared DSP design has yet\n  to be placed. Reciprocal setup and DSP-to-residual subtraction are its\n  main timing risks.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: low\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/alu.sv\n    description: |\n      Replace the two radix-4 instances with the reciprocal ROM, residual\n      and quotient registers, and the six-use DSP sequencer. Infer one\n      multiply expression for both architectural products and divider steps.\n      Hold the divide response until consumed. Apply the existing ALTOPS\n      formulas to the same captured operands with the same external timing.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Route the selected product through the ordinary result register.\n      Forward that single result to adjacent consumers. Launch a divide\n      once from resolved operands and preserve both captured values for RVFI.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect accepted ID operands and product mode to the DSP input\n      registers. Interlock only a validated multiply with a matching nonzero\n      adjacent writer. A waiting consumer holds fetch and bubbles ID/EX\n      while its producer advances, subject to older memory backpressure.\n  - file: test/test_alu.py\n    description: |\n      Check all M operations, including reciprocal bucket transitions,\n      magnitudes 0 and 1, unsigned maxima, and dividends one either side\n      of an exact multiple. Exercise reset and held results in each phase.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check dependent and independent multiplies, signed-high variants,\n      MUL to DIV and DIV to MUL transfers, and a held MEM transaction.\n      Verify exact values, expected interlock cycles and gap-free RVFI order.\nYAML\npython - <<'\"'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r15s1.yaml')\nd=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()); jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nassert d['id']==p.stem and d['category']=='structural' and type(d['expected_impact']['fitness_delta_pct']) is int\nprint('Valid', d['title'],292.05*1.046*3091220/1e7)\nPY\ngit status --short\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
