{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith('r1s1') or d['id'].endswith('r2s1'): print(d['id'],d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],d.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20261122-002-r1s1 Give DIV, DIVU, REM and REMU one shared, blocking restoring divider in EX. Capture the forwarded operands and sign flags at issue. Run four registered rounds of eight binary quotient steps each on unsigned magnitudes. Each step uses the borrow from a trial subtraction to choose the next remainder and quotient bit. A separate completion cycle selects quotient or remainder and applies the RV32M sign and exceptional-case rules. The start, four rounds and completion take six EX cycles, which leaves room for a load-use or redirect bubble within the formal progress window.\n\nHold fetch and ID/EX while the divide is pending. Drain older instructions and inject side-effect-free EX/MEM bubbles until the result can advance. Issue once, only when an older memory request can advance, and capture both original source values for RVFI before the forwarding sources drain. Transfer the result once through the ordinary EX/MEM forwarding and retirement path. Integer ALU operations and all multiplies retain their single-cycle EX latency.\n\nA 50% gain at the baseline's 4,491,485 bracketed cycles would require 8.16 MHz. The prediction is that cutting the division recurrence from 32 steps to eight per clock will clear that frequency with little change in bracketed cycles. Compare median Gowin Fmax and marker-to-marker cycles separately to test that prediction.\n \nhyp-20261122-002-r2s1 Rebuild the blocking divider as a clocked radix-4 engine. On issue, capture unsigned operand magnitudes and the existing sign and operation flags. In one preparation cycle, register three times the divisor. Use the registered divisor and its left shift for the other two multiples. Each RUN cycle produces six quotient bits through three successive radix-4 digit decisions. For each incoming bit pair, form T = 4R + pair and compute T-D, T-2D and T-3D in parallel. Select the highest digit with a nonnegative difference, using its difference as the new remainder. Digit zero passes T through.\n\nInitialize a 36-bit quotient shift register with four leading zeros and the 32-bit dividend magnitude. Six identical RUN cycles consume all 36 bits and leave the quotient in the low 32 bits. Unsigned trials and multiples need 34 bits of magnitude and an explicit borrow bit on subtraction. Apply the existing quotient/remainder selection and sign correction at completion. Use the same issue, preparation, six RUN cycles and result transfer for real arithmetic and ALTOPS. This gives nine EX cycles per divide and a ten-cycle retirement gap after a dependent load on ready buses, within the configured formal liveness window.\n\nI predict about 40 MHz median Gowin Fmax and roughly the champion's marker-to-marker cycle count, or about 89 CoreMark iterations/s. Measure both independently. The chief uncertainty is whether routing the three parallel trials per digit or a path outside the divider limits Fmax first.\n \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
