{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d.get('category')=='predictor':\n  print(d['title'],d['cycles'],d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:1400])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"Predict direct branches at fetch with a 128-entry bimodal table 4202272 Use the fetched instruction to identify legal conditional branches and JAL.\nIn parallel with immediate generation, read a 128-entry table of two-bit\nsaturating counters indexed by the fetch PC. For a conditional branch use\nthe counter MSB, and for JAL predict taken. Select the aligned PC-relative\ntarget on a taken prediction and PC+4 otherwise. JALR resolves in EX.\nComputing direct targets from the current instruction makes table aliasing\naffect only direction and requires just one prediction bit per instruction.\n\nAfter initialization, train once per valid conditional branch advancing\nfrom EX. A correct prediction lets an accepted fetch enter ID/EX on the\nresolution edge.\nA mismatch flushes that fetch and loads the architectural successor into\nthe PC. The prototype's three Gowin placement options reached 5.155,\n5.488 and 5.488 MHz. Combining the median with the measured cycle count\nyields 13.06 iter/s, 7.75% above the 12.12 baseline. Placement option 0\nused 533 additional logic cells and 265 additional registers.\n\nPredict direct transfers at fetch on the multicycle-divider champion 4202272 Read 128 two-bit direction counters by fetch PC in parallel with forming\nthe direct target from the fetched B or J immediate. Use the counter MSB\nfor legal conditional branches and predict aligned, in-range JALs taken.\nBoth IF and EX derive direct targets from the same instruction, so a single\ndirection bit suffices to detect a wrong successor. Resolve JALR in EX.\nA correct prediction lets the successor enter ID/EX on the resolution edge.\nA mismatch kills that fetch and redirects to the architectural successor.\nA temporary prototype on the current champion completed ten iterations\nin 4,202,272 bracketed cycles with both bus stalls and all CRCs validated.\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 32.148, 32.241 and 32.241 MHz.\nThe median yields 76.72 iter/s, 7.71% above the champion. The worst path\nremains inside the divider. Placement option 0 used 3,587 logic cells\nand 827 registers.\n\nPredict control transfers from ready operands during fetch 4151767 Use the youngest available producer to predict control flow. For each\nsource of the fetched instruction, start with the write-first regfile\nvalue. Bypass a matching non-load result from EX/MEM. Mark the source\nbusy on a matching ID/EX writer or a matching EX/MEM load. The ID/EX\nmatch takes priority even when EX/MEM also matches. Ignore writes to x0.\n\nFor a legal conditional branch with both sources ready, evaluate its\ncomparison in IF. If either is busy, predict from a 128-entry table\nof two-bit counters. Form one PC-relative sum for branches and JAL.\nFor a legal zero-offset JALR with rs1 ready, use the ready value with\nbit zero cleared. Other JALRs predict PC+4. Require every speculative\ntarget to be word aligned and inside the 1 MiB instruction window.\n\nCarry the chosen direction to EX. Each predicted taken target comes\nfrom the actual instruction, and early JALR requires a current base\noperand. EX therefore needs only a direction comparison for recovery.\nKeep the normal forwarded EX resolution for traps, jump links, RVFI\nand the recovery address. Decode raw fetch data in parallel with\nresolution and squash younger work through the ID/EX controls.\n\nA temporary prototype on the current champion completed ten iterations\nin 4,151,767 bracketed cycles with both bus stalls and all five CRCs\nvalidated. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 47.587, 49.383\nand 49.383 MHz. The me\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
