{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_74","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r5s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-002-r5s1\ntitle: Pair each issue slot with a registered successor for replay during fetch stalls\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 4,164,193 cycles for ten CoreMark iterations. Decode\n  inserts a bubble whenever the instruction bus stalls, including on hot\n  paths whose instruction sequence the core has issued many times.\n  The previous two-entry fetch queue could supply an instruction during a\n  stall only if an earlier cycle had delivered a spare word. A replay path\n  can supply consecutive issue slots throughout a run of fetch stalls.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a registered second source of instructions to single-issue decode.\n  As one instruction issues, use its word PC modulo 512 to look up the\n  successor observed on an earlier traversal. Read the candidate word and\n  its destination PC synchronously, in parallel with ordinary next-PC\n  selection. A ready bus supplies the live instruction. On a bus wait,\n  the registered word can issue if its saved PC exactly matches the PC\n  selected for this cycle. An accepted replay starts its own lookup, so\n  a sequence can keep issuing at one word per cycle. EX retains authority\n  over redirects, and decode still waits for data hazards and backpressure.\n\n  In a scratch prototype, ten iterations took 3,394,055 marked cycles with\n  both stall generators and all five canonical CRCs. Gowin placement options\n  0, 1 and 2 reached 58.255, 60.339 and 60.339 MHz. The median gives 177.78\n  iterations/s, 14.34% above 155.48. The acceptance threshold at that cycle\n  count is 55.20 MHz. The median's worst path runs from the replay RAM\n  through register-file lookup to the fetch PC.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 14\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Keep a 512 by 51-bit simple dual-port block RAM. Record the live\n      successor word, its PC[19:2] and a valid bit at the previous\n      issued PC's index when the successor issues from a ready bus inside\n      the 1 MiB instruction window. Check the full window and word PC on\n      replay. Read on advance and hold the candidate across a decode hold.\n      Disable a colliding read on a simultaneous write to the same row.\n      After reset or an executed FENCE.I, sweep valid bits clear while live\n      fetch continues. Also invalidate the registered candidate and predecessor.\n      A read taken during the sweep must stay ineligible even if the sweep\n      finishes while decode is held.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Feed the selected word to decode, both RF reads, readiness checks and\n      forwarding compares. Give the hazard unit the combined instruction\n      availability. On an advancing FENCE.I in EX, invalidate replay state,\n      squash the younger issue slot and restart at the fence's PC+4 after\n      older memory work advances. Drive all prediction logic from the word\n      that decode sees. Retain single retirement on channel zero.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Train a loop, stall imem for several adjacent instructions, and check\n      consecutive ordered retirements from replay. Cover a changed branch\n      outcome, aliased predecessor indices, and a load-use or dmem hold\n      during replay. Supply a different instruction at the same PC after\n      FENCE.I and check that only the new word retires. Check stale-word\n      rejection across reset and across the final cycle of a validity sweep.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
