{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_96", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r6s1.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-002-r6s1\ntitle: \"Reserve the data port in decode and carry unfinished accesses into MEM\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 3,394,055 marked cycles for ten iterations at\n  62.730 MHz. A load first sees the bus in MEM, where an immediately\n  dependent instruction already costs a bubble. Round five demonstrated\n  that a ready base can move address generation into ID. The opportunity\n  on this champion is to overlap the first bus attempt with execution\n  while preserving a registered address and an in-order retry path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Schedule the single data port one edge ahead. On an advancing EX edge,\n  give an unfinished EX load or store the next port cycle in MEM.\n  Otherwise load the upcoming ID instruction's base-plus-offset address\n  into the port register. Record an EX reservation on that edge when its\n  base is available. In the next cycle, EX may use the reservation only\n  for an issued, aligned load or store. This makes consecutive EX accesses\n  possible and keeps the address stable for a retry.\n\n  Capture an accepted EX load's extended value in a register alongside\n  the ALU result. Capture a separate early-load select on the same edge.\n  The following consumer uses that value through the adjacent-producer\n  bypass. A busy base, port conflict or denied handshake sends the access\n  through MEM, retaining the ordinary load-use interlock for that load.\n  Record early acceptance for stores as well, so each RAM or MMIO request\n  is accepted once in program order.\n\n  A scratch implementation completed ten iterations in 3,172,994 marked\n  cycles with both stall generators and all five canonical CRCs. Gowin\n  placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 62.630, 63.248 and 63.248 MHz.\n  That median gives 199.33 iterations/s, 7.85% above the champion. At\n  this cycle count the acceptance threshold is 61.341 MHz. The prototype\n  passed all 53 fast formal checks, the stalled and ready-bus reference\n  traces, and all five cocotb suites with the revised pipeline tests.\nexpected_impact:\n \n…[1647 of 4719 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5f96c72a9c25083e]…\n an early completion. For that instruction\n      use the saved word for RVFI and the saved extended value for decode\n      bypass and writeback. Serve pending instructions with the usual lane\n      extraction, trap and memory backpressure. Use the same architectural\n      address, masks and data whichever stage performed the transfer.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Verify adjacent retirement after an accepted EX byte, halfword or\n      word load. Deny an early store for several clocks and check address,\n      data and byte enables on each attempt, followed by one handshake.\n      Exercise a busy base, a dependent store value, two contenders for\n      the port, misalignment, MMIO, and a wrong-path store. Check RVFI\n      order and operands. In the branch-bypass test, deny the second load\n      once so it still exercises the MEM bypass. In the ordered-loop test,\n      deny the EX attempt and the three existing MEM attempts. This keeps\n      both tests' exact fetch-sequence assertions.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
