{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_35", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(l)\n if d['id'].endswith(('r7s2','r7s0','r4s2','r5s2')):\n  print(d['id'],d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'][:2100])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r4s2 Give fetch one 32-bit equality comparator. For a legal BEQ or BNE whose\nsources are both ready, use the comparison of the existing MEM/WB-bypassed\nID operands. A source is ready when the instruction in ID/EX has no pending\nwrite to that nonzero register. For ordered branches and busy-source\nequality branches, use a PC-indexed table of 128 two-bit saturating counters.\nPredict direct JAL taken. For legal zero-offset JALR with a ready rs1,\nuse rs1 with bit zero cleared. Require word alignment and a target inside\nthe 1 MiB instruction window for a taken prediction. Default to PC+4.\n\nForm PC+4 and the direct B/J target during fetch. Register the unused\ndirect successor at issue so EX has the recovery address before it resolves\nthe condition. A predicted JALR target is exact because its base is ready\nand its displacement is zero. A single direction bit therefore suffices\nfor recovery. EX still resolves every control transfer with the true\nforwarded operands and preserves misalignment traps and jump links.\n\nA scratch RTL prototype on this champion completed ten stalled iterations\nin 4,164,193 marked cycles with all five canonical CRCs. Gowin placement\noptions 0, 1 and 2 reached 61.308, 62.308 and 62.308 MHz. The median gives\n149.63 iter/s, 7.26 percent above 139.50. At this cycle count the 4.6 percent\nacceptance margin requires a median above 60.763 MHz. Placement option 0\nused 3,278 logic cells and 628 registers. The prototype passed all 53\nstandard formal checks. Its RVFI traces matched the reference for\nselftest and the three random programs, both with and without bus stalls.\n\nhyp-20261122-003-r5s2 Add a 512-row last-successor predictor indexed by the issuing PC[10:2].\nAfter instruction A is followed by B, A's row holds one valid bit, B's\nPC[19:2] and B's instruction word. When A issues again, read its row\nsynchronously while the existing direction and target logic selects the\nnext PC. Use the live word whenever imemReady is high. On a bus stall,\nissue the saved word only if its address exactly \n…[1974 of 5046 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5233da7d4b7ebafc]…\n addresses and the I-immediate used to predict a load\nrow. Give these 17 bits a distributed RAM with an asynchronous read at\nthe predicted next index and a capture flop on the same edge as the\nblock RAM read. Reassemble the saved instruction in decode. This makes\nthe saved operand fields available at flop clock-to-Q and keeps the\ndata-RAM address calculation out of the instruction block RAM output path.\n\nA prototype with the entire instruction in block RAM completed ten\niterations in 3,104,776 marked\ncycles with both bus stalls, the five expected CRCs and the validation\nbanner. Gowin measured 62.193, 65.910 and 65.910 MHz, yielding 212.29\niterations/s. That is 3.25% above 205.60. At that cycle count, the\n4.6% replacement margin requires more than 66.77 MHz. Its median\nplacement had a path from the instruction block RAM through the\nload predictor to lookup_ok_q. The proposed split targets that path.\nThe split layout has not been timed. A 67.5 MHz median at the same\ncycle count would yield 217.4 iterations/s.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
