{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_160","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cat > 'cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r8s1.yaml' <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261122-002-r8s1\ntitle: Select the adjacent producer's result bank separately for each EX operand\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The champion's median Gowin placement reaches 71.73 MHz. Its worst path begins\n  at the registered rs1 forwarding match, passes through the EX address adder\n  and load-hit comparison, and ends at the fetch predecessor register. The\n  previous round registered the ordinary result and both product halves in\n  parallel. Today EX first merges those banks onto one shared 32-bit bus, then\n  decides whether each operand needs that bus. Both decisions are already\n  knowable on the edge that admits the consumer to ID/EX.\nhypothesis: |\n  Move bank selection across the ID/EX boundary. For each consumer source,\n  compare its register number in ID with the adjacent producer's rd in EX.\n  On operand capture, latch three bits for that source. They indicate a match\n  to an ordinary writer, a match to any multiply writer, and a match to a\n  high-half multiply writer. Qualify matches with a real nonzero destination\n  and suppress the link write for a jump with a misaligned target.\n  \n  In EX, two 32-bit muxes run in parallel for each source. One chooses the\n  consumer's captured operand or the raw ordinary EX/MEM result. The other\n  chooses the registered low or high product. The registered multiply-match\n  bit selects between their outputs. This shortens the forwarding path into\n  the address adder, branch comparator and ALU without adding a pipeline\n  stage or an interlock. MEM still selects the retiring producer's result\n  using its producer-side bank enables.\n  \n  A scratch build reached 79.683 MHz on Gowin placement option 1, using 3,998\n  logic cells, 743 registers, nine block RAMs and two DSPs. The champion needs\n  a median above 75.03 MHz at 3,132,302 marked cycles to clear the 4.6 percent\n  replacement margin.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 11\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Pass both ID source comparisons against id_ex_w.rd and the ID/EX operand\n      capture enable to EX. Replace the old shared EX/MEM-to-EX forwarding\n      connection with these controls. Keep the normal MEM result path to the\n      register file and the existing load-use interlock.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Beside the result registers, form each source's three selects from its\n      match, producer write permission and MUL class. Register all six selects\n      on the same edge as the consumer operands and hold them on stalls. Feed\n      the ordinary operand mux directly from reg_q.alu_result and the ID/EX\n      source latch. Feed the product mux from product_high and product_low.\n      Use the resulting operand pair everywhere EX consumes forwarded sources,\n      including stores, cached-load verification, multiplier inputs and divide\n      launch. A missed or misaligned adjacent load retains its existing\n      interlock, and a faulting JAL/JALR selects the previously captured value.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Exercise consecutive MUL and high-half multiply producers with rs1-only,\n      rs2-only and both-source dependencies. Include a dmem hold while the\n      selected product is live. Check exact operands, results and retirement\n      cycles across transitions back to an ordinary writer and a faulting link.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-002-r8s1.yaml');d=yaml.safe_load(p.read_text());jsonschema.validate(d,json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()));print(d['id'],d['category'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
