{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['id'].endswith(('r6s2','r7s2','r8s1')):\n  for k in ('id','hypothesis','implementation_notes','critical_path','changes'):\n   print(k,r.get(k))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "id hyp-20261122-003-r6s2\nhypothesis {'motivation': 'The 512-entry instruction successor predictor already recovered 770,138\\nmarked cycles. Loads still wait for MEM and interlock an adjacent consumer.\\nAn earlier ready-base data-port experiment recovered 243,368 cycles on\\nthe previous core. Predicting the data RAM row at issue can recover both\\nan adjacent load-use bubble and a data-bus wait on the same load.\\n', 'hypothesis': \"Predict one of 1024 word rows at issue. With a ready source, add the\\nI-immediate to the bypassed decode base and select sum[11:2]. With a\\npending base, use the load PC's last observed byte stride to predict its\\nnext row.\\nThis launches one synchronous block RAM read while the load moves into EX.\\nCompare the candidate's entire word address to the true forwarded load\\naddress. A valid, aligned RAM hit can forward the extended word on the\\nnext clock and complete locally. On a miss, MEM requests the resolved\\naddress and the usual load-use interlock protects a dependent consumer.\\n\\nA scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,122,498 marked cycles\\nwith both bus stalls and all five canonical CRCs, down from 3,394,055.\\nEX verified 435,226 of 544,852 loads, including 29,196 for which the\\npreceding instruction was still producing the base at issue.\\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 gave 61.281, 64.904 and 64.904 MHz.\\nThe median yields 207.86 iterations/s, 12.47% above the champion. At\\nthis cycle count 60.37 MHz clears the tournament margin. The prototype\\nused 4032 logic cells and nine block RAMs. It passed all 53 fast formal\\nchecks, all five cocotb suites and the selftest and three random\\nreference traces with and without bus stalls.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 12, 'confidence': 'high'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/load_predictor.sv', 'description': 'Select the synchronous word-table read address from the decode sum\\nor a 128-entry PC[8:2] stride table. Each stride entry records the\\nlast actual byte address modulo 4096 and the predicted next word\\nindex. Initi\n…[17586 of 20658 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:0ecae58e6e75c5c7]…\nge.sv', 'description': \"Beside the result registers, form each source's three selects from its\\nmatch, producer write permission and MUL class. Register all six selects\\non the same edge as the consumer operands and hold them on stalls. Feed\\nthe ordinary operand mux directly from reg_q.alu_result and the ID/EX\\nsource latch. Feed the product mux from product_high and product_low.\\nUse the resulting operand pair everywhere EX consumes forwarded sources,\\nincluding stores, cached-load verification, multiplier inputs and divide\\nlaunch. A missed or misaligned adjacent load retains its existing\\ninterlock, and a faulting JAL/JALR selects the previously captured value.\\n\"}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Exercise consecutive MUL and high-half multiply producers with rs1-only,\\nrs2-only and both-source dependencies. Include a dmem hold while the\\nselected product is live. Check exact operands, results and retirement\\ncycles across transitions back to an ordinary writer and a faulting link.\\n'}]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
