{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_33", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['id'].endswith(('r8s2','r8s0')):\n  print(r['title'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\n  for e in r['changes']: print(e)\nPY\ngit log --oneline -7\"", "aggregated_output": "Forward the ordinary EX/MEM result directly and interlock multiply consumers Drive the adjacent EX bypass from the registered ordinary result alone. Keep\nthe full product advancing beside it and select the architectural result in\nMEM for writeback. If a valid multiply in EX names either source field of\nthe instruction in ID, reuse the load-use interlock for one cycle. ID then\ncaptures the product from the register file's write-first port as MEM\ncompletes. Independent multiplies can still issue on consecutive cycles.\nThis trades occasional dependency bubbles for a shorter path on every\nordinarily forwarded operand, including the load base and branch operands.\nA multiply that consumes an adjacent ordinary ALU result still gets that\noperand on time through the direct bypass.\n\nA scratch implementation completed ten iterations in 3,167,223 marked cycles,\nup 1.11% from 3,132,302. With both bus stalls enabled it produced all five\ncanonical CRCs and the validation message. Gowin placement options 0, 1 and\n2 reached 73.720, 76.884 and 76.884 MHz. The median gives 242.75 iterations\nper second, 6.00% above the champion and above the 239.53 acceptance cutoff.\nPlacement 0 used 4,001 logic cells, 739 registers and two DSPs. The scratch\ncore passed all five cocotb suites with the revised timing expectations\nand matched the reference on selftest and all three random traces with\nready and stalled buses. All four ALTOPS multiply checks, single-channel\nretirement uniqueness and liveness passed.\n\n{'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Expose a 32-bit fast_bypass output driven by reg_q.alu_result and a\\none-bit slow_producer output driven by mul_low || mul_high.\\nKeep the registered product halves and one-hot selection on out.alu_result\\nfor the MEM path. The ordinary bank already carries integer results,\\nextended load-hit data and completed divides.\\n'}\n{'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': \"Connect the dedicated ordinary-result output to u_ex.fwd_ex_mem. Drive\\nthe existing hazard unit's late-result input with\\n(id_ex_w.ctrl\n…[3735 of 6807 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:98a5e064f4e7c25d]…\nn advancing repair edge, clear EX/MEM valid and\\nside-effect controls. This drains the load and releases its forwarding\\nselects before the held consumer executes with repaired operands.\\n'}\n{'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Warm a row, alternate two RAM pages at the same index, and delay\\nmiss responses. Check an ADD using both repaired sources, a DIVU,\\na store, a branch over a wrong-path store, and a misaligned load\\npreserving its old destination. Assert exact accepted bus transactions,\\nRVFI order and values, and load-to-ADD retirement gaps of two cycles\\non a repaired miss and one on a verified hit.\\n'}\n5049128 log: hyp-20261122-003-r8s2 regression\n79d4392 log: hyp-20261122-002-r8s1 improvement\ncbc39ee log: hyp-20261122-001-r8s0 regression\n3f97612 hyp-20261122-002-r8s1: Select the adjacent producer's result bank separately for each EX operand (+9.2%)\n26000bf log: hyp-20261122-003-r7s2 regression\nbf3ea70 log: hyp-20261122-002-r7s1 improvement\n6030490 log: hyp-20261122-001-r7s0 regression\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
