{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_35", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r8s2','r9s1','r6s2','r7s2')):\n  print(r['id']);print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis']);print(r['hypothesis']['changes'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r6s2\nPredict one of 1024 word rows at issue. With a ready source, add the\nI-immediate to the bypassed decode base and select sum[11:2]. With a\npending base, use the load PC's last observed byte stride to predict its\nnext row.\nThis launches one synchronous block RAM read while the load moves into EX.\nCompare the candidate's entire word address to the true forwarded load\naddress. A valid, aligned RAM hit can forward the extended word on the\nnext clock and complete locally. On a miss, MEM requests the resolved\naddress and the usual load-use interlock protects a dependent consumer.\n\nA scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,122,498 marked cycles\nwith both bus stalls and all five canonical CRCs, down from 3,394,055.\nEX verified 435,226 of 544,852 loads, including 29,196 for which the\npreceding instruction was still producing the base at issue.\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 gave 61.281, 64.904 and 64.904 MHz.\nThe median yields 207.86 iterations/s, 12.47% above the champion. At\nthis cycle count 60.37 MHz clears the tournament margin. The prototype\nused 4032 logic cells and nine block RAMs. It passed all 53 fast formal\nchecks, all five cocotb suites and the selftest and three random\nreference traces with and without bus stalls.\n\n[{'file': 'rtl/load_predictor.sv', 'description': 'Select the synchronous word-table read address from the decode sum\\nor a 128-entry PC[8:2] stride table. Each stride entry records the\\nlast actual byte address modulo 4096 and the predicted next word\\nindex. Initialize the two distributed RAM arrays to zero at FPGA\\nconfiguration. At each advancing load, compute the next byte address\\nas (2 * actual - previous) modulo 4096 and store bits [11:2].\\nCapture the prior address at issue so each array needs one read\\nport and one write port. Hold the candidate with its EX instruction\\nthrough stalls. Each word-table row contains a\\nvalid bit, the full in-RAM word address and a 32-bit raw word.\\nRefill on accepted in-window reads or aligned full-word RAM stores.\\nInvalidate\n…[12688 of 15760 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ac605b879676659c]…\nure the concurrent MEM update enable\\nand write row on the same edge and compare the registered rows in EX.\\nHold them with the candidate through stalls. Retain the scrub gate,\\nlive older-store veto, refill rules and stride training.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Exercise offsets 0 through 3 on an aligned live base and a byte load\\ncrossing into the next word from an unaligned base. Check the accepted\\nreads, byte masks, values and adjacent consumer retirement gaps.\\nFor collision, alias and warm-reset cases that require a first-visit\\nhit on a previously filled row, put the target word address in rs1\\nand use displacement zero. Keep the same effective addresses,\\naccepted-transaction checks and retirement assertions. Warm the\\nstride path before requiring bus-free hits for negative or larger\\ndisplacements. Exercise a replayed branch, JALR and load with\\nunrelated live bus bits. Check the exact RVFI sequence and absence\\nof wrong-path stores, including under data backpressure.\\n'}]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_36","type":"agent_message","text":"The free-running RAM prototype matched the champion’s 3,131,081 cycles, but Gowin placement option 1 fell from 85.513 to 82.333 MHz. The timing report traced the new limit through load-hit verification. I’ll test a predictor that moves that check off the path controlling issue."}}
