{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261122-001-r7s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; r=json.loads(sys.stdin.read()); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"changes\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_46","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg 'hyp-20261122-001-r7s0' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl | python -c 'import json,sys; r=json.loads(sys.stdin.read()); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"hypothesis\\\"]); print(r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"][\\\"changes\\\"])'\"","aggregated_output":"Give the multiplier registered inputs. Capture the two MEM/WB-bypassed\noperands on decode issue, with the signedness appropriate to MUL,\nMULH, MULHSU or MULHU. The DSP can start directly from these flops.\nA multiply with an unresolved adjacent writer takes one repair cycle\nin EX to overwrite the same input flops with the actual forwarded\nvalues. It then executes through the identical product path. This\nremoves both the adjacent bypass and PC/immediate selection from the\nDSP inputs while preserving one-cycle issue for independent multiplies.\nMeasure repair cycles and median Fmax together. The acceptance clock\nis 67.15 MHz at the original cycle count and about 69.2 MHz if every\nmultiply costs one extra cycle. Fetch and divider timing are the main\nrisks to reaching that range.\n\n[{'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Capture dedicated multiplier operands at the ID/EX boundary using\\nthe existing bypassed ID values. Record whether either source awaits\\na nonzero EX destination on an issued multiply. Qualify capture and\\npending state with issue, stall, flush and reset. Route EX repair\\nvalues back to these same registers with priority on the repair edge.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'For a pending multiply, wait behind an older stalled memory request,\\nthen sample both resolved EX sources exactly once. Hold ID/EX and\\nfeed a bubble to EX/MEM on that edge as for a waiting divide. On\\nthe following advance use the product and the saved source values,\\nincluding for RVFI. Feed the existing arithmetic wait interlock.\\n'}, {'file': 'rtl/alu.sv', 'description': 'Feed one signed 33-by-33 multiply from the dedicated registers.\\nAppend a sign bit to both inputs for MULH, to rs1 for MULHSU,\\nand zero otherwise. Select bits 31:0 for MUL or 63:32 for a high\\nproduct. Use the same captured operands for the four ALTOPS formulas.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_alu.py', 'description': 'Drive the dedicated multiply operands in the existing product checks.\\nCheck mixed-sign and unsigned high halves with bit 31 set and vary\\nthe ordinary ALU inputs independently of the multiply inputs.\\n'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Check independent back-to-back multiplies at one retirement per\\ncycle and adjacent producers of rs1, rs2 and both sources. Include\\na stalled older load, an x0 destination and a flushed multiply.\\nCheck retirement order, both RVFI source values and every product.\\n'}]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
