{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_6", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\ndata=[json.loads(s) for s in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\")]\nfor d in data[-6:]:\n print(d['id'],d['hypothesis']); print(d.get('implementation_notes'))\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-001-r9s0 {'motivation': \"On the champion's median Gowin placement, the slowest path launches\\nfrom the successor block RAM, passes through the register file and ends\\nat the load-table collision check. The same RAM also feeds the fetch\\nbranch decision. Starting its read one issue earlier gives both paths a\\nflip-flop output to start from. The tradeoff is that a PC two issues\\nback can identify the desired instruction less often at a branch.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Retime the existing 512-row instruction replay RAM. For three successive\\nissued instructions A, B and C, read row A when A issues. On issue of B,\\ntransfer that response into a fabric register for use at C. Train row A\\nwith C's word PC and instruction when C issues from a ready bus. Replay\\nrequires a valid entry and an exact match to the current PC inside the\\nRAM window. Live bus data takes priority.\\n\\nRead the block RAM every clock. If issue pauses between A and B, save\\nA's response on the first intervening edge and hold it until B issues.\\nQueue each training write for the following clock. These two port\\nchanges remove the late issue decision from both RAM enables.\\n\\nA scratch prototype completed ten iterations with randomized instruction\\nand data stalls in 3,148,248 marked cycles versus 3,132,302 for the\\nchampion. All five CRCs and the validation banner matched. Gowin\\nplacements 0, 1 and 2 reached 78.619, 82.612 and 82.612 MHz. The\\n5.44 percent median clock gain outweighs the 0.51 percent cycle\\nincrease. The resulting throughput is 262.41 iterations/s, 4.90 percent\\nabove 250.15 and just above the 261.66 cutoff. Placement 0 used\\n4,056 logic cells, 917 registers, nine block\\nRAMs and two DSPs. All five cocotb suites passed, including the new\\nshared-JALR replay test. Selftest and all three random programs matched\\nthe reference RVFI traces on both ready and stalled buses. All 53\\nfast formal checks passed.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 5, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/if_stage.sv', 'descriptio\n…[19006 of 22078 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5acd92f19c601847]…\nalidate at EX/MEM and use that result for MEM and repair. Expose the registered forwarding-source matches, and suppress the held consumer's control transfer, divider start, training and EX/MEM side effects on repair.\"}, {'file': 'rtl/hazard_unit.sv', 'description': 'Replace the EX load-use address check with the registered-stage miss repair interlock. Let the older load complete while fetch and ID/EX hold the consumer.'}, {'file': 'rtl/id_stage.sv', 'description': \"On repair completion, patch only source operands matching a legal nonzero load destination. Preserve the consumer's PC, instruction, other operand and retirement metadata.\"}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Check irregular pointer and ADD-to-load sequences, including a byte offset crossing a word boundary, an incorrect producing LW candidate, and a stalled retry feeding a branch or store. Assert full RVFI, bus transactions and one-cycle forwarding on hits. Keep the stride-alias test on a producer ineligible for the new hint.'}]}\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
