{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\ndata=[json.loads(s) for s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')]\nfor d in data:\n if d['category']=='predictor' and ('r6s2' in d['id'] or 'r8s2' in d['id'] or 'r7s2' in d['id']): print(d['hypothesis']['motivation'])\nprint(data[-4]['critical_path'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"The 512-entry instruction successor predictor already recovered 770,138\nmarked cycles. Loads still wait for MEM and interlock an adjacent consumer.\nAn earlier ready-base data-port experiment recovered 243,368 cycles on\nthe previous core. Predicting the data RAM row at issue can recover both\nan adjacent load-use bubble and a data-bus wait on the same load.\n\nIn the champion's 3,122,498 marked cycles, fetch waited for an instruction\non 46,468 cycles even after excluding redirects, data stalls and interlocks.\nA saved word belongs to a predecessor's last transition. A branch can\ntherefore miss on a warm destination whenever it arrives by a different\nedge. The median placement also has a path from the successor RAM through\nthe register file to the fetch PC.\n\nOn the champion's 71.73 MHz placement, the longest path runs from the\nrs1 forwarding select through the effective-address adder and load-hit\ncomparator to the fetch predictor's predecessor register. A dependent\ninstruction only needs the verdict one edge later. At the champion's\n3,132,302 marked cycles, a median above 75.03 MHz clears the 4.6% margin.\n\n{'from': 'cpu/u_ex/rs1_high_q_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/words_words_0_2_s', 'slack_ns': -6.902, 'arrival_ns': 17.144, 'logic_cells': 24, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu']}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
