{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_11", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor o in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if 'r11' in o['id'] or o['id'].endswith('r10s1') or o['id'].endswith('r8s2'):\n  print(o['id'],o['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r8s2 {'motivation': \"On the champion's 71.73 MHz placement, the longest path runs from the\\nrs1 forwarding select through the effective-address adder and load-hit\\ncomparator to the fetch predictor's predecessor register. A dependent\\ninstruction only needs the verdict one edge later. At the champion's\\n3,132,302 marked cycles, a median above 75.03 MHz clears the 4.6% margin.\\n\", 'hypothesis': \"Use a valid, eligible load-table candidate to predict that the adjacent\\nconsumer can issue. Keep the existing interlock for a definite miss.\\nOn the issue edge, register one bit recording whether either source\\nmatches the preceding nonzero destination. In parallel, EX verifies\\nthe load's full forwarded word address and alignment. Only an exact\\nhit can supply data or suppress the external read.\\n\\nIf the prediction was wrong, hold the consumer in ID/EX while MEM\\ncompletes the older load. On completion, patch each matching source\\nwith the extended load result and insert one EX/MEM bubble. A trapping\\nload preserves the captured pre-load operands. The consumer then\\nexecutes once at its original PC. This costs one bubble on a dependent\\nmiss and preserves back-to-back execution on a hit.\\n\\nA scratch implementation made 189,167 such predictions in ten marked\\nCoreMark iterations. It repaired all 31,113 false hits in place and\\nfinished in the champion's 3,132,302 cycles with the canonical CRCs.\\nGowin placement 1 reached 77.694 MHz, equivalent to 248.04 iterations/s\\nat that cycle count. The selftest and three random RVFI comparisons\\npassed on both zero-wait and stalled buses.\\n\", 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': 8, 'confidence': 'medium'}, 'changes': [{'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Drive the issue-time load-use interlock with id_ex_w.ctrl.mem_read &&\\n!(candidate.valid && candidate_eligible). On each ID/EX capture,\\nregister rd != 0 && (rd == rs1 || rd == rs2), using the EX destination\\nand the two ID source fields. Hold this bit with ID/EX. Detect repair\\nfrom the registered bit, ID/\n…[16726 of 19798 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:6c4497e04e810721]…\nnterlock. Retain the dmem and divide holds.'}, {'file': 'rtl/load_predictor.sv', 'description': 'Add the producer row input to the synchronous probe. Keep delayed stride training, reset scrub, full word tags, read/write collision rejection and the older-store veto aligned with the candidate.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Carry provisional hit metadata into EX/MEM. Use the verified verdict for the MEM request and RVFI. Give a valid dependent retry priority over branch recovery, suppress its EX/MEM valid and controls, branch training and divide launch, and redirect exactly once when the older memory operation completes.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Exercise a warmed irregular pointer chain, an incorrect producer hint, an aliased page and a misaligned load feeding a store or branch. Check bus transfers, exact RVFI values and single retirement during delayed completion. Check the extra fetch of the dependent PC on a false hit and adjacent retirement on a verified hit.'}]}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
