{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat Makefile && rg --files fpga tools -g '*gowin*' -g '*timing*' && ls -la /opt\"", "aggregated_output": ".PHONY: help lint test test-infra cosim formal fpga timing next loop report bench clean\n.DEFAULT_GOAL := help\n\n# Resolve OSS CAD Suite path so commands work even when the user hasn't\n# sourced .toolchain/oss-cad-suite/environment in this shell.\nTOOLCHAIN_DIR := $(CURDIR)/.toolchain\nOSS_BIN       := $(TOOLCHAIN_DIR)/oss-cad-suite/bin\nLOCAL_BIN     := $(TOOLCHAIN_DIR)/bin\nexport PATH := $(OSS_BIN):$(LOCAL_BIN):$(PATH)\n\n# Orchestrator knobs (pass-through to tools.orchestrator):\n#   N        — number of rounds for `make loop` (default 10).\n#   K        — tournament size, slots per round (default 1 = sequential).\n#   AGENT    — codex (default) or claude. Honors a pre-existing\n#              AGENT_PROVIDER env var if AGENT isn't set on the make\n#              command line.\n#   BASE     — when forking a new TARGET, copy from cores/<BASE>/.\n#              Default: baseline (orchestrator-side default).\n#   COREMARK — target CoreMark iterations/sec (e.g. '370').\n#   LUT      — target LUT count (e.g. '3000').\n#   WORKTREE — default ON. `make loop` first creates a dedicated git worktree\n#              at .worktrees/<TARGET>/ on branch core-<TARGET> and re-execs\n#              itself there. Lets two `make loop` invocations for different\n#              TARGETs run in parallel without git index/merge collisions.\n#              To opt out (run directly on the current branch + cwd) pass\n#              an explicit empty value: `make loop TARGET=v1 WORKTREE=`.\nN        ?= 10\nK        ?= 1\nAGENT    ?= $(or $(AGENT_PROVIDER),codex)\nBASE     ?=\nCOREMARK ?=\nLUT      ?=\nWORKTREE ?= 1\n\n# Compose optional CLI flags for the orchestrator. Empty vars produce\n# empty strings so the orchestrator falls back to its defaults.\nORCH_FLAGS  = --iterations $(N) --tournament-size $(K)\nifneq ($(strip $(BASE)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --base $(BASE)\nendif\nifneq ($(strip $(COREMARK)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --coremark-target $(COREMARK)\nendif\nifneq ($(strip $(LUT)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --lut-target $(LUT)\nendif\n\n# Multi-core: TARGET selects the core under cores/<TARGET>\n…[4885 of 7957 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:fff635150ba83f93]…\n) N=$(N) K=$(K) AGENT=$(AGENT) \\\n\t  $(if $(strip $(BASE)),BASE=$(BASE),) \\\n\t  $(if $(strip $(COREMARK)),COREMARK=$(COREMARK),) \\\n\t  $(if $(strip $(LUT)),LUT=$(LUT),)\nelse\n\tAGENT_PROVIDER=$(AGENT) python3 -m tools.orchestrator $(ORCH_FLAGS) $(ORCH_TARGET_FLAG)\nendif\n\nreport:\n\tpython3 -m tools.orchestrator --report $(ORCH_TARGET_FLAG)\n\nclean:\n\trm -rf test/cosim/sim_build sim_build out\n\trm -rf experiments/worktrees\n\trm -f bench/programs/*.elf\n\trm -f test/*.result.xml\n\tfind test -name __pycache__ -prune -exec rm -rf {} + 2>/dev/null || true\n\tfind tools -name __pycache__ -prune -exec rm -rf {} + 2>/dev/null || true\nifneq ($(strip $(TARGET)),)\n\trm -rf $(OBJ_DIR)\n\trm -rf $(GEN_DIR)\n\trm -rf cores/$(TARGET)/sim_build\nendif\ntools/eval/gowin.py\ntotal 20\ndrwxr-xr-x  5 nobody nogroup 4096 Sep 28 05:13 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 20 nobody nogroup 4096 Sep 28 01:00 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x  5 nobody nogroup 4096 Sep 28 05:13 gowin\ndrwxr-xr-x  4 nobody nogroup 4096 Sep 28 02:57 hwe-python\ndrwxr-xr-x  5 nobody nogroup 4096 Sep 28 02:44 hwe-toolchain\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['id'].endswith(('r4s2','r7s2','r11s1','r9s2')):\n  print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],r['critical_path'],r.get('implementation_notes'),sep='\\\\n')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_10", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor r in map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')):\n if r['id'].endswith(('r4s2','r7s2','r11s1','r9s2')):\n  print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'],r['critical_path'],r.get('implementation_notes'),sep='\\\\n')\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r4s2\nGive fetch one 32-bit equality comparator. For a legal BEQ or BNE whose\nsources are both ready, use the comparison of the existing MEM/WB-bypassed\nID operands. A source is ready when the instruction in ID/EX has no pending\nwrite to that nonzero register. For ordered branches and busy-source\nequality branches, use a PC-indexed table of 128 two-bit saturating counters.\nPredict direct JAL taken. For legal zero-offset JALR with a ready rs1,\nuse rs1 with bit zero cleared. Require word alignment and a target inside\nthe 1 MiB instruction window for a taken prediction. Default to PC+4.\n\nForm PC+4 and the direct B/J target during fetch. Register the unused\ndirect successor at issue so EX has the recovery address before it resolves\nthe condition. A predicted JALR target is exact because its base is ready\nand its displacement is zero. A single direction bit therefore suffices\nfor recovery. EX still resolves every control transfer with the true\nforwarded operands and preserves misalignment traps and jump links.\n\nA scratch RTL prototype on this champion completed ten stalled iterations\nin 4,164,193 marked cycles with all five canonical CRCs. Gowin placement\noptions 0, 1 and 2 reached 61.308, 62.308 and 62.308 MHz. The median gives\n149.63 iter/s, 7.26 percent above 139.50. At this cycle count the 4.6 percent\nacceptance margin requires a median above 60.763 MHz. Placement option 0\nused 3,278 logic cells and 628 registers. The prototype passed all 53\nstandard formal checks. Its RVFI traces matched the reference for\nselftest and the three random programs, both with and without bus stalls.\n\n{'from': 'cpu/u_fwd/rs2_match_q_s0', 'to': 'dmem_1_dmem_1_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -11.246, 'arrival_ns': 21.541, 'logic_cells': 10, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']}\n\nhyp-20261122-003-r7s2\nPredict the next PC from a 128-entry tagged branch target buffer and the\nexisting two-bit direction counters. Use that address both to advance\nfetch and to launch a synchronous read of a 1,024-word instruct\n…[4003 of 7075 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:19c34b6bbedacbc5]…\nnt\nsign, so the access stayed on that page. For a pending ADD, ADDI or LW base\nand a displacement of 0 through 3, compare the ordinary EX/MEM result bits\n31:2 directly to the pending port's zero-extended page and registered row.\nCheck the two-bit lane addition for word crossing and the access for alignment.\nThese tests let the load-use interlock use the two parallel verdicts while\nthe full forwarded effective address is computed independently for MEM and RVFI.\nA failed check takes the ordinary MEM access and dependency bubble.\n\nA scratch implementation completed ten iterations in 3,061,795 marked cycles\nwith all five canonical CRCs. Gowin placements reached 79.600, 86.496 and\n86.496 MHz, giving 282.50 iterations/s, about 3.4 percent above the champion.\nReplacement at this cycle count requires more than 87.47 MHz.\n\n{'from': 'cpu/u_ex/reg_q.alu_result_0_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/older.source_4_s0', 'slack_ns': -6.93, 'arrival_ns': 17.225, 'logic_cells': 11, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu']}\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
