{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nrows=list(map(json.loads,open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl')))\nfor r in rows:\n if r['id'].endswith(('r5s2','r7s2','r9s0','r9s2')):\n  print(r['id'],r['hypothesis']['motivation'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20261122-003-r5s2 The champion takes 4,164,193 marked cycles at 64.744 MHz. The earlier\ndesign that fetched the resolved EX successor took 4,136,096 cycles,\njust 0.68% fewer. Even a correctly predicted branch can lose its next fetch slot\nto instruction-bus backpressure. Repeating instruction pairs offer a\nway to recover those slots on both sequential and taken paths.\n\nhyp-20261122-003-r7s2 In the champion's 3,122,498 marked cycles, fetch waited for an instruction\non 46,468 cycles even after excluding redirects, data stalls and interlocks.\nA saved word belongs to a predecessor's last transition. A branch can\ntherefore miss on a warm destination whenever it arrives by a different\nedge. The median placement also has a path from the successor RAM through\nthe register file to the fetch PC.\n\nhyp-20261122-001-r9s0 On the champion's median Gowin placement, the slowest path launches\nfrom the successor block RAM, passes through the register file and ends\nat the load-table collision check. The same RAM also feeds the fetch\nbranch decision. Starting its read one issue earlier gives both paths a\nflip-flop output to start from. The tradeoff is that a PC two issues\nback can identify the desired instruction less often at a branch.\n\nhyp-20261122-003-r9s2 The champion's median Gowin path starts at the successor block RAM, passes through the register file and ends at the load predictor's collision check. Reading a word two instructions before it is needed gives the RAM output a full cycle to reach a register before it drives decode. Indexing the word store by destination PC lets one fill serve multiple control-flow paths.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
