{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_7", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor l in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"):\n r=json.loads(l)\n if r[\\\"id\\\"].split(\\\"-\\\")[-1] in [\\\"r11s1\\\",\\\"r12s1\\\",\\\"r13s2\\\",\\\"r13s1\\\",\\\"r9s1\\\",\\\"r7s2\\\"]:\n  print(r[\\\"id\\\"],r[\\\"hypothesis\\\"]);print(r[\\\"implementation_notes\\\"]); print(\\\"Critical path\\\",r[\\\"critical_path\\\"])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r7s2 {'motivation': \"In the champion's 3,122,498 marked cycles, fetch waited for an instruction\\non 46,468 cycles even after excluding redirects, data stalls and interlocks.\\nA saved word belongs to a predecessor's last transition. A branch can\\ntherefore miss on a warm destination whenever it arrives by a different\\nedge. The median placement also has a path from the successor RAM through\\nthe register file to the fetch PC.\\n\", 'hypothesis': 'Predict the next PC from a 128-entry tagged branch target buffer and the\\nexisting two-bit direction counters. Use that address both to advance\\nfetch and to launch a synchronous read of a 1,024-word instruction RAM.\\nA single fill at a destination PC then serves sequential, taken and\\nrecovery paths to that instruction. The predicted address is available\\nwithout waiting for decode to read branch operands.\\n\\nSplit the instruction field instr[31:15] from the block RAM row. It\\ncontains both RF addresses and the I-immediate used to predict a load\\nrow. Give these 17 bits a distributed RAM with an asynchronous read at\\nthe predicted next index and a capture flop on the same edge as the\\nblock RAM read. Reassemble the saved instruction in decode. This makes\\nthe saved operand fields available at flop clock-to-Q and keeps the\\ndata-RAM address calculation out of the instruction block RAM output path.\\n\\nA prototype with the entire instruction in block RAM completed ten\\niterations in 3,104,776 marked\\ncycles with both bus stalls, the five expected CRCs and the validation\\nbanner. Gowin measured 62.193, 65.910 and 65.910 MHz, yielding 212.29\\niterations/s. That is 3.25% above 205.60. At that cycle count, the\\n4.6% replacement margin requires more than 66.77 MHz. Its median\\nplacement had a path from the instruction block RAM through the\\nload predictor to lookup_ok_q. The proposed split targets that path.\\nThe split layout has not been timed. A 67.5 MHz median at the same\\ncycle count would yield 217.4 iterations/s.\\n', 'expected_impact': {'fitness_delta_pct': \n…[21479 of 24551 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5c81805504af2295]…\nv', 'description': 'Capture the match bits, direct target, predicted PC and fallthrough with the same issue as the decoded instruction.'}, {'file': 'rtl/ex_stage.sv', 'description': 'Use the registered matches for direct recovery and compare resolved JALR targets explicitly. Qualify recovery with valid EX advance and retain precise trap, link and RVFI behavior.'}, {'file': 'rtl/core.sv', 'description': 'Remove fetch-only RF readiness and bimodal training connections and route recovery through the existing flush and stall controls.'}, {'file': 'test/test_pipeline.py', 'description': 'Exercise an alternating successor, a changed live word, a changing JALR target, read/write aliasing, and a wrong-path store or FENCE.I during a bus hold. Check actual RVFI PCs, words, links, traps and store handshakes for each recovery.'}]}\n\nCritical path {'from': 'cpu/u_ex/rs1_high_q_s0', 'to': 'cpu/u_if/words_words_0_0_s', 'slack_ns': -6.799, 'arrival_ns': 17.041, 'logic_cells': 40, 'modules': ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu']}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
