{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_8", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat Makefile | head -220 && cat test/cosim/build.sh | head -130'", "aggregated_output": ".PHONY: help lint test test-infra cosim formal fpga timing next loop report bench clean\n.DEFAULT_GOAL := help\n\n# Resolve OSS CAD Suite path so commands work even when the user hasn't\n# sourced .toolchain/oss-cad-suite/environment in this shell.\nTOOLCHAIN_DIR := $(CURDIR)/.toolchain\nOSS_BIN       := $(TOOLCHAIN_DIR)/oss-cad-suite/bin\nLOCAL_BIN     := $(TOOLCHAIN_DIR)/bin\nexport PATH := $(OSS_BIN):$(LOCAL_BIN):$(PATH)\n\n# Orchestrator knobs (pass-through to tools.orchestrator):\n#   N        — number of rounds for `make loop` (default 10).\n#   K        — tournament size, slots per round (default 1 = sequential).\n#   AGENT    — codex (default) or claude. Honors a pre-existing\n#              AGENT_PROVIDER env var if AGENT isn't set on the make\n#              command line.\n#   BASE     — when forking a new TARGET, copy from cores/<BASE>/.\n#              Default: baseline (orchestrator-side default).\n#   COREMARK — target CoreMark iterations/sec (e.g. '370').\n#   LUT      — target LUT count (e.g. '3000').\n#   WORKTREE — default ON. `make loop` first creates a dedicated git worktree\n#              at .worktrees/<TARGET>/ on branch core-<TARGET> and re-execs\n#              itself there. Lets two `make loop` invocations for different\n#              TARGETs run in parallel without git index/merge collisions.\n#              To opt out (run directly on the current branch + cwd) pass\n#              an explicit empty value: `make loop TARGET=v1 WORKTREE=`.\nN        ?= 10\nK        ?= 1\nAGENT    ?= $(or $(AGENT_PROVIDER),codex)\nBASE     ?=\nCOREMARK ?=\nLUT      ?=\nWORKTREE ?= 1\n\n# Compose optional CLI flags for the orchestrator. Empty vars produce\n# empty strings so the orchestrator falls back to its defaults.\nORCH_FLAGS  = --iterations $(N) --tournament-size $(K)\nifneq ($(strip $(BASE)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --base $(BASE)\nendif\nifneq ($(strip $(COREMARK)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --coremark-target $(COREMARK)\nendif\nifneq ($(strip $(LUT)),)\n  ORCH_FLAGS += --lut-target $(LUT)\nendif\n\n# Multi-core: TARGET selects the core under cores/<TARGET>\n…[6743 of 9815 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:a8ae8d4ed84fe990]…\nRET:-2}\"\n\n# Ensure OSS CAD Suite tools are on PATH for non-interactive shells.\nTOOLCHAIN=\"$REPO_ROOT/.toolchain\"\nif [ -d \"$TOOLCHAIN/oss-cad-suite/bin\" ]; then\n  export PATH=\"$TOOLCHAIN/oss-cad-suite/bin:$PATH\"\nfi\n\nmkdir -p \"$OBJ_DIR\"\n\n# Glob $RTL_DIR/*.sv. core_pkg.sv first; the rest in a stable lexicographic\n# order (modulo case-insensitive sort, which Verilator/gcc don't care\n# about). If core_pkg.sv is missing the build will catch that downstream\n# via undefined-typedef errors.\nRTL_FILES=()\n[ -f \"$RTL_DIR/core_pkg.sv\" ] && RTL_FILES+=(\"$RTL_DIR/core_pkg.sv\")\nfor f in \"$RTL_DIR\"/*.sv; do\n  [ \"$(basename \"$f\")\" = \"core_pkg.sv\" ] && continue\n  RTL_FILES+=(\"$f\")\ndone\n\nif [ ${#RTL_FILES[@]} -eq 0 ]; then\n  echo \"ERROR: no rtl/*.sv files found.\" >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\n\nverilator --cc --exe --build \\\n  -Mdir \"$OBJ_DIR\" \\\n  \"+incdir+$RTL_DIR\" \\\n  --top-module core \\\n  -Wall -Wno-fatal -Wno-style \\\n  -CFLAGS \"-DNRET=$NRET\" \\\n  \"${RTL_FILES[@]}\" \\\n  \"$COSIM_DIR/main.cpp\" \\\n  -o cosim_sim\n\necho \"Built: $OBJ_DIR/cosim_sim\"\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"):\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r13s0','r12s2','r11s0','r8s2')):\n  print(r['id']); print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis']); print(r['implementation_notes'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_9", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor line in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"):\n r=json.loads(line)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r13s0','r12s2','r11s0','r8s2')):\n  print(r['id']); print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis']); print(r['implementation_notes'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-003-r8s2\nUse a valid, eligible load-table candidate to predict that the adjacent\nconsumer can issue. Keep the existing interlock for a definite miss.\nOn the issue edge, register one bit recording whether either source\nmatches the preceding nonzero destination. In parallel, EX verifies\nthe load's full forwarded word address and alignment. Only an exact\nhit can supply data or suppress the external read.\n\nIf the prediction was wrong, hold the consumer in ID/EX while MEM\ncompletes the older load. On completion, patch each matching source\nwith the extended load result and insert one EX/MEM bubble. A trapping\nload preserves the captured pre-load operands. The consumer then\nexecutes once at its original PC. This costs one bubble on a dependent\nmiss and preserves back-to-back execution on a hit.\n\nA scratch implementation made 189,167 such predictions in ten marked\nCoreMark iterations. It repaired all 31,113 false hits in place and\nfinished in the champion's 3,132,302 cycles with the canonical CRCs.\nGowin placement 1 reached 77.694 MHz, equivalent to 248.04 iterations/s\nat that cycle count. The selftest and three random RVFI comparisons\npassed on both zero-wait and stalled buses.\n\n\nhyp-20261122-001-r11s0\nGive the load RAM a ten-bit bypass from the preceding producer. For\nADD or ADDI, compute the low twelve result bits from write-first\noperands in ID and register bits 11 through 2 when the used sources\nare ready. For an LW in EX, tap bits 11 through 2 of its raw candidate\nword. When the younger load needs that producer and its displacement\nis 0 through 3, use the bypass as the synchronous RAM read index.\nThis replaces the per-PC stride history with wires and an ID register.\n\nVerify the word using two short comparisons in EX. For a ready base,\nID already selected the exact row with its low twelve-bit sum. Capture\nwhether carry out equals displacement sign, then compare the saved\nbase's page against the RAM page tag and check the RAM window. For a\ndependent base, compare the ordinary EX/MEM result directly again\n…[3199 of 6271 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:ceaef97cfe343ed9]…\nlls only from a ready bus inside the instruction window.\n\nKeep the fabric candidate and its collision-permission bit together until an architectural issue replaces them. This keeps the current word available when the PC holds. Register the launched read row and simultaneous fill row at the probe edge and compare those registers in the response stage, so the exception lookup and collision comparison occupy different cycles. Preserve live-bus priority, the full destination-PC match, and reset and FENCE.I invalidation of every in-flight permission. PC advance, ID/EX validity and fence issue continue to use the architectural issue rules.\n\nA preliminary version that advanced the candidate on every available word reached 90.023 MHz in Gowin placement 1 and completed ten iterations in 3,098,509 marked cycles with all five CRCs. Holding the candidate and splitting the read-row collision check target its cycle cost and clock margin. At the champion's 3,061,795 cycles the acceptance threshold is just over 93.53 MHz.\n\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
