{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_12", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(s)\n if any(d['id'].endswith(x) for x in ['r7s2','r9s1','r9s2','r13s1','r14s1','r14s0','r13s2','r11s1']):\n  print('\\\\n', d['id'], d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20261122-003-r7s2 Predict the next PC from a 128-entry tagged branch target buffer and the\nexisting two-bit direction counters. Use that address both to advance\nfetch and to launch a synchronous read of a 1,024-word instruction RAM.\nA single fill at a destination PC then serves sequential, taken and\nrecovery paths to that instruction. The predicted address is available\nwithout waiting for decode to read branch operands.\n\nSplit the instruction field instr[31:15] from the block RAM row. It\ncontains both RF addresses and the I-immediate used to predict a load\nrow. Give these 17 bits a distributed RAM with an asynchronous read at\nthe predicted next index and a capture flop on the same edge as the\nblock RAM read. Reassemble the saved instruction in decode. This makes\nthe saved operand fields available at flop clock-to-Q and keeps the\ndata-RAM address calculation out of the instruction block RAM output path.\n\nA prototype with the entire instruction in block RAM completed ten\niterations in 3,104,776 marked\ncycles with both bus stalls, the five expected CRCs and the validation\nbanner. Gowin measured 62.193, 65.910 and 65.910 MHz, yielding 212.29\niterations/s. That is 3.25% above 205.60. At that cycle count, the\n4.6% replacement margin requires more than 66.77 MHz. Its median\nplacement had a path from the instruction block RAM through the\nload predictor to lookup_ok_q. The proposed split targets that path.\nThe split layout has not been timed. A 67.5 MHz median at the same\ncycle count would yield 217.4 iterations/s.\n\n\n hyp-20261122-002-r9s1 Fork operand delivery before the instruction-source mux. Two extra\nasynchronous register-file read ports take their addresses directly\nfrom the live bus. When imemReady is high, fetch can use those values\nfor its existing ready-operand BEQ/BNE and zero-offset JALR predictions.\nWhen issuing a replayed word, fetch uses the existing branch counter\nfor a conditional branch and lets EX resolve JALR. Architectural ID\ncontinues to read the selected instruction through its write-first por\n…[10308 of 13380 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2acf0a38b9dd8908]…\natching writer in EX,\nMEM or WB. Capture fallthrough, direct target and the direction bit\nseparately so the late comparison does not select a wide recovery PC in ID.\n\nIn EX, give the branch comparator and JALR adder their own operand muxes\nbetween captured ID values and the ordinary EX/MEM result. Interlock a\nbranch using either source of an adjacent multiply, or a JALR using its\nrs1, until ID can capture the product from the write-first port. Keep the\nproducer advancing and apply the usual memory backpressure and redirect\npriority. An interlocked transfer resolves, trains and retires exactly once.\n\nA scratch version with one selected metadata read address per source\nvalidated all five CoreMark CRCs in 3,067,156 marked cycles. Placement 1\nreached 88.370 MHz with the common EX operand muxes. At this cycle count\nacceptance needs a median above 93.6968 MHz. The parallel index lookups\nand ordinary-only resolution bypass target the remaining control paths.\nThe MEM-to-load eligibility path could still limit Fmax.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
