{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_44", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor s in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n d=json.loads(s)\n if any(d['id'].endswith(x) for x in ['r7s0','r8s0','r8s1','r7s1']):\n  print(d['id'],d['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"", "aggregated_output": "hyp-20261122-001-r7s0 Give the multiplier registered inputs. Capture the two MEM/WB-bypassed\noperands on decode issue, with the signedness appropriate to MUL,\nMULH, MULHSU or MULHU. The DSP can start directly from these flops.\nA multiply with an unresolved adjacent writer takes one repair cycle\nin EX to overwrite the same input flops with the actual forwarded\nvalues. It then executes through the identical product path. This\nremoves both the adjacent bypass and PC/immediate selection from the\nDSP inputs while preserving one-cycle issue for independent multiplies.\nMeasure repair cycles and median Fmax together. The acceptance clock\nis 67.15 MHz at the original cycle count and about 69.2 MHz if every\nmultiply costs one extra cycle. Fetch and divider timing are the main\nrisks to reaching that range.\n\nhyp-20261122-002-r7s1 Move result selection across the EX/MEM register. Capture the full product beside\nthe ordinary EX/MEM result. In MEM, use registered enables to select the high\nproduct, the low product or the ordinary result. Forward that value to the adjacent\ninstruction and write the register file when MEM completes. Decode sees that write\nthrough the file's write-first port. Fetch takes the array output before the\nbypass and uses its existing operand-based predictions only when all required\nvalues are already in the array.\n\nFit division to the same result boundary by splitting the radix-4 recurrence at\ndivisor magnitude 256. Divisors from 1 through 255 need at most an eight-bit\npartial remainder. Any divisor of 256 or more exceeds the dividend's top byte,\nleaving only 24 quotient bits to calculate. Both paths fit the existing six RUN\nedges. This removes the three-deep chain of full-width trial selections without\nadding a stall to any RV32M operation.\n\nA scratch implementation completed ten iterations in 3,132,302 marked cycles with\nboth bus stalls and all five expected CRCs, versus 3,122,498 for the champion.\nGowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 68.729, 72.115 and 72.115 MHz with one\nregistered enable per result b\n…[2350 of 5422 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:2e1070cbaaaec2ee]…\nrun in parallel for each source. One chooses the\nconsumer's captured operand or the raw ordinary EX/MEM result. The other\nchooses the registered low or high product. The registered multiply-match\nbit selects between their outputs. This shortens the forwarding path into\nthe address adder, branch comparator and ALU. MEM selects the producer's\nresult independently using its existing bank enables.\n\nA scratch build completed ten stalled CoreMark iterations in 3,132,302\nmarked cycles, matching the champion, with all five expected CRCs and the\nvalidation banner. Gowin placements 0, 1 and 2 reached 72.536, 79.683\nand 79.683 MHz. The median gives 254.39 iterations/s, 11.09 percent\nabove 229.00. The build used 3,998 logic cells, 743 registers, nine\nblock RAMs and two DSPs. All five cocotb suites passed. Its RVFI traces\nmatched the reference on selftest and all three random programs with\nready and stalled buses. Of 53 fast formal checks, 52 had passed at\nsubmission and the register-consistency check was still running.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
