{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_48","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r1s2.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-003-r1s2\ntitle: Predict direct branches at fetch with a 128-entry bimodal table\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  IF and ID share a cycle in this core. Fetch always selects PC+4, so a taken\n  transfer resolved in EX discards one fetch slot. An isolated 128-entry\n  prototype completed the ten-iteration CoreMark run in 4,202,272 bracketed\n  cycles with both bus stalls enabled and all five canonical CRCs. This saves\n  289,213 cycles over the baseline and raises iterations/cycle by 6.88%.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use the fetched instruction to identify legal conditional branches and JAL.\n  In parallel with immediate generation, read a 128-entry table of two-bit\n  saturating counters indexed by the fetch PC. For a conditional branch use\n  the counter MSB, and for JAL predict taken. Select the aligned PC-relative\n  target on a taken prediction and PC+4 otherwise. JALR resolves in EX.\n  Computing direct targets from the current instruction makes table aliasing\n  affect only direction and requires just one prediction bit per instruction.\n\n  Train once per valid conditional branch advancing from EX. A correct\n  prediction lets the current fetch enter ID/EX on the resolution edge.\n  A mismatch flushes that fetch and loads the architectural successor into\n  the PC. The prototype's three Gowin placement options reached 5.155,\n  5.488 and 5.488 MHz. Combining the median with the measured cycle count\n  yields 13.06 iter/s, 7.75% above the 12.12 baseline.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use a 128x2 array with combinational reads at the fetch and resolving\n      PC[8:2] and one clocked write. After reset, use an eight-bit counter to\n      initialize one entry per clock to weakly not taken. Fetch continues with\n      conditional predictions disabled until all 128 entries are initialized.\n      Then train with saturation. Predecode raw imem_data for JAL and BRANCH\n      with a legal funct3. Form one direct-target sum from the B/J\n      displacement. Predict a target only if word-aligned and inside the\n      1 MiB instruction memory. EX still computes the full architectural target.\n      Advance to the prediction only on fetch acceptance. A recovery redirect\n      has priority over an imem wait. Keep imem_addr driven by the registered PC.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: Carry a predicted_taken bit in the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: Latch predicted_taken with the accepted instruction, hold it on a dmem freeze and clear it on a bubble or reset.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Derive actual_taken from the forwarded branch comparison or jump and\n      the existing misalignment check. On valid EX advance, redirect only on\n      a direction mismatch, to the resolved target or PC+4 as appropriate.\n      Use the same resolved successor for RVFI pc_next. Emit the training PC,\n      direction and one update pulse per advancing legal conditional branch.\n      Preserve the existing trap and link-write behavior.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Wire EX training to IF and use the qualified EX recovery pulse for the existing hazard flush and PC redirect inputs.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check a warmed taken loop and its exit, alias two opposite branches at\n      the same index, and hold a resolving branch behind a stalled data access.\n      Check fetch timing on a correct prediction, recovery across an imem wait,\n      RVFI successor/order and suppression of a wrong-path store.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
