{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r2s2.yaml && git status --short'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-003-r2s2\ntitle: \"Predict direct transfers at fetch on the multicycle-divider champion\"\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 4,491,485 cycles for ten marked CoreMark iterations.\n  Its combinational IF/ID path loses one fetch slot when EX redirects a taken\n  transfer. In round one, a 128-entry bimodal predictor with direct JAL\n  prediction cut that count to 4,202,272 on the older core. The champion's\n  multicycle divider improved Fmax while preserving the original cycle count.\nhypothesis: |\n  Read 128 two-bit direction counters by fetch PC in parallel with forming\n  the direct target from the fetched B or J immediate. Use the counter MSB\n  for legal conditional branches and predict aligned, in-range JALs taken.\n  Both IF and EX derive direct targets from the same instruction, so a single\n  direction bit suffices to detect a wrong successor. Resolve JALR in EX.\n  A correct prediction lets the successor enter ID/EX on the resolution edge.\n  A mismatch kills that fetch and redirects to the architectural successor.\n  A temporary prototype on the current champion completed ten iterations\n  in 4,202,272 bracketed cycles with both bus stalls and all CRCs validated.\n  Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 32.148, 32.241 and 32.241 MHz.\n  The median yields 76.72 iter/s, 7.71% above the champion. The worst path\n  remains inside the divider. Placement option 0 used 3,587 logic cells\n  and 827 registers.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Index the direction table with PC[8:2]. Initialize entries to weakly\n      not taken one per cycle after reset, allowing sequential fetch during\n      initialization. Use two combinational reads, one at the fetch index and\n      one at the training index. Share a single clocked write between\n      initialization and saturation of the addressed counter on an EX update.\n      Predecode legal B and JAL from raw imem_data, select the corresponding\n      signed displacement, and form one PC-relative sum. Qualify speculative\n      targets for word alignment and the 1 MiB instruction window. Drive imem\n      from the registered PC. Accept a prediction only when fetch advances.\n      Give reset and EX recovery priority over a fetch hold.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: \"Add predicted_taken to the IF/ID and ID/EX bundles.\"\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: \"Capture the prediction with its instruction, hold both on a freeze, and clear both on a bubble.\"\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Resolve with forwarded operands and the existing trap checks. On valid\n      EX advance, compare predicted_taken with the resolved aligned transfer.\n      Recover to the target for an unexpected transfer or PC+4 for an\n      unexpected fall-through. Use the existing architectural successor for\n      RVFI and retain jump link and trap semantics. Train once per legal\n      advancing conditional branch using its own PC and resolved outcome.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Route EX training to IF and the recovery pulse to the existing PC redirect and hazard flush inputs.\"\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check fetch and RVFI for a trained backward loop, loop exit, direct JAL,\n      JALR, and a taken misaligned branch. Include a resolving branch held by\n      dmem backpressure and recovery during an imem wait. Check that correct\n      predictions admit the successor and a squashed store never reaches dmem.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
