{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_62", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r3s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-003-r3s2\ntitle: Predict direct transfers on the radix-4 core with one fetch-table read\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The 47.7 MHz champion spends 4,491,485 cycles on ten marked iterations.\n  A taken transfer discards one fetch because IF and ID share a cycle.\n  Two earlier cores completed the same workload in 4,202,272 cycles with\n  a 128-entry bimodal predictor and fetch-time JAL targets. Both still had\n  slower dividers than the current champion. At that cycle count the new\n  core needs a median above 46.68 MHz to clear the 4.6% acceptance margin.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict legal conditional branches with the high bit of a 128-entry\n  table of two-bit saturating counters indexed by PC[8:2]. Predict direct\n  JAL taken. Decode the B/J displacement from raw fetch data and add it\n  to the current PC in parallel with the counter lookup. Select a target\n  only if word aligned and in the instruction-memory window. JALR uses\n  the sequential prediction and resolves with the forwarded operand in EX.\n\n  Give the direction table one asynchronous fetch read and one synchronous\n  write. Carry the counter read at fetch to EX along with the prediction.\n  On an advancing legal branch, train that counter with the actual outcome\n  and write it at the resolving PC index. A fetch concurrent with a write\n  to the same index may sample the old value. This affects prediction\n  quality. It preserved the earlier predictor's marked cycle count in\n  simulation.\n\n  EX compares predicted and actual direction. A correct prediction admits\n  the successor on the same edge. A mismatch discards that fetch and\n  restores the full architectural successor even when imem is waiting.\n  Decode the raw instruction in parallel with EX resolution. Handle\n  recovery by clearing the ID/EX valid bit and control word so the\n  recovery signal can bypass the instruction decode and operand paths.\n\n  A temporary prototype completed ten iterations in 4,202,272 bracketed\n  cycles with both bus stalls and all five CRCs validated. Gowin placemen\n…[1657 of 4729 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:47e85d75cb71bd3d]…\ntage.sv\n    description: |\n      Qualify recovery and training with valid EX advance so a branch\n      held behind a waiting memory operation trains and redirects at most\n      once. Actual taken requires an aligned target. Recover to PC+4 on a\n      wrongly predicted taken branch, or to the full resolved target on\n      a missed taken branch or jump. Keep RVFI pc_next, trap behavior and\n      jump link writes derived from the architectural resolution. Train\n      once per advancing nontrapping conditional branch using its snapshot.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Connect the EX counter write port to IF and route EX recovery to the PC and younger-instruction flush controls.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check the fetch sequence for a warmed taken loop, its exit and a\n      direct JAL. Exercise counter aliases, a branch held by dmem, an\n      imem wait on recovery, JALR and a misaligned target. Check RVFI\n      order and successor PCs and that a wrong-path store never issues.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
