{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_64", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r4s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-003-r4s2\ntitle: Predict the next fetch address from a small branch target buffer\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The 62.655 MHz champion spends 4,491,485 cycles on ten marked CoreMark\n  iterations. Each EX redirect discards one fetch opportunity. Earlier\n  direct-transfer prediction saved 289,213 cycles on this pipeline.\n  A PC-indexed target buffer can supply a learned successor in parallel\n  with instruction decode and operand capture, including the target of a\n  recurring indirect jump. This makes it possible to keep the champion's\n  short path from its registered operands through EX.\nhypothesis: |\n  Use a 128-entry direct-mapped branch target buffer indexed by PC[8:2].\n  Store a PC tag, an 18-bit target word address and a two-bit saturating\n  direction counter. On a valid tag hit with the counter MSB set, fetch\n  the cached aligned target. Otherwise fetch PC+4. Learn targets only\n  inside the 1 MiB instruction window. The registered fetch PC addresses\n  the table directly, so a prediction consumes neither RF operands nor\n  an instruction-dependent target addition on the next-PC path.\n\n  Carry the prediction with each issued instruction. Precompute PC+4 and\n  the direct B/J target at issue. EX independently determines the actual\n  successor for the current instruction, including fall-through on a\n  misalignment trap. Check both direction and target, including a stale\n  hit on an ordinary instruction and a changed JALR destination. Recover\n  with the full architectural address and kill the younger instruction on\n  a mismatch. Train once per advancing legal control transfer. Allocate\n  on a taken miss, update the counter on a conditional hit, and record\n  the latest legal target as strongly taken on a jump.\n\n  At 4.20 million cycles, the 4.6 percent acceptance margin requires a\n  median Fmax above 61.29 MHz. The estimated gain assumes this lookup\n  retains about the champion's Fmax. The main risks are table conflicts\n  and the delay from EX target validation to the squash controls.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Read tag, counter and target from asynchronous distributed RAM.\n      Reset entry validity separately. Pack only aligned, in-window\n      targets into the table. Select the predicted successor when the\n      fetch is accepted and give an advancing EX correction priority\n      over an imem wait. Keep imem_addr driven by the registered PC.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Add the hit, counter snapshot and predicted-taken target metadata\n      to IF/ID and ID/EX. Carry the direct target and fall-through in ID/EX.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Compute PC+4 and PC plus the B/J immediate in parallel with operand\n      capture. Capture prediction metadata on issue, hold it on backpressure\n      and clear valid and side-effect controls on a squash.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Resolve branches with the existing forwarded operands. Compare the\n      chosen path and cached target with the actual transfer, using the\n      registered direct target for B/J and the bit-zero-cleared sum for\n      JALR. Drive recovery and table updates only on valid EX advance.\n      Feed the resolved successor to RVFI and preserve link and trap behavior.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Connect EX table updates to IF. Drive the PC redirect and existing\n      hazard flush inputs from the same qualified misprediction event.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Check a warming loop and its exit, two taken PCs sharing an index,\n      a JALR whose target changes and a stale taken hit on a replaced\n      instruction. Hold EX behind a stalled load and deny imem on recovery.\n      Check fetch addresses, RVFI successor, link, trap and order, and that\n      a wrong-path store never asserts a data-bus write enable.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
