{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_75","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"rg -n 'FAIL|ERROR' /srv/hwebench/tmp/19d02a47/27087833/successor-predictor-3oo4kawf/registered/formal_summary.log | tail -4 && head -30 cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r5s2.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20261122-003-r5s2\ntitle: Predict the next instruction fetch from the last successor of each PC\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  The champion takes 4,164,193 marked cycles at 64.744 MHz. An earlier\n  design that fetched the resolved EX successor took 4,136,096 cycles,\n  only 0.68% fewer. Improving branch direction alone has little headroom.\n  A correct next-fetch prediction can also supply an instruction during\n  the roughly 22% of fetch slots in which the instruction bus stalls.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a 512-row last-successor predictor indexed by the issuing PC[10:2].\n  Each entry records one valid bit, successor PC[19:2] and the 32-bit\n  instruction previously issued there. Read the entry synchronously\n  as the predecessor issues. On the next fetch, compare its saved successor\n  with the current fetch PC, which the existing branch predictor or EX\n  recovery selected. A match can supply the saved word on a stalled bus.\n  A miss waits for the normal memory handshake. Repeated sequential pairs\n  and repeated taken edges can both save a cycle.\n\n  The lookup uses a registered RAM output and runs in parallel with next-PC\n  selection. Use bus readiness to select the live or saved word and qualify\n  issue separately with bus readiness or a verified match. Record a pair\n  when its successor issues from the live bus, retaining the predecessor\n  across holds and redirect bubbles. Admit only aligned successor addresses\n  in the 1 MiB instruction window. EX still resolves each control transfer.\n\n  An isolated RTL prototype completed ten CoreMark iterations in 3,394,055\n  marked cycles with both stall generators enabled and all five CRCs correct.\n  Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 reached 58.264, 61.666 and 61.666 MHz.\n  The median gives 181.69 iterations/s, 16.86% above the champion.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
