{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_47", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r6s2.yaml')\ns=p.read_text().replace(\\\"A load PC that revisits one word or walks an array can also\\\\n  predict which on-chip RAM row will hold its next word, even while an older\\\\n  instruction is producing its base.\\\",\\\"Predicting the data RAM row at issue offers a way to recover both an\\\\n  adjacent load-use bubble and a data-bus wait on the same load.\\\")\nstart=s.index('  At issue,')\nend=s.index('changes:',start)\ns=s[:start]+'''  Predict one of 1024 word rows at issue. With a ready source, use the low\n  12 bits of the bypassed decode base plus the I-immediate. With a pending\n  base, use the load PC's last observed byte stride to predict its next row.\n  This launches one synchronous block RAM read while the load moves into EX.\n  Compare the candidate's entire word address to the true forwarded load\n  address. A valid, aligned RAM hit can forward the extended word on the\n  next clock and complete locally. A misprediction takes the normal MEM\n  handshake and load-use bubble. The predictor never sends a speculative\n  address to the data bus.\n\n  A scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,122,498 marked cycles\n  with both bus stalls and all five canonical CRCs, down from 3,394,055.\n  Gowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 gave 61.281, 64.904 and 64.904 MHz.\n  The median yields 207.86 iterations/s. At this cycle count 60.37 MHz\n  clears the tournament margin.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 12\n  confidence: high\n'''+s[end:]\nstart=s.index('      Index a 128-row')\nend=s.index('  - file: test/test_pipeline.py',start)\ns=s[:start]+'''      Select the synchronous word-table read address from the decode sum\n      or a 128-entry PC[8:2] stride table. Each stride entry records the\n      last actual byte address modulo 4096 and the predicted next word\n      index. On load advance train next = (2 * actual - previous) modulo\n      4096, using bits [11:2] as the row. Capture the prior address at issue\n      so training needs only one read and one write per array. Hold the\n      candidate during a pipeline hold. Each word-table row contains a\n      valid bit, the full in-RAM word address and a 32-bit raw word.\n      Refill on accepted in-window reads or aligned full-word RAM stores.\n      Invalidate the indexed row on an accepted partial store. Suppress\n      colliding reads and veto a held candidate on an older same-row\n      store in MEM. Disable hits during the reset validity sweep.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Supply the selected issue PC, bypassed rs1, I-immediate and existing\n      rs1_ready to the predictor. Capture with ID/EX and train on a valid\n      advancing load. Feed accepted MEM accesses to the word table. Qualify\n      the load-use interlock with an unresolved EX load and the MEM hold\n      with a pending bus access. Preserve the single EX/MEM forwarding wire.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry the effective bus address, verified-hit bit and raw candidate\n      word in EX/MEM. Use the existing result field for the extended load\n      value so a dependent instruction can forward it like an ALU result.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add forwarded rs1 and the decoded immediate on a dedicated address\n      path. Check the complete word address, RAM window, access alignment\n      and candidate validity before declaring a load hit. Use the resolved\n      low bits to select a signed or unsigned byte, halfword or word. For\n      loads select that value into the EX/MEM result independently of the\n      hit decision. Only a verified hit releases the interlock.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Drive memory addressing, lane masks and alignment checks from the\n      registered effective address. Suppress the bus read on a verified\n      hit and use its saved raw word for RVFI and the saved extended result\n      for decode bypass and WB. Misses, traps, stores and MMIO retain their\n      ordinary handshake and retirement behavior.\n'''+s[end:]\np.write_text(s)\nPY\ndate -u && git status --short\"", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
