and the predicted next word\n      index. On load advance train next = (2 * actual - previous) modulo\n      4096, using bits [11:2] as the row. Capture the prior address at issue\n      so training needs only one read and one write per array. Hold the\n      candidate during a pipeline hold. Each word-table row contains a\n      valid bit, the full in-RAM word address and a 32-bit raw word.\n      Refill on accepted in-window reads or aligned full-word RAM stores.\n      Invalidate the indexed row on an accepted partial store. Suppress\n      colliding reads and veto a held candidate on an older same-row\n      store in MEM. Disable hits during the reset validity sweep.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Supply the selected issue PC, bypassed rs1, I-immediate and existing\n      rs1_ready to the predictor. Capture with ID/EX and train on a valid\n      advancing load. Feed accepted MEM accesses to the word table. Qualify\n      the load-use interlock with an unresolved EX load and the MEM hold\n      with a pending bus access. Preserve the single EX/MEM forwarding wire.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry the effective bus address, verified-hit bit and raw candidate\n      word in EX/MEM. Use the existing result field for the extended load\n      value so a dependent instruction can forward it like an ALU result.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Add forwarded rs1 and the decoded immediate on a dedicated address\n      path. Check the complete word address, RAM window, access alignment\n      and candidate validity before declaring a load hit. Use the resolved\n      low bits to select a signed or unsigned byte, halfword or word. For\n      loads select that value into the EX/MEM result independently of the\n      hit decision. Only a verified hit releases the interlock.\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Drive memory addressing, lane masks and alignment checks from the\n      registered