{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_79", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r7s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261122-003-r7s2\ntitle: Predict the next fetch address early enough to read instruction RAM\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  In the champion's 3,122,498 marked cycles, fetch waited for an instruction\n  on 46,468 cycles even after excluding redirects, data stalls and interlocks.\n  A saved word belongs to a predecessor's last transition. A branch can\n  therefore miss on a warm destination whenever it arrives by a different\n  edge. The median placement also has a path from the successor RAM through\n  the register file to the fetch PC.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict the next PC from a 128-entry tagged branch target buffer and the\n  existing two-bit direction counters. Use that address both to advance\n  fetch and to launch a synchronous read of a 1,024-word instruction RAM.\n  A single fill at a destination PC then serves sequential, taken and\n  recovery paths to that instruction. The predicted address is available\n  without waiting for decode to read branch operands.\n  \n  An isolated prototype completed ten iterations in 3,104,776 marked\n  cycles with both bus stalls, the five expected CRCs and the validation\n  banner. Gowin measured 62.193, 65.910 and 65.910 MHz, yielding 212.29\n  iterations/s. That is 3.25% above 205.60. At that cycle count, the\n  4.6% replacement margin requires more than 66.77 MHz.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use PC[8:2] to read a distributed-RAM entry holding a valid bit,\n      PC[19:9], a conditional bit and an aligned target[19:2]. Train from\n      advancing legal control transfers. Qualify both source and target\n      for the 1 MiB window and the target for word alignment. Predict\n      taken on a tag hit for an unconditional jump or a taken direction\n      counter. Otherwise select PC+4. Scrub targets after reset.\n      \n      Fill the instruction RAM from a ready, issued bus word. Each row\n      stores validity, PC[19:12] and 32 instruction bits. Launch the read\n      using EX recovery first, the predicted successor on issue, and the\n      held PC otherwise. Carry the sampled row and suppress collisions.\n      On a bus wait, admit a word only if its entire in-window address\n      matches. Give ready bus data priority. Reset and issued FENCE.I\n      cancel pending replay and scrub all rows through the write port.\n      Hold younger issue while the fence waits behind an older store.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry the predicted target word address, predicted-taken bit and\n      a precomputed direct-target match through IF/ID and ID/EX.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture the prediction with its instruction and hold both on a freeze.\n      Compare the BTB target with the direct target computed from the fetched\n      word on the issue side of the ID/EX register.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Recover on a direction mismatch or a wrong destination on a taken\n      prediction. Compare JALR against its full forwarded target after bit\n      zero is cleared. Check stale predictions on newly fetched noncontrol\n      words as well. Redirect to the architectural target or fallthrough,\n      preserve trap and link semantics, and train once per EX advance.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Route EX target training and recovery to fetch. Preserve redirect\n      priority and the existing issue, stall and side-effect qualification.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Train two predecessors reaching the same instruction, then hold the\n      instruction bus low at that destination. Exercise BTB aliasing, a\n      JALR that stays taken while its target changes, a same-row fill/read\n      collision, and code replacement across FENCE.I. Check fetch cycles,\n      the exact RVFI instruction/PC/order trace and accepted stores.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r7s2.yaml')\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint(205.60*1.046*3104776/1e7)\nPY\nPYTHONPATH=. PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/r7/scores.py\" bh2k bh4k row1k row2k && date -u'", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_79", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r7s2.yaml <<'YAML'\nid: hyp-20261122-003-r7s2\ntitle: Predict the next fetch address early enough to read instruction RAM\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  In the champion's 3,122,498 marked cycles, fetch waited for an instruction\n  on 46,468 cycles even after excluding redirects, data stalls and interlocks.\n  A saved word belongs to a predecessor's last transition. A branch can\n  therefore miss on a warm destination whenever it arrives by a different\n  edge. The median placement also has a path from the successor RAM through\n  the register file to the fetch PC.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict the next PC from a 128-entry tagged branch target buffer and the\n  existing two-bit direction counters. Use that address both to advance\n  fetch and to launch a synchronous read of a 1,024-word instruction RAM.\n  A single fill at a destination PC then serves sequential, taken and\n  recovery paths to that instruction. The predicted address is available\n  without waiting for decode to read branch operands.\n  \n  An isolated prototype completed ten iterations in 3,104,776 marked\n  cycles with both bus stalls, the five expected CRCs and the validation\n  banner. Gowin measured 62.193, 65.910 and 65.910 MHz, yielding 212.29\n  iterations/s. That is 3.25% above 205.60. At that cycle count, the\n  4.6% replacement margin requires more than 66.77 MHz.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 3\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Use PC[8:2] to read a distributed-RAM entry holding a valid bit,\n      PC[19:9], a conditional bit and an aligned target[19:2]. Train from\n      advancing legal control transfers. Qualify both source and target\n      for the 1 MiB window and the target for word alignment. Predict\n      taken on a tag hit for an unconditional jump or a taken direction\n      counter. Otherwise select PC+4. Scrub targets after reset.\n      \n      Fill the instruction RAM from a ready, issued bus word. Each row\n      stores validity, PC[19:12] and 32 instruction bits. Launch the read\n      using EX recovery first, the predicted successor on issue, and the\n      held PC otherwise. Carry the sampled row and suppress collisions.\n      On a bus wait, admit a word only if its entire in-window address\n      matches. Give ready bus data priority. Reset and issued FENCE.I\n      cancel pending replay and scrub all rows through the write port.\n      Hold younger issue while the fence waits behind an older store.\n  - file: rtl/core_pkg.sv\n    description: |\n      Carry the predicted target word address, predicted-taken bit and\n      a precomputed direct-target match through IF/ID and ID/EX.\n  - file: rtl/id_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Capture the prediction with its instruction and hold both on a freeze.\n      Compare the BTB target with the direct target computed from the fetched\n      word on the issue side of the ID/EX register.\n  - file: rtl/ex_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Recover on a direction mismatch or a wrong destination on a taken\n      prediction. Compare JALR against its full forwarded target after bit\n      zero is cleared. Check stale predictions on newly fetched noncontrol\n      words as well. Redirect to the architectural target or fallthrough,\n      preserve trap and link semantics, and train once per EX advance.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: |\n      Route EX target training and recovery to fetch. Preserve redirect\n      priority and the existing issue, stall and side-effect qualification.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Train two predecessors reaching the same instruction, then hold the\n      instruction bus low at that destination. Exercise BTB aliasing, a\n      JALR that stays taken while its target changes, a same-row fill/read\n      collision, and code replacement across FENCE.I. Check fetch cycles,\n      the exact RVFI instruction/PC/order trace and accepted stores.\nYAML\npython - <<'PY'\nimport yaml,json,jsonschema\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r7s2.yaml')\njsonschema.validate(yaml.safe_load(p.read_text()),json.loads(Path('schemas/hypothesis.schema.json').read_text()))\nprint(205.60*1.046*3104776/1e7)\nPY\nPYTHONPATH=. PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python \\\"\"'$TMPDIR/r7/scores.py\" bh2k bh4k row1k row2k && date -u'", "aggregated_output": "66.77056750976\nbh2k 3100306 64.733 None 208.8\nbh4k 3095612 None None -\nrow1k 3098187 None None -\nrow2k 3093610 None None -\nSun Nov 22 06:02:32 UTC 2026\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
