{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_113", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20261122-003-r8s2.yaml'", "aggregated_output": "id: hyp-20261122-003-r8s2\ntitle: Issue dependent instructions on a predicted load hit and repair a miss in place\ncategory: predictor\nmotivation: |\n  On the champion's 71.73 MHz placement, the longest path runs from the\n  registered rs1 forwarding match through the effective-address adder and\n  load-hit comparator to the fetch predictor's predecessor write enable.\n  The consumer only needs the verdict when it executes one edge later.\n  Ten CoreMark iterations take 3,132,302 marked cycles, so moving that\n  decision across the issue edge pays if median Fmax exceeds 75.03 MHz\n  at the same cycle count.\nhypothesis: |\n  Predict a bypass whenever the load's selected data RAM row is valid\n  and eligible. Admit an adjacent dependent instruction to ID/EX on that\n  prediction. An invalid or vetoed row takes the existing load-use interlock.\n  In parallel, EX checks the full forwarded word address and alignment.\n  Only this exact hit may suppress the memory request or supply load data.\n\n  A false hit leaves the consumer in ID/EX while the older load uses MEM.\n  Hold both source matches. On MEM completion, copy the extended return\n  value into each dependent operand, drain the load, and insert one\n  EX/MEM bubble. A faulting load leaves the captured operands intact.\n  The consumer executes on the following edge with its original PC and\n  instruction. A false prediction therefore costs one bubble plus actual\n  bus waits and needs no instruction refetch.\n\n  Register the load probe's collision metadata beside its RAM output.\n  Give the fetch predictor a separate registered recovery PC so a held\n  consumer's eventual branch correction only has to set a select bit.\n  These two boundaries keep load issue and recovery off the wide PC enables.\n  A scratch implementation completed ten iterations in 3,132,302 marked\n  cycles with both stall generators and the canonical CRCs. Gowin measured\n  76.373 MHz on placement 1 and 77.723 MHz on placement 0. The five cocotb\n  suites passed, as did the selftest and all three random RVFI comparisons\n…[1882 of 4954 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:7754c1e41cad1909]…\ning the saved prediction direction.\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: |\n      Split EX recovery into its own full-width word-PC register and a\n      registered select. Sample the recovery target whenever its bank is\n      inactive or a new redirect arrives. A redirect wins over a hold.\n      Keep an active recovery PC stable until fetch advances. Clock the\n      normal direct and sequential/JALR banks on normal advances. Pass\n      the direct target to ID alongside PC+4 for later recovery selection.\n  - file: test/test_pipeline.py\n    description: |\n      Warm a data row, then force an eligible same-row wrong-tag load\n      followed by a consumer. Delay the response and check one request,\n      one retirement per instruction, both repaired operands, and a\n      two-cycle load-to-consumer retirement gap after completion. Exercise\n      a dependent branch, store and divide, and a misaligned load preserving\n      the old register value. A verified hit should retire its consumer on\n      the next cycle.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
