{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"ls /opt/hwe-toolchain\nls /opt/gowin\nls /opt/hwe-python\npython - <<'PY'\nimport os,sys,shutil\nprint(sys.executable)\nfor k in ['riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc','riscv64-linux-gnu-gcc','verilator','sby','gw_sh','python3','pytest']: print(k,shutil.which(k))\nfor k in ['TMPDIR','GOWIN_HOME','GOWIN_SH','GOWIN_BIN']:print(k,os.environ.get(k))\nPY\npython - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor x in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(x)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r7s2','r6s2')):print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"ls /opt/hwe-toolchain\nls /opt/gowin\nls /opt/hwe-python\npython - <<'PY'\nimport os,sys,shutil\nprint(sys.executable)\nfor k in ['riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc','riscv64-linux-gnu-gcc','verilator','sby','gw_sh','python3','pytest']: print(k,shutil.which(k))\nfor k in ['TMPDIR','GOWIN_HOME','GOWIN_SH','GOWIN_BIN']:print(k,os.environ.get(k))\nPY\npython - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfor x in open('cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl'):\n r=json.loads(x)\n if r['id'].endswith(('r7s2','r6s2')):print(r['hypothesis']['hypothesis'])\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"bin\noss-cad-suite\nxpack-riscv-none-elf-gcc-15.2.0-1\nIDE\nProgrammer\ndeps\ngowin.tgz.sha256\ncpython-3.13-linux-x86_64-gnu\ncpython-3.13.12-linux-x86_64-gnu\n/srv/hwebench/venv/bin/python\nriscv32-unknown-elf-gcc /srv/hwebench/toolchain/bin/riscv32-unknown-elf-gcc\nriscv64-linux-gnu-gcc None\nverilator /srv/hwebench/toolchain/oss-cad-suite/bin/verilator\nsby /srv/hwebench/toolchain/oss-cad-suite/bin/sby\ngw_sh None\npython3 /srv/hwebench/venv/bin/python3\npytest /srv/hwebench/venv/bin/pytest\nTMPDIR /srv/hwebench/tmp/19d02a47/1a1d0dec\nGOWIN_HOME /opt/gowin\nGOWIN_SH None\nGOWIN_BIN None\nPredict one of 1024 word rows at issue. With a ready source, add the\nI-immediate to the bypassed decode base and select sum[11:2]. With a\npending base, use the load PC's last observed byte stride to predict its\nnext row.\nThis launches one synchronous block RAM read while the load moves into EX.\nCompare the candidate's entire word address to the true forwarded load\naddress. A valid, aligned RAM hit can forward the extended word on the\nnext clock and complete locally. On a miss, MEM requests the resolved\naddress and the usual load-use interlock protects a dependent consumer.\n\nA scratch prototype completed ten iterations in 3,122,498 marked cycles\nwith both bus stalls and all five canonical CRCs, down from 3,394,055.\nEX verified 435,226 of 544,852 loads, including 29,196 for which the\npreceding instruction was still producing the base at issue.\nGowin placement options 0, 1 and 2 gave 61.281, 64.904 and 64.904 MHz.\nThe median yields 207.86 iterations/s, 12.47% above the champion. At\nthis cycle count 60.37 MHz clears the tournament margin. The prototype\nused 4032 logic cells and nine block RAMs. It passed all 53 fast formal\nchecks, all five cocotb suites and the selftest and three random\nreference traces with and without bus stalls.\n\nPredict the next PC from a 128-entry tagged branch target buffer and the\nexisting two-bit direction counters. Use that address both to advance\nfetch and to launch a synchronous read of a 1,024-word instruction RAM.\nA single fill at a destination PC then serves sequential, taken and\nrecovery paths to that instruction. The predicted address is available\nwithout waiting for decode to read branch operands.\n\nSplit the instruction field instr[31:15] from the block RAM row. It\ncontains both RF addresses and the I-immediate used to predict a load\nrow. Give these 17 bits a distributed RAM with an asynchronous read at\nthe predicted next index and a capture flop on the same edge as the\nblock RAM read. Reassemble the saved instruction in decode. This makes\nthe saved operand fields available at flop clock-to-Q and keeps the\ndata-RAM address calculation out of the instruction block RAM output path.\n\nA prototype with the entire instruction in block RAM completed ten\niterations in 3,104,776 marked\ncycles with both bus stalls, the five expected CRCs and the validation\nbanner. Gowin measured 62.193, 65.910 and 65.910 MHz, yielding 212.29\niterations/s. That is 3.25% above 205.60. At that cycle count, the\n4.6% replacement margin requires more than 66.77 MHz. Its median\nplacement had a path from the instruction block RAM through the\nload predictor to lookup_ok_q. The proposed split targets that path.\nThe split layout has not been timed. A 67.5 MHz median at the same\ncycle count would yield 217.4 iterations/s.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
